window.CONCEPT_META = {"built":"2026-10-16 05:36 UTC","concepts":[{"id":"baseline-3r","name":"Baseline: spherical 3R (r20 s1414 #4, O20 hubs, strap links)","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"baseline","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":null,"t_screened":"2026-10-15T15:59:49Z","reach":0.775,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.4381,"inband10":0.2789,"contact":0.1024,"gap":68.93,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":6,"doc":"tools/concept_baseline.py","short":"Baseline","description":"The swarm's reference: a pure spherical 3R chain with every joint axis through the GH centre, 20 mm hubs and 8 x 3 mm strap links. The base axis is fixed on the scapula, the last axis on the humerus, and J2 follows from the two-circle closure.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1 hub (axis b through GH) -> link 1 (great-circle strap) -> J2 hub (axis through GH) -> link 2 -> J3 hub (axis W fixed in the humerus) -> arm-end neck -> the user's cuff.","main_risk":"J2 folds near overhead (the three axes become coplanar); hub 2 has no quiet skin under it in abduction.","verdict":"the bar to beat"},{"id":"acromial-rocker-3r","name":"Fold lever on the pin-knee leader: J1 on a passive acromial rocker whose angle the arm sets through an S-U tie rod (rotations only)","family":"scheduled 4th axis: spherical/offset 3R + passive base rocker + tie rod","lens":"round3:behind / on top of the scapula - a graded-fraction J1 that keeps the J1-J3 angle in the fold window","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:03:54Z","reach":0.2,"follow":0.6,"inband14":0.7849,"inband10":0.431,"contact":0.0933,"gap":125.84,"v_gap":125.84,"v_reach":0.2,"v_follow":0.6,"v_inband14":0.7849,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":65,"doc":"docs/concepts/acromial-rocker-3r.md","short":"Fold lever on the pin-knee leader","description":"A passive pivot boss on the lateral acromion top (S-quiet skin, 0.95-1.0 in band at 47-50 mm), axis through GH, carries a small rocker plate. One arm holds the J1 hub (axis 14-20 deg from the pivot axis, so J1's line still passes through GH and the pin-knee closure theorem still holds); the other holds a ball stud ~45 deg from the pivot toward the posterior-superior shoulder. A 55-62 mm Ti tie rod joins it to a stud on the arm-end plate by hub 3. Overhead the arm pulls the rod, the rocker turns and J1 precesses on its cone away from J3, widening the fold margin; across the body / behind the back it turns the other way.","topology":"scapula -> J0 PASSIVE R (pivot, through GH) -> rocker -> J1 ACTIVE R (through GH) -> link 1 -> J2 ACTIVE R -> link 2 -> J3 ACTIVE R -> pin knee -> arm; tie: rocker stud -> PASSIVE spherical (ball link) -> rod -> PASSIVE U (miniature U-joint) -> arm-end plate. The judge requires S-U, not S-S: an S-S rod has an idle spin about its own axis, an undetermined passive coordinate. q0 has a closed form (q0 = phi + s0 acos x) from the rod length; transmission angle >= 25 deg and |q0| <= 75 deg.","main_risk":"No real-skin gain shown: in the proxy the reach gain (+0.03-0.09) is paid back in in-band (net break-even), and the per-point proxy was refuted by the kit at the crest seats. Capping the rocker at +-75 deg may keep little of the fold gain. The rod over the acromion edge is a pinch point overhead; the pivot adds a 6 mm boss under J1; MVP2 rod reaction on the arm.","verdict":"kill test FAILS (needs reach > 0.75 at <= 2 pts in-band loss): best rocker 125.84 (reach 0.20, follow 0.60) vs the control's 26.34 in the same file; not converged (48 configs, top-5 spread 28 pts), so status stays screened, not dead"},{"id":"acromion-pointer-arm-ctrack","name":"Acromion gimbal + arm-fixed skin-shaped C-track (2 DOF at the shoulder, axial rotation lower on the arm)","family":"","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Acromion gimbal + arm-fixed skin-shaped C-track","description":"This is the user's own decomposition: two hubs form a clean gimbal at the shoulder, and humeral rotation gets its own joint lower on the arm. That joint is not a circular ring (a ring stands off a non-round arm: skin radius 38-73 mm around the humeral axis at s 90-120). It is an arm-fixed track that follows the arm's skin, so the long arc of the axial joint is arm-fixed metal over arm skin.\n- Rest: J1 sits on the bony lateral acromion top (2-5 mm skin spread in the scapula frame). J2 is behind the humeral head at GH level (posterior branch). The short yoke runs down the posterolateral deltoid to the carriage on the lateral arm ~100 mm below GH. The track wraps from the biceps edge over the anterolateral strip (2-4 mm spread) and the posterolateral strip (0.7-1.6 mm) to the triceps edge.\n- Overhead: the scapula-relative elevation peaks at ~128 deg, so the arm stays 10-25 deg (by a1…","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: O20 hub, active R. Its axis a1 is fixed to the scapula and passes through GH; the hub sits on the lateral acromion top (default a1 = sdir(40,0)) -> L1 strap -> J2: O20 hub, active R. Its axis u2 passes through GH; this is an orthogonal gimbal by default (alpha12 = alpha23 = 90) -> L2: a short pointing yoke. Its frame follows the arm direction but not its axial rotation -> carriage, with a passive radial slide and a passive swivel about an axis parallel to the humeral axis -> rides an ARM-FIXED C-track at station s_t ~100 mm (deltoid insertion). The track is shaped to the arm's own skin at +4 mm and spans ~190 deg (anteromedial -> lateral -> posterolateral), open medially -> a track bracket merged into a shortened BOTTOM. Active joints: J1, J2, and the carriage's arc position on the track (the humeral rotation about the GH-elbow line). Passive: the radial slide and the swivel.","main_risk":"1. Axial travel. Kinematic precheck (orthogonal gimbal, carriage angle about the humeral axis): the 6 fit clips need only 93/116/138 deg of track for a1 = (30,-10)/(40,0)/(50,10). The functional workspace needs 311/335/353 deg, because its 90-165 deg of axial width at each direction adds to the gimbal's twist across directions. A medially open track cannot exceed ~200 deg, so expect high follow but reach capped around 50-65 %.\n2. The singularity-free pole sdir(30,-10) has no hub site: its +pole is inside the abducted arm and its -pole inside the trunk. J1 must therefore tilt to the acromion, trading margin.\n3. The yoke rides in the pointing frame over the deltoid while the arm rotates under…","verdict":""},{"id":"annular-ring-3r","name":"Annular-ring 3R: open-bore thin-section rings at every joint axis","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"ideation A","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:48:25Z","reach":0.8875,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.3728,"inband10":0.2933,"contact":0.0051,"gap":57.81,"v_gap":78.01,"v_reach":0.8361,"v_follow":0.9273,"v_inband14":0.2116,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":55,"doc":"docs/concepts/annular-ring-3r.md","short":"Annular-ring 3R","description":"The baseline's joint layout, but each 12.25 mm O20 hub becomes a flat annulus, 8 mm thick and ~O38-56 mm across. Its open bore surrounds the axis pierce point, so the skin dome rises into the hole instead of lifting a solid hub; the links leave from the ring rims. REST: the J1 ring sits around the top of the shoulder with the acromion/supraspinatus apex inside the bore. The J2 ring sits wherever p2 is, on the posterior-superior shoulder in most poses. The J3 ring sits on the lateral deltoid ~19 deg below the GH level. OVERHEAD: the J2 ring travels onto the back over the medial scapula. The champion's J2 hub went under the trunk skin there; the bore lets that bulge through. ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: as on the champion, J2 swings anterior-superior / posterior, and the links run over the front / back of the shoulder. On a dome of radius 44 mm, a ring of radius 19 / 25 mm sits 4.3 / 7.8 mm…","topology":"S -> J1: thin-section 4-point ring (KAA15XL0, 38.1x47.6x4.76) centred on b's pierce point; lower race on the base, upper race on L1 -> L1 strap, attached at the ring rims -> J2: the same ring type about p2 -> L2 strap -> J3: the same ring type about W; lower race on the arm-side plate (BOTTOM stub) -> H. No metal inside the O38 bores. Active: J1, J2, J3. Passive: none.","main_risk":"The bore only helps on a dome. On the moving J2 skin the flanks move too, and a bulge that rises above the ring plane at the rim still hits the ring. On a steep flank (35 deg at rho 25 on r 44) a flat ring lifts its outer rim. The O48-66 footprint covers more skin, and the links get shorter by the ring radii. Thin-section rings need stiff, precise Ti housings: ~18 g per ring + ~10 g of housings, ~90 g for three joints.","verdict":"screened: best honest gap 57.81 (k52, Bench(60,160) fast cache, K3 march, K2 dense, K6, audit clean) vs 68.93 baseline and 65.05 for its own O20 twin at q 1.0. But the open bore loses to the O20 hub at every joint and every bore (rings at all three: 91.6-112.0 vs 69.29); the gain is the skin-contoured skirt under the J1 ring (98.5 % in band), which the champion can take too. Not worth a GPU sweep of ring sizes; worth one short sweep of the champion with a J1 skirt."},{"id":"armband-pointer","name":"Closed thin-section armband at the user's cuff + 2R donut pointer with a lateral spar","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Closed thin-section armband at the user's cuff","description":"The shoulder carries only two donuts and the strap between them. The spar runs down the lateral arm 8-15 mm off the deltoid to a ring around the arm just above the user's cuff, where the arm is slimmer: 40-50 mm skin radius, so 7-17 mm of gap inside a 57 mm bore radius. Overhead the spar lies along the arm's lateral side. Across-body the ring's medial arc meets the chest. Behind-back it sits between the arm and the trunk.","topology":"S -> J1 R active: donut at p* ~ [0.36, 0.72, 0.60] -> link 1 -> J2 R active: donut, a2 = +-normalize(a1 x t) -> link 2 = a long lateral spar from J2 down the outside of the arm (pointing frame: no twist) -> J3 R active: a CLOSED 4-point thin-section ring coaxial with the humerus axis around the distal upper arm, s = 190-240 mm from GH (RBC/Kaydon KA045XP0 114.3x127x6.35 or KA050XP0 127x139.7x6.35; a KA040XP0 is ~86 g with ~310 N.m static moment). Stator on the spar; rotor bolted into the user's upper-arm cuff / BOTTOM.","main_risk":"The ring's medial arc between arm and trunk: in the rig the arm touches or enters the torso in walking and wash-opposite-shoulder poses, so adducted poses will count as contact (follow and reach losses). The arm must be threaded through the ring (donning). The spar's gap changes with axial twist over a non-round arm (13.5-25 mm skin-radius spread 80-280 mm down the arm, FINDINGS), so the spar is often over 14 mm. The p* pole limit (32 deg) is the same as in pointer-cslide.","verdict":""},{"id":"back-anchored-ring-3r","name":"Back-anchored ring-ended 3R (top of the shoulder left bare)","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"weird-compliant","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:29:49Z","reach":0.6438,"follow":0.7667,"inband14":0.3622,"inband10":0.1606,"contact":0.1858,"gap":116.31,"v_gap":116.31,"v_reach":0.6438,"v_follow":0.7667,"v_inband14":0.3622,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":29,"doc":"docs/concepts/back-anchored-ring-3r.md","short":"Back-anchored ring-ended 3R","description":"Overhead-first placement. The skin that bunches overhead is the top of the shoulder (acromion and deltoid cap, 25-50 mm of radial change over the poses). That is exactly where a dome-rooted 3R puts its J1 hub and link-1 ribbon: the baseline's J1 is at S lat 80 / lon -57, 14 deg from the dome pole p*. This design leaves the top bare. J1 is a low ring (open centre, ~8 mm stack) lying on the infraspinous fossa; the S-fixed ray at lat 0 / lon -110 meets skin at 84-100 mm from GH in every bench pose. J3 is a second low ring on the user's cuff saddle on the lateral/posterolateral arm strip, where skin sits 55-65 mm from the humerus axis laterally and moves only 3-6 mm over all poses at s 120-210. Only J2 and the two straps cross the moving transition, and they do it over the back of the shoulder. REST: J1 is on the back of the scapula; link 1 rises laterally over the posterior deltoid to J2…","topology":"Scapula handler plate on the infraspinous fossa -> J1: ACTIVE R, a thin-section ring bearing (Kaydon Reali-Slim KAA type, 4.76 mm square section, pitch O45-60, open centre). Its axis u1 passes through GH and points posterior-medial-down (S-local lat -25..+5, lon -130..-100; nominal lat -10 / lon -120). The ring is seated where that ray meets the back, 85-150 mm from GH -> link 1 (shaped Ti strap 14 x 5.5, axis angle a12 70-110 deg) -> J2: ACTIVE R, O20 v5 hub (12.25 mm stack), axis through GH, placed by closure (nominally at the posterolateral corner of the acromion at rest, S lat ~25 / lon ~-40) -> link 2 (strap, a23 70-110) -> J3: ACTIVE R, a second thin-section ring (pitch O50-65) on the user's upper-arm cuff saddle. Its arm-fixed axis j3 passes through GH and pierces the lateral/posterolateral arm strip at s 110-150 mm, th 0..60 deg posterior of lateral -> upper-arm cuff / BOTTOM. There is no neck and no JN hub. All axes meet at GH.","main_risk":"Behind the back and Reach back pocket: a posterior route puts link 2 between the arm and the back. That is the same squeeze that holds 28 of 49 back-pocket frames on the baseline. Both straps are long (~90 deg arcs at 60-140 mm radius, ~150-200 mm each) and cross the posterior deltoid, which moves. The J1 site is sensitive. The ray at lat -22 / lon -119 sees skin at 123-198 mm (the back rolls away), so the base needs lat >= ~-15. That costs margin: lat -10 / lon -125 with j3 at s120 / th60 gives 60-147 deg (~33 deg margin, skin 141-153 mm, far but stable); lat 0 / lon -110 with j3 at s140 / th40 gives 63-156 deg (~24 deg). Thin-section rings of O45-65 are catalogue parts (KAA20-KAA25).…","verdict":"dead, not worth a GPU sweep. Best honest gap 116.31 (k18) vs 68.93 for the champion 3R. k18: spec J1 site as an O20 hub, J3 hub at d3 50 / th3 30, a12 = a23 = 90, q 0.92. The lowest number in the sweep (82.26, k12, q 0.97) parks hub 2 and the links at the 400 mm never-clears cap, which the kit audit flags; it is not a design. The variant aimed at both limiters (J1 on its square, still posterosuperior seat + O20 hubs + a12 66-75) brings in-band 14 up to the champion's level (0.43, k19), but J2 then swings over the top to the front overhead and reach / follow fall to 0.44 / 0.58. Rings lose to O20 hubs at both ends (k12 82.26 vs k4 86.84)."},{"id":"base-rail-tripod","name":"Base-rail tripod: three carriages on scapula rails pushing fixed-length leg arcs (spherical 3-PRR, Hexaglide-like)","family":"","lens":"literature","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Base-rail tripod","description":"All of the variable-length motion happens on the scapula. Three short curved rails (10 x 4 mm) on the acromion, spine and supraspinatus carry carriages that slide as the arm moves. From each carriage a rigid titanium leg arc of fixed length, shaped to its own skin profile, runs over the shoulder cap to an arm plate on the deltoid. Rest: carriages sit mid-rail and the legs go to the anterior, lateral and posterior deltoid. Overhead: the deltoid rises under the acromion and the carriages run toward the lateral ends of their rails. Across-body: the carriage feeding the posterior leg runs medially along the spine. Behind-back: the anterior leg's carriage runs forward on the acromion. Legs swing over the cap but never change length.","topology":"Three legs in parallel. Each leg: a curved rail fixed on the scapula base (a great-circle arc on the acromion / scapular spine / supraspinatus) -> carriage on the rail (ACTIVE curved P) -> carriage pivot (passive R, radial axis through GH) -> rigid fixed-length leg arc -> arm pivot (passive R, radial) on an arm plate on the deltoid -> the user's passive cuff slides (outside the count).","main_risk":"Rail length. A frames-only check found that the angular distance from any S-fixed point to an arm-fixed point varies by about 100 deg over the bench poses, while the S-fixed patch is only about 50 deg across. A leg may run off its rail (reach loss) unless the rails extend onto skin that moves: medially along the spine is fine, the lateral acromion rim is not. Other risks: serial singularities where a rail runs perpendicular to its leg (pushing the carriage does nothing), legs crossing, and curved rails at r 50-80 mm being custom parts (no catalogue part fits, per docs/research/ring_and_shell_hardware.md).","verdict":""},{"id":"breathing-hubs","name":"Breathing hubs: cam-slaved radial joint stages on a spherical 3R","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Breathing hubs","description":"It looks like the current 3R: a base hub on the acromion top, the J2 hub, an arm hub on the lateral deltoid and two strap links. The difference is that the link ends are short double-leaf stages, and the J2 hub and the arm hub 'breathe': each rides 2-4 mm over its skin and rises by up to 15 mm only where the cam profile says that q1 (or q3) needs it. Rest (arm hanging): J2 sits over the posterior-superior shoulder at its lowest lift, and the whole mechanism is 2-8 mm off the skin. Overhead (142-150 deg): this is where the final's J2 hub sinks 2.7-6.7 mm into the back over the medial scapula and link 1 crosses the acromion. Here the J1 cam has lifted J2 by the 10-15 mm it needs, only in that q1 range, and the links S-bend down to J1 and J3. Across-body (Wash opposite shoulder): J2 swings anterior-superior and its lift follows the cam's envelope for that q1. The arm hub lifts with q3 over…","topology":"Scapula base (S) -> J1: R hub, ACTIVE, axis b through GH, base-fixed, fixed radius -> link 1: rigid Ti strap whose distal end is a radial parallelogram stage (two 0.6 x 12 mm Ti leaves, 30 mm long, 4 mm apart, tangent to the sphere at J2; passive P along p2, 8-15 mm travel) -> J2: R hub, ACTIVE, axis p2 through GH; its pin block floats along p2 -> link 2: rigid strap with a radial parallelogram stage at each end (the J2 end follows the J2 block, the J3 end follows the arm-hub block) -> J3: R hub, ACTIVE, axis W (arm-fixed) through GH -> arm-hub block on BOTTOM's neck, itself on a radial parallelogram stage (passive P along W). Lift drivers: a groove face cam (O32 x 3 mm ramp ring) fixed to the base around J1, an MR63 follower on link 1's root and a 1.5 mm Ti pushrod along link 1 to a bell crank on the J2 stage, so lift2 = c2(q1); a groove face cam on link 2's J3 end read by a follower on BOTTOM's stage, so lift3 = c3(q3). Optional level 2: each link's mid-span is a 3-segment flexure four-bar slaved to the same cam (mid-span lift m_k(q)). Active: q1, q2, q3. Passive: lift2 and lift3, both set by cams.","main_risk":"The skin need under J2 is a function of the whole pose, not of q1 alone. If, at a fixed q1, the need still spreads by more than 12 mm (the arm is under J2 in some poses and not in others), the cam must take each bin's maximum and most of the gain disappears. The bench's per-bin spread answers this in minutes. Second risk: parts and mass (three stages, two cams, a pushrod and a bell crank) within a 500 g budget, and the stages must stay stiff in tangential bending and torsion under MVP2's ~10 N.m (leaf sizing). A skin-referenced follower (a skid on the base layer) would remove the first risk entirely, but it touches the skin, which the user's rule excludes.","verdict":""},{"id":"cam-coupled-4r","name":"Cam-scheduled 4R: the 4R's skin-hugging fold without a sagging spare (pin-in-slot coupling on the base)","family":"","lens":"literature","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Cam-scheduled 4R","description":"It looks like the r13 4R: a J1 hub on the base, then J2, J3, J4, with links that fold over the shoulder to hug it. Link 2 has a short tail that reaches back over the base plate, where a roller pin rides a circular slot in the plate on the acromion or spine (S-fixed). As the arm moves, the slot steers J2 along the fold the free 4R would have chosen to keep its links low (a fixed schedule instead of a per-pose choice). Rest, overhead, across-body and behind-back follow the seed 4R's metal placement as closely as one schedule allows; the slot and the tail add a small amount of base metal on S-fixed skin.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1 R (ACTIVE) -> link 1 -> J2 R (passive, scheduled) -> link 2, whose tail carries a roller pin -> J3 R (ACTIVE) -> link 3 -> J4 R (ACTIVE, arm hub) -> arm member -> the user's passive cuff slides (outside the count). Closure: the roller pin on link 2 runs in a circular slot cut in the base plate (a curved P plus the pin's R).","main_risk":"A single schedule cannot be optimal for both overhead and behind-back (the arm-rom linear law lost 15-25 pts vs the free spare). The pin can lock where the slot is nearly parallel to the pin's J2 motion (pressure angle), causing a stall (R17). The extra tail and slot add metal and a fourth hub stack; links must keep >= 35 deg arcs (the short-link rule). The IK is a 1-D root search.","verdict":""},{"id":"cam-unfold-4th","name":"Cam-unfolding link: a slaved flexure knee in link 2","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Cam-unfolding link","description":"It looks like the baseline 3R with a thin 'knee' in the middle of link 2 (a crossed-flexure pivot, 6 mm tall, no bearing stack) and a small cam ring around J2. Arm hanging: the knee folds link 2, so its effective arc a2 is short and J2 sits closer to J3, lower on the side of the shoulder. Overhead: J2 has turned far and the cam opens the knee, so link 2 lengthens and J2 is held up over the top of the shoulder instead of sinking onto the medial scapula. Across-body and behind-back: c(q2) chooses the arc that keeps link 2 out of the arm-trunk gap (the squeeze that cost Reach back pocket).","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: R hub, ACTIVE (b) -> link 1 -> J2: R hub, ACTIVE (p2), with an O28 x 3 mm groove face cam on link 1 around the J2 hub -> link 2a (MR52 follower on a lever, O2 Ti pushrod along 2a) -> J4: PASSIVE cross-spring flexure pivot (two crossed 0.4 x 6 mm Ti leaves standing radially; axis p4 through GH; +-18 deg). Its angle is slaved, q4 = c(q2), by the cam through the pushrod and a 6 mm crank on 2b -> link 2b -> J3: R hub, ACTIVE (W, the arm end on BOTTOM). Variant: a spherical four-bar coupler between link 1 and link 2b instead of the cam.","main_risk":"The cam and pushrod sit in link 2b's load path: MVP2's 10 N.m needs a stout cam or a four-bar coupler instead. A single c(q2) may help some poses and hurt others. The flexure's +-18 deg limits how far the path can change. The IK of the coupled chain can have two roots (branch jumps). The extra parts at J2 (cam ring, follower) add a lump exactly where R1 already loses most.","verdict":""},{"id":"clavicular-front-route","name":"Front route: J1 on the bony acromio-clavicular front (spherical 3R + scapula bridge)","family":"spherical 3R (front-anchored, scapula bridge)","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T22:07:12Z","reach":0.875,"follow":0.95,"inband14":0.3088,"inband10":0.2413,"contact":0.0664,"gap":68.26,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":440,"doc":"docs/concepts/clavicular-front-route.md","short":"Front route","description":"Most of the functional workspace is forward (planes 30-150). This route puts the scapula anchor near the centre of that forward motion, on bone (acromion front / clavicle), and lands J3 on the anterolateral arm strip (2-4 mm spread).\n- Rest: J1 in front of the acromion, J2 over the anterolateral deltoid, J3 high on the anterolateral arm, neck down the strip, bridge over the acromion.\n- Overhead (scaption/flexion): the chain folds over the front-top of the shoulder with the two links overlapping. It folds rather than stretches, with an 8 deg fold margin.\n- Across the body: J1 is closest to the arm/hand.\n- Behind the back: the chain stretches over the front-lateral shoulder.","topology":"Scapula base + a scapula-fixed bridge strap from the handler over the acromion top to the front seat -> J1: O20 hub, active R, a1 = sdir(40,-100) over the AC joint / lateral clavicle -> L1 (alpha12 ~60) -> J2: O20 hub, active R, floating in front of / over the top of the shoulder -> L2 (alpha23 ~60) -> J3: active R, an O20 hub on the anterolateral upper arm at armpt(30,-40,~76) plus a neck down the anterolateral strip (th -45..0) to BOTTOM's top. Option: an anterolateral septum barrel at s 150, th -30.","main_risk":"1. The front seat is not as quiet as the back: scapula-frame skin at lat 30-45, lon -90..-120 has 8-11 mm spread, and the arm/hand reaches it in 8-38 % of the probe poses (across the body, comb hair). Across-body reach may collapse.\n2. J2 floats over the anterior deltoid / biceps root (arm-frame spread 10-15 mm).\n3. The bridge adds ~80-100 mm of base strap (in band, but mass).\n4. The front of the shoulder is where the shoulder-pad cover and clothing straps go.","verdict":"screened: best honest gap 68.26 (score 75.18) on Bench(60,160), level with the champion baseline 68.93 and behind the verified leaders; the optimum left the spec's AC-front seat for the front-TOP edge (lat 66), i.e. the front route converges toward the champion's top seat. Gate NOT converged (top-5 spread 3.81 > 3)."},{"id":"closure-set-arc-knee-3r","name":"Closure-set arc knee: hub 3 on a passive trunnion yoke whose tilt changes the J2-J3 arc, set by the radial closure through GH (fold lever, rotations only)","family":"offset axes + closure-set passive orientation DOF","lens":"round3:self-alignment / loop closure through cheap rotational passive joints","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:10:56Z","reach":0.6625,"follow":0.9833,"inband14":0.4867,"inband10":0.2756,"contact":0.0351,"gap":68.59,"v_gap":76.06,"v_reach":0.5889,"v_follow":0.9909,"v_inband14":0.4856,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":339,"doc":"docs/concepts/closure-set-arc-knee-3r.md","short":"Closure-set arc knee","description":"A skin-normal 3R (snh / osn seats) in which hub 3's housing sits in a fork at the end of link 2 on two trunnion pins, axis dK = u2 x u3 through the hub centre. Tilting the housing by psi changes the J2-J3 arc a2, so the fold window [|a1 - a2| + 10, a1 + a2 - 10] breathes with the pose. psi is set by the radial closure (the one direction the planar cuff pins cannot take); the in-plane drift goes to a pinned cuff link as in skin-normal-3r-pin-knee. Rest: hub level in its fork. Overhead / across the body the closure tilts it (+-31 deg), lifting the rim ~5 mm on one side.","topology":"scapula -> J1 ACTIVE R -> L1 -> J2 ACTIVE R -> L2 with fork -> K PASSIVE R (trunnion pair, axis u2 x u3 through hub 3) -> yoke = hub-3 housing -> J3 ACTIVE R (line 6.8 mm off GH) -> E arm -> pin a PASSIVE R -> cuff link -> pin b PASSIVE R -> arm pad -> humerus (closed by GH). M = 6(7-1-7) + 9 = 3. psi fixed by the u3-normal closure equation, qa/qb by the two-circle closure; no spring, no 4th active joint.","main_risk":"(1) Branch jumps (measured above): the closure root is not single-valued along the clips, so follow drops. (2) Conditioning: 1 mm liner compression or radial GH translation moves psi 2-5.7 deg; R10's real GH translation (15 mm rigid worst) can swing the knee tens of degrees - judge R10 before L1. (3) Unpowered the knee carries only ~0.05-0.07 N.m before the cuff exceeds 5 N. (4) c0 is tuned on the bench poses (fit); check held-out. (5) +-31 deg tilt lifts the rim ~5 mm; 6 angles to sense. Upper bound if psi were free: fold-free 0.973 vs 0.768 - the largest lever left, if it can be made well-conditioned.","verdict":"screened, far behind its own control: best honest 68.59 (score 75.06) on Bench(60,160) fast vs the SAME chain with the knee locked (pin dyad) 31.32; the knee is inert unless J2's line is offset (closed form: with J1 through GH the radial closure is F(q2, psi) = 0, a cam of the elbow), and with an explicit 24-30 mm J2 offset + the flank rule it removes the J2 fold kinematically (kin-ok 0.895, 0 fold vs 47, overhead 0.88) but the offset lifts hub 2 out of band and the knee's in-plane drift (hK sin dpsi, 70-90 mm span) spreads the pin dyad over the curved arm: in-band 0.49-0.58 vs 0.79. Not converged (CMA still gaining); farm CMA streams A-C queued. Not a candidate for score 90."},{"id":"coaxial-rus-turret","name":"Coaxial RUS turret: three stacked crank bearings on one posterior scapula seat, O5 rods to arm studs, the user's GH closes the loop","family":"human-closed RUS truss (spherical parallel mechanism on the user's GH)","lens":"deployable structures / overconstrained and spherical linkage kinematics","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":15,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T02:25:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:17:36Z","reach":0.0,"follow":0.0,"inband14":0.0,"inband10":0.0,"contact":0.0,"gap":275.0,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":4,"doc":"docs/concepts/coaxial-rus-turret.md","short":"Coaxial RUS turret","description":"Coaxial-input spherical parallel mechanism worn on the user (the humerus is the platform, GH its centre), all three legs on the single seat that is fold-free with many studs.","topology":"- One O20 hub shaft on the fold-free posterior-horizontal scapula seat carrying three crank layers (each on its own thin-section bearing, 4.5 mm per layer, stack 21.25 mm): the three ACTIVE crank axes coincide.\n- Each crank: arc beta_k, O12 U-joint at the tip, O5 rod to an O12 rod end on a ball stud on the arm saddle.\n- The user's GH closes the loops; no base brackets.","main_risk":"Studs that are fold-free for one axis cluster -> parallel singularity; stacked long cranks.","verdict":"KILLED after the coarse screen: 3 honest full-model configurations on the fold-free posterior seat: gap 320.51 / 358.13 / 275.0 (0 taken); red-team ceiling with collisions OFF 280.01 (reach 0.30, in-band 0.07). One fold-free seat forces the three studs onto one patch of the posterior humeral head, so the coaxial-input SPM is parallel-singular on 19-50 % of the workspace; the stacked 60-70 deg cranks still sweep under the arm (march ceiling); on the trunk-free branch each rod folds back over its own crank"},{"id":"convergent-spm-twistleg","name":"Convergent 2+1 SPM: a five-bar A-frame on the arm plus a third 'twist' leg, all actives on the scapula","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Convergent 2+1 SPM","description":"As in fivebar-roll, a two-legged A-frame on the dome holds a double pin on the upper-lateral arm. Here the arm's roll is set by a third, light leg from a third base hub to a second pin on the same arm plate, so nothing active rides the arm. REST: three base hubs spread over the posterior, top and anterior dome. The elbows are on the rim, the two converging couplers meet at the arm hub near the acromion edge, and the twist leg reaches a pin 25-45 deg away, posterolateral on the arm plate. OVERHEAD: the arm plate climbs to the lateral dome, the A-frame folds over the top, and the twist leg swings behind the acromion. ACROSS-BODY: as fivebar-roll; the twist leg crosses the posterior deltoid. BEHIND-BACK: the twist leg crosses the anterior deltoid.","topology":"S -> three ACTIVE base revolutes on the dome (O20 hubs, axes u1, u2, u3 through GH).\n- Legs 1 and 2: crank (arc a) -> elbow pin P14 -> coupler (arc c) -> passive pin on the arm about the SAME arm-fixed axis W (two coaxial pins in one double-row arm hub).\n- Leg 3: crank -> elbow pin -> distal link -> passive pin about a second arm-fixed axis v3 (P14 on the same arm plate, 25-45 deg from W).\n- Arm plate -> neck to BOTTOM.\n\n9 joints: 3 active, 6 passive. Every link after the base is a two-force member. It is a 3-RRR SPM with two coincident platform axes, which puts its metal differently from seed-spm-3rrr's ring of three spread platform pins.","main_risk":"Reach is the five-bar's times leg 3's. The twist leg must close in every pose and keep det away from 0, i.e. its distal plane must keep twisting the arm about W. It likely fails somewhere in large axial rotation (the bench poses include axial -180..135). Three legs means three elbows and three straps over the cap: more covered skin out of band if the elbows sit on the rim. Both arm pins must stay inside the rigid-cuff window (theta -20..+62). Six passive pins (joint count). Over-constrained in 6-DOF terms; ball studs at the arm pins fix that.","verdict":""},{"id":"core-cone-chain","name":"Conical CORE chain: rolling-contact joints in a single layer","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Conical CORE chain","description":"A flat chain: strap - disc|disc - strap - disc|disc - strap. Each joint is a pair of Ti discs (O14, 4.5 mm tall including the band) lying side by side in the skin's tangent plane, wrapped in a figure-8 of three thin bands. The links leave each disc on its far side, all in the same 4.5-5 mm layer. When a joint turns by ~2psi, the distal disc rolls psi around the proximal one (coin around coin), so the distal link's root orbits the joint instead of turning on a pin (psi <= 85 deg). Rest: the J1 pair is on the acromion top (scapula), the J2 pair over the posterior-superior shoulder and the J3 pair on the lateral deltoid. With discs 2-4 mm off the skin, every metal point is within about 9 mm of it. Overhead: the J1 and J2 pairs have rolled 50-80 deg and the straps fan around the pairs. No hub cap sits 12 mm up, so where the final's link-1 J2 hub went 2.7-6.7 mm under the back skin at…","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: a conical rolling-contact pair (CORE). The base-fixed cone segment C1A (axis c1A) and the link-1 cone segment C1B (axis c1B) have equal half-angles beta, their apex at GH, and touch along a generator. Three crossed 0.1 x 4 mm Ti or 17-7PH bands in a figure-8 hold them; ACTIVE coordinate: rolling angle psi1 -> link 1 (a 14 mm Ti strap in the same layer as the discs) -> J2: CORE pair C2A (link 1) / C2B (link 2), ACTIVE psi2 -> link 2 -> J3: CORE pair C3A (link 2) / C3B (the arm end on BOTTOM's neck), ACTIVE psi3 -> arm end. There are no pins and no stacked plates: each joint is two O14 x 4.5 mm discs side by side. Variant: unequal cones per joint (rolling ratio != 1).","main_risk":"These are not rolling bearings, so the user must accept rolling-contact joints. A pair carries compression at the contact. Separation and tilt moments ride on the bands alone: MVP1's <= 0.5 N.m of tilt means ~70 N of band load at rho 7 mm, and MVP2's 10 N.m is impossible without a retainer (a spherical cap or a lipped track). The two-disc footprint (28 x 14 mm) and the orbiting distal disc may cost reach, since hub diameter was worth 20 points. The cone apexes must meet GH within ~1 mm. Bands fatigue. Sensing a rolling joint is non-trivial: the relative angle is ~2psi, readable by a hall sensor on one disc and a magnet ring on its partner, or by IMUs.","verdict":""},{"id":"coupler-path-j1","name":"Four-bar base: J2 carried along a synthesised coupler path","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Four-bar base","description":"On the scapula sits a compact 'hand': two base hubs, a short crank and a short rocker holding link 1 at two points. Link 1 swings J2 not around a single base axis but along a synthesised coupler curve, for example one that keeps J2 over skin of nearly constant radius from GH (the circle about b crosses skin 58-93 mm from GH, which is the J2 zone's problem). Rest: J2 over the posterior-superior shoulder. Overhead: the coupler lifts and slides J2 over the top instead of carrying it down onto the medial scapula. Across-body: J2 swings anteriorly along the curve. Behind-back: J2 stays posterior-superior while link 2 reaches down the posterior arm. Link 2 and the arm hub are as in the baseline.","topology":"Scapula base with two hubs, A0 (crank pivot, ACTIVE) and B0 (rocker pivot, PASSIVE), axes through GH, 25-40 mm apart on the acromion/spine -> a crank (arc alpha) and a rocker (arc beta) -> the coupler = link 1, whose joints A1 and B1 are PASSIVE with axes through GH (together, a spherical four-bar). The coupler carries J2's axis p2 at a coupler point -> J2: R hub, ACTIVE -> link 2 -> J3: R hub, ACTIVE, on BOTTOM's arm end. 6 joints, 3 active.","main_risk":"The coupler also rotates link 1 in a coupled way, so J2's axis direction along the path is not free and the reachable set changes (it may lose workspace). Two extra hubs on the base add metal near the acromion, where the raised arm meets the base tab. Four-bar dead points or branch changes may fall inside the range. More parts.","verdict":""},{"id":"crossed-muscle-rails","name":"Crossed muscle-line rails (scapula-fixed acromial arch crossed by an arm-fixed humeral-head collar)","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Crossed muscle-line rails","description":"Two curved rails and one small double carriage, no links. Rail 1 (~10 x 5 mm Ti with a rolling-element track) is part of the exo-scapula: an arch over the top of the shoulder along the acromion / scapular spine, ~52-60 mm from the GH centre. That skin is the most stable of the shoulder in the scapula frame: 37-58 mm from GH both at rest and at 150 deg elevation (my lookup on the rig). Rail 2 is part of the arm piece: a collar arc around the top of the humeral head, ~20 deg above the arm's equator. This is the arm's low-motion band (delta 100-125; the proximity agent's best arm-fixed spot w(112,-15): point ceiling 99 %, disc ceiling 94 %). The collar carries on as the neck to BOTTOM. The rails cross at one point, where the double carriage holds the J2 bearing. Rest: the arch sits on the acromion, the collar's lateral arc lies under its lateral end over the deltoid cap, and they cross…","topology":"Spherical 3R with no strap links. Scapula base -> R1 = carriage 1 on a scapula-fixed circular arc rail (rail 1, the 'acromial arch': a small circle of half-angle a12 about axis a1 through GH, lying over the acromion / supraspinatus / spine) [ACTIVE] -> R2 = bearing between carriage 1 and carriage 2 on the radial axis a2 through the rails' crossing point (v5 O20 hub or a thin-section ring) [ACTIVE] -> R3 = carriage 2 on an arm-fixed circular arc rail (rail 2, the 'collar': a small circle of half-angle a23 about an arm-fixed axis a3, around the top of the humeral head just under the acromion edge; part of the arm piece that runs on to BOTTOM) [ACTIVE] -> arm. All three axes through GH. Passive in the chain: none (the user's torque-free cuff slides stay outside the count).","main_risk":"(1) Stack at the crossing: the upper rail's skin face sits Delta ~12-16 mm above the lower one's (rail 5 + carriage plate 3 + bearing 5-7 + plate 1). The crossing visits the whole travel of both rails, so the upper rail is ~15-20 mm off the skin wherever it crosses: probably outside 10 mm, marginal at 14 mm. (2) Rail length: the same screen needs rail-1 travel of ~120-200 deg (115-160 mm) and rail-2 travel of ~65-150 deg (80-180 mm) on the fit clips, and more for the workspace. That costs mass and stiffness: open curved cantilevers twist ~2-5 deg per 75 mm under 25 N.m (docs/research/ring_and_shell_hardware.md). The rail ends become joint limits that cap reach. (3) A constant-radius rail…","verdict":""},{"id":"crossed-skin-tracks","name":"Crossed skin tracks with a telescoping swivel","family":"skin tracks (crossing guides)","lens":"ideation A","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:24:14Z","reach":0.4688,"follow":0.65,"inband14":0.7029,"inband10":0.4066,"contact":0.0172,"gap":94.55,"v_gap":96.04,"v_reach":0.4625,"v_follow":0.65,"v_inband14":0.703,"v_contact":0.0259,"n_configs":387,"doc":"docs/concepts/crossed-skin-tracks.md","short":"Crossed skin tracks with a telescoping swivel","description":"Rest:\n- The halo sits on top of the shoulder, on scapula skin.\n- A second track, fixed to the arm, runs over the deltoid cap and up toward the acromion.\n- The only moving metal is the swivel block (about 25 x 25 x 13 mm: two carriages plus a ring bearing plus the slide) where the two tracks cross, over the posterior or posterolateral shoulder.\n\nOverhead and across-body: the arm track turns with the arm and its crossing with the halo slides along both tracks. Where the arm track passes over the halo, it rides above it.","topology":"- Scapula base (S): the halo track T_S, as in scapula-halo-track.\n- J1 ACTIVE: carriage A on T_S, coordinate s.\n- J2 ACTIVE: a thin-section swivel bearing between carriage A and carriage B. Its axis p2 is the radial direction at the crossing, through GH.\n- PASSIVE: a radial slide inside the swivel, along p2. The closure sets it; it takes up the radius difference of the two tracks at the crossing.\n- J3 ACTIVE: carriage B on the arm-fixed track T_H, coordinate t.\n- T_H merges with BOTTOM's neck. T_H seed: the arc of the circle {angle(v, W) = a2} that the J2 pole visits in the humerus frame, i.e. scapula-halo-track's link 2 + J3 replaced by a track. Alternative: the deltoid oval.","main_risk":"- Kinematic reach: in my screen, two unoptimised zone loops (scapula halo plus the deltoid oval) cross in only 40-59 % of the workspace and 37-86 % of clip frames. With T_H on the circle (W, a2), reach equals scapula-halo-track's 67-88 %, but that arc must cover the J3-like travel (180-290 deg of the circle over the workspace), and it passes over the top of the humeral head, which faces the neck at high elevation: contact risk.\n- Wherever the arm track can pass over the halo it must ride at the halo top + about 8 mm, i.e. 10-15 mm above scapula skin, at the edge of the band.\n- Two bogies and a swivel between two curved tracks make the tolerance chain long.","verdict":"dead after polish: gap 94.55 (score 65.6) vs bar 68.93 (74.9); sweep NOT converged by the gate but the kinematic ceiling of the polished best (reach_kin 0.65, follow_kin 0.72) leaves little room; overhead still not taken (crossing exists, off the 93 deg arm track)"},{"id":"crown-cospm","name":"Coaxial spherical parallel manipulator driven from a nested ring stack on the crown (bold)","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Coaxial spherical parallel manipulator driven from a nested ring stack on the crown","description":"At rest a flat ring stack sits on the crown, on skin that moves with S (48-55 mm from GH). Three thin curved legs fan out over the front, top-lateral and back of the deltoid to the arm saddle. Because the inputs are coaxial, rotation about a0 is unlimited, so the arm can sweep any azimuth around the crown pole. Overhead the legs fold toward the crown; across-body the front leg flattens over the anterior deltoid; behind-back the rear leg crosses the posterior deltoid.","topology":"S -> three ACTIVE R joints, all coaxial with a0 (S-local, near c* = [-0.38, 0.91, 0.16]): three concentric thin-section rings nested in one ~7 mm layer on the crown (RBC KA020XP0 50.8x63.5x6.35, KA025XP0 63.5x76.2, KA030XP0 76.2x88.9), or two nested and one stacked. Each rotor carries a proximal arc link (arc ap) -> passive R micro-hub (2x MR85-2RS 5x8x2.5, or 683ZZ 3x7x3, in an O12 boss, ~7 mm stack) -> distal arc link (arc ad) -> passive R micro-hub on the arm platform, axes v_i humerus-fixed through GH (platform = a C-saddle on the lateral upper arm under BOTTOM). Three RRR legs, every axis through GH.","main_risk":"Orientation workspace. Each leg needs v_i to stay within [|ap-ad| + m, ap + ad - m] of a0, while any humerus-fixed direction swings at least ~95 deg about the crown. That forces ap ~ ad ~ 80-90 deg: long legs over the deltoid. Parallel singularities inside the range are likely. Legs may collide with each other and with the arm across-body. Nine joints of metal over the shoulder.","verdict":""},{"id":"crown-ring-3r","name":"Crown ring 3R: J1 an open thin-section ring on the crown","family":"spherical 3R (J1 thin-section ring)","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T22:45:53Z","reach":0.9,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.1146,"inband10":0.0457,"contact":0.005,"gap":82.37,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":236,"doc":"docs/concepts/crown-ring-3r.md","short":"Crown ring 3R","description":"At rest the ring frames the acromion and supraspinatus. That skin moves with the base: measured today, the skin along S-fixed superior stays at 47.7-48.6 mm and along superior-posterior-lateral at 49.6-54.8 mm, from rest to 150 deg elevation. The ring sits like an eyelet with the bony crown inside its bore, 2-9 mm above the skin around it. Link 1 leaves the rotor and runs down the front or back of the crown to J2, 58-80 deg from the crown pole (top of the anterior or posterior deltoid). Link 2 runs to J3 on the lateral upper arm, 100-160 mm from GH, inside the -20..+62 deg arm sector. Overhead: the humerus never comes within 82 deg of c* (one-sided, all bench poses; 58 deg for plain superior), so J1 and the ring never meet the deltoid; J2 swings to the back or front of the crown. Across-body and behind-back: J1 turns, J2 travels around its circle, and link 2 is the only metal over the…","topology":"S (handler) -> J1 R active: 4-point-contact thin-section ring (RBC/Kaydon Reali-Slim KA025XP0 63.5x76.2x6.35 or KA030XP0 76.2x88.9x6.35, ~60-70 g). It lies flat on the shoulder crown (top-back of the acromion and supraspinatus), axis a1 through GH near c* = [-0.38, 0.91, 0.16] (S-local; S-local = thorax at rest because scap(0) = I). Stator on the handler, rotor = link 1 -> link 1 = rotor flange + 14 mm Ti strap from the ring rim to J2 at alpha12 = 58-80 deg from a1 -> J2 R active: donut hub (RBC KAA15XL0 38.1x47.6x4.76, 18 g, ~40 N.m static moment; or IKO CRBT305A 30x41x5), axis a2 through GH -> link 2 strap over the deltoid -> J3 R active: KAA15XL0 donut on the lateral upper arm, axis a3 humerus-fixed through GH (NOT the humerus axis). Its outer race sits in the arm-side saddle under BOTTOM. All axes meet at GH. Passive (outside the count): the cuff's self-aligning slides, zero travel in the bench.","main_risk":"alpha12 >= ~58 deg means J2 still travels a circle 58-80 deg off the crown. That is the same circle on which the baseline's J2 hub lands on the back over the medial scapula overhead, so this concept may only move J1's share of the problem. A flat ring on a dome tilts against the skin across its land: on a 55 mm-radius dome, a 76 mm ring's skin side drops ~6-7 mm across its section. The ring needs a stiff round housing (1.5 mm Ti both sides), so J1 alone weighs 60-90 g.","verdict":"screened, not worth a GPU sweep as a ring: the open ring at J1 loses to an O20 hub on the same axes by 20-26 gap at every size and seat (best ring passing the parking rule 82.37 vs its O20 control 56.04, k235); convergence gate NOT met (ring g2 70.05 / g3 70.05 / top-5 1.16 with the parking rule, 1.84 / 1.84 / 1.47 without)"},{"id":"cruciate-knuckle-3r","name":"Cruciate-ligament knuckles: a spherical 3R whose joints are rolling crossed four-bars instead of stacked hubs","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":9,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Cruciate-ligament knuckles","description":"It looks like the baseline's straps, but the members butt end to end at small knuckles instead of overlapping at O20 hubs. Each knuckle is two short Ti ligament bars (~20 mm) crossing in an X between the rounded ends of the two members, on 4 tiny bearings (O6-8). All members ride on ONE layer: no 3.15 mm step per joint and no 12.25 mm cap. The ligaments sit 3-6 mm above. The joint 'rolls' like the knee, or like the humeral head on the glenoid: its instantaneous axis travels along the member ends by half the rotation, spreading each joint's footprint. Rest, overhead, across-body and behind-back: the chain follows the path of the 3R it replaces (seeded from the baseline and from the crossed-rails seeds), with every member at the height it needs.","topology":"A spherical serial chain where each revolute is replaced by a spherical crossed four-bar 'knuckle': two crossing ligament bars between the ends of adjacent members, like the knee's cruciate ligaments. Scapula base -> knuckle 1 [1 ACTIVE input + 3 PASSIVE pivots] -> link 1 -> knuckle 2 [1 ACTIVE + 3 PASSIVE] -> link 2 -> knuckle 3 [1 ACTIVE + 3 PASSIVE] -> arm piece -> BOTTOM. All pivot axes pass through GH. Each knuckle's relative motion is a rolling of two pitch cones about a moving instantaneous axis.","main_risk":"Range: a crossed four-bar has ~150-170 deg of total travel before toggle, but well-conditioned travel is ~+-70 deg, and some 3R joints need more (J1 / J3 rotations can exceed 150 deg), so some poses become unreachable. Ligament strength and buckling under MVP2's 10 N.m (~1 kN at a 10 mm lever). 12 small bearings. Numerical IK. An unusual CAD.","verdict":""},{"id":"cuff-sleeve-3r","name":"Spherical 3R with the twist joint as a GH-aimed rod-and-sleeve near the cuff (ring-free, cable-free axial joint)","family":"","lens":"literature","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Spherical 3R with the twist joint as a GH-aimed rod-and-sleeve near the cuff","description":"The J1 hub sits on the anterior acromion. Link 1 reaches the J2 hub, which rides around the shoulder cap. Link 2 runs from J2 over the round deltoid cap and down the lateral arm to a short O5 rod near the lower upper arm (s ~230 mm, close to the user's BOTTOM bar), whose axis points back at GH. A slim sleeve (O11 x 30, two MR85 bearings that also float axially) on a small cuff bracket is the twist joint. There is no hub on the deltoid, no open ring around the arm and no arm-end neck. Overhead: link 2 lies along the raised arm's lateral (now medial-facing) side. Across-body: along the outer arm. Behind-back: down the posterior-lateral arm. Near GH, link 2 rides the deltoid cap, which is roughly a sphere about GH (4.7 mm radius spread), so turning about e3 keeps it at a nearly constant gap there.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1 R hub (ACTIVE, radial axis at the anterior acromion) -> link 1 (64 deg arc) -> J2 R hub (ACTIVE, radial) -> link 2 (over the deltoid cap and down the lateral arm) -> short rod coaxial with the arm-fixed line e3 through GH (about 13-14 deg off the humerus axis, grazing the lateral arm at s ~230 mm) -> J3 = sleeve bearing on the cuff (ACTIVE R about e3) with a 5 mm axial float (passive P) -> the user's cuff, with one passive lateral cuff slide outside the count.","main_risk":"The sweep principle (pipeline/arm-rom/RESEARCH_NOTES.md): between the cap and the sleeve (s ~70-150 mm), e3 runs inside the arm, so as the arm turns about e3 relative to link 2 (|J3| up to ~90 deg in the bench workspace, axial -180..135), link 2's mid part sweeps an annulus over the arm. A rough estimate gives a 3-5 mm gap at +/-45 deg and skin contact near +/-90 deg, so J3 travel may need capping and large-twist reach is lost. Link 2 is a ~170 mm cantilever (stiffness, mass). The sleeve's top sits about 14 mm off the skin.","verdict":""},{"id":"deltoid-oval-track","name":"Deltoid oval track: J3 is a closed cam track fixed to the arm, carrying the J2 hub (mirror of the halo track)","family":"","lens":"ideation A","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Deltoid oval track","description":"Rest:\n- A racetrack ring of about 60 x 125 mm (track 8 x 5 mm, about 310 mm long, about 65 g Ti) lies 2-5 mm over the lateral deltoid cap, fixed to the arm and joined to BOTTOM's top.\n- It reaches from just under the acromion edge (delta about 125) down to the deltoid belly (delta about 65), and from the posterolateral to the anterolateral cap.\n- The J1 hub sits on the top of the lateral acromion. A 70 deg link 1 runs down over the acromion edge to the carriage and J2 hub on the oval.\n\nOverhead: the oval rotates up with the arm and the carriage slides toward the part of the oval nearest the acromion, so link 1 stays short over the top. Across-body and behind-back: the carriage runs to the anterior or posterior end of the oval. No link 2, no J3 hub, no separate neck.","topology":"Scapula base (S) -> J1 ACTIVE: O20 hub, axis b on the lateral acromion top. b = bvec(106, 64) in the scapula frame: the radial ray is within 30 deg of the skin normal there, and the skin moves at most 15 mm. -> link 1: a strap with arc a1 of about 70 deg. -> J2 ACTIVE: an O20 hub on a carriage. Its axis p2 is the radial direction through the carriage, with angle(b, p2) = a1. -> J3 ACTIVE: the carriage runs on a closed oval track fixed to the arm on the deltoid cap. The oval is centred at delta 95 / az -10 with semi-axes of 30 deg in delta and 60 deg in az. Its coordinate t is the joint value. -> The oval is the arm end and merges with BOTTOM's top and neck.","main_risk":"- Arm skin under a part fixed to the arm still moves 11-25 mm over the clips (the best arm spot moves 11-12 mm), and the oval's posterior end reaches skin that moves 36-50 mm. Its in-band ceiling is therefore lower than the scapula halo's.\n- The top of the oval goes under the acromion edge at elevation, where the deltoid bunches: contact and squeeze risk.\n- A long track adds mass on the arm, inertia that the arm must carry.\n- Near-tangent crossings: same singularity issue as the halo track.","verdict":""},{"id":"deltoid-strut-tripod","name":"Deltoid-strut tripod: three arc struts on the paths of the three deltoid heads (spherical 3-RPR parallel)","family":"","lens":"ideation A","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Deltoid-strut tripod","description":"Three thin curved struts run over the shoulder from scapula anchors to the arm saddle, along the paths of the deltoid's anterior, middle and posterior heads, and lengthen and shorten as those heads do. Each strut slides through a swivelling guide on its scapula anchor, and its free end sticks out past the anchor, away from the arm. REST: the middle strut runs from the acromion top down to the lateral deltoid; the anterior one from the clavicle/upper-trapezius anchor over the front of the shoulder; the posterior one from the scapular spine over the back. OVERHEAD: the arm anchors rise toward the top, the struts shorten, and their tails slide out over the trapezius / clavicle / upper back. ACROSS-BODY: the posterior strut lengthens over the back of the shoulder and the anterior one shortens. BEHIND-BACK: the anterior strut lengthens over the front of the shoulder and the posterior one…","topology":"Three legs in parallel from the scapula to an arm saddle. Leg i: S -> passive swivel about b_i: an O20 v5 hub with a guide on top, on stable scapula skin (top; anterior-medial over the clavicle/upper trapezius; posterior over the scapular spine) -> active arc slide: strut i is a Ti arc on the great circle through b_i and W_i, sliding through the guide; its angular length d_i between b_i and W_i is the active coordinate -> passive hinge about W_i: an O20 v5 hub on a humerus-fixed deltoid saddle (anterior, lateral and posterior deltoid, ~15 deg below the GH level) -> H. Active: d1, d2, d3. Passive: 6 swivels/hinges, set by closure.","main_risk":"Three moving arcs cross the gap (3x the trombone's moving metal), with three tails. In the start set, strut lengths reach ~155 deg, which would wrap past the shoulder dome into the chest/neck; anchors with d_max <~120 deg are needed. Struts that cross each other need radial layers, +5 mm each. Workspace conditioning is poor (cond p98 48, |det| p2 0.04), and the anterior-medial anchor comes within 25 deg of the arm on the workspace. Reading the arm pose from three strut lengths needs forward kinematics (Newton on SO(3)). Six hubs.","verdict":""},{"id":"deltoid-trilateration","name":"Muscle-arc trilateration: three telescoping 'deltoid' arcs whose lengths are the active joints","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Muscle-arc trilateration","description":"Three curved struts lie along the geodesics of the shoulder cap, like the anterior, middle and posterior deltoid wrapping the humeral head. Their origins are pads with radial pivots on the exo-scapula's top / posterior ground. Their insertions are three pads on the arm's cap band, tied by an arm-fixed bridge to BOTTOM. Each strut is a band 10-16 mm wide at a constant radius just over the cap (skin face ~3 mm above the pads). Rest: the struts run down from the acromion region over the front, side and back of the cap. Overhead: the middle strut shortens as the insertion rises under the acromion, and the posterior one lengthens. Across-body: the posterior strut is long and the anterior short. Behind-back: the reverse. Axial twist shows up as a difference between the three lengths.","topology":"Parallel. Three legs, each R-P-R along a great circle about GH, from a scapula-fixed pad A_k (origin: anterior, middle and posterior heads) to an arm-fixed pad B_k (insertion on the cap band): radial pivot at A_k [PASSIVE] -> curved telescoping arc whose angular length theta_k = angle(A_k, B_k) is the 'muscle length' [ACTIVE] -> radial pivot at B_k [PASSIVE] -> arm (pads tied by an arm bridge to BOTTOM). Realised as a lazy-tongs scissor or a 3-stage curved telescope.","main_risk":"Span ratio: for every scapula / arm point pair, the arc angle varies by at least 2.1x (best pairs: 75-165 deg). For good-ground pairs it varies 2.6x on the fit clips and 5.4x over the workspace (top / tubercle: 46-128 deg on the clips, 27-150 deg in the workspace; my check). A 2-stage telescope (< 1.8x) cannot do this. A lazy-tongs leg can (3-5x), but brings 15-25 small pivots, varying width and backlash; a 3-stage telescope is thick. Long struts at 150-170 deg approach the undefined-plane singularity. The struts cross each other under twist (stacking). Sensing singularity: when the three leg normals become coplanar, the lengths stop determining the pose.","verdict":""},{"id":"dome-telescope-tripod","name":"Dome tripod: three curved telescopes between scapula and deltoid (CAREX made rigid, spherical 3-RPR)","family":"","lens":"literature","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Dome tripod","description":"Three slender telescoping arcs (12 x 6.5 mm channels) fan out from the acromion and spine over the shoulder cap to the anterior, lateral and posterior deltoid, like tent poles. The arm plate on the deltoid is the only arm-side metal and a strap joining the three base pivots is the only base metal. Rest: legs are about 60-100 deg long; the front leg runs over the anterior acromion, the middle one over the top, the back one over the posterior shoulder. Overhead: the deltoid rises under the acromion, the lateral and posterior legs shorten and swing up, and the anterior leg rolls over the front. Across-body: the posterior leg stretches over the back of the shoulder and the anterior one shortens. Behind-back: the anterior leg stretches across the front of the shoulder and the posterior one shortens. Each leg is an arc of constant radius about GH.","topology":"Three legs in parallel between the scapula base and an arm plate on the deltoid. Each leg: base pivot (passive R, radial axis through GH, on the acromion / scapular spine) -> curved telescope = spherical prismatic along the great circle b_i -> a_i (ACTIVE: an outer Ti channel arc fixed to the base pivot and an inner bar arc fixed to the arm pivot, sliding on caged balls) -> arm pivot (passive R, radial axis through GH) on a rigid arm plate -> the user's passive cuff slides absorb GH translation (outside the count).","main_risk":"Range. A frames-only check found that even the best single leg (base on the posterior scapula, arm point on the antero-lateral deltoid) spans 85-177 deg over the bench poses. A 2-segment telescope covers only about L - 15 deg of range, and the great circle degenerates near 180 deg, so reach may stay well under the baseline's ~78 %. Constant-radius arcs cannot follow skin whose GH radius runs 47 mm (top) to 73 mm (back), so in-band share is uncertain. Legs may cross, and layering one of them costs 6.5 mm. Near a parallel singularity the unpowered plate goes loose.","verdict":""},{"id":"dwell-fourbar-knee-3r","name":"Dwell four-bar knee (Watt six-bar) 3R: a crank-rocker loop between link 1 and a split link 2 shortens link 2 only near the folded side, with pins only and no spring","family":"spherical 3R with a polycentric (Watt six-bar) middle joint; all axes through GH","lens":"round4: move the fold boundary with a determined element (spherical four-bar replacing one link so the effective J2-J3 arc varies with the pose)","status":"modelling","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T04:40:00Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":272,"doc":"","short":"Dwell four-bar knee (Watt six-bar) 3R","description":"This is the leader's chain with link 2 split at a knee 40 deg from J2 (on the J2-J3 great circle, chi about 0) and closed by a short rocker.\n\nParts of the loop:\n- Pin P sits on link 1, 17.8 deg from J2, over the posterior acromion.\n- Pin Q sits on L2b, 15 deg from the knee.\n- A 44.6-deg rocker strap C joins P to Q.\n\nHow it moves:\n- Over the leader's working range the loop sits near its dwell: the knee stays within a few deg of straight, so a2_eff stays about 73.7, which is the leader.\n- As J2 folds toward the leader's folded margin, the loop pulls the knee in, and a2_eff shortens to 61.0. The fold therefore moves from theta 43.1 to 23.1.\n- The knee angle is a fixed function of q2, so all three encoders stay J1-J3 and MVP2 still drives everything with 3 motors.\n\nWhere the metal sits:\n- P, the rocker and hub 2 sit on the posterior acromion (0.96-0.98 in band).\n- The knee and Q sit at the…","topology":"scapula -> J1 ACTIVE R (O20) -> link 1 (carries J2 and pin P) -> J2 ACTIVE R (O15, sensed) -> L2a (40 deg) -> knee K PASSIVE R -> L2b (carries Q and J3) -> J3 ACTIVE R (O15) -> K7 neck -> BOTTOM; loop closure: link 1 -> P PASSIVE R -> rocker C (44.6 deg) -> Q PASSIVE R -> L2b. All axes through GH; spherical Gruebler: 6 bodies, 6 revolutes, M = 3(6 - 1) - 2*6 = 3.","main_risk":"1. Pin Q: the posterior axillary fold (poses #93 / #92 / #115) parks it high. It must be moved, or those poses dropped by q, at some in-band cost.\n2. Four pins plus a rocker within 20-45 deg of each other near J2 and the knee is a stacking problem: rocker vs L2a vs link 1 layers. The proxy has no K6.\n3. Spread is loop-tolerance sensitive: the three pins must meet at GH within about 0.5 deg, or the loop binds (R17).\n4. The search is not converged: 3 local streams, top-3 spread 9 gest.","verdict":""},{"id":"far-end-posterior-3r","name":"Far-end posterior 3R (fold-free posterior J1 line, hubs at either end of their axis lines, raglan-routed links)","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"weird-tailoring (far-end hubs)","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:20:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T01:30:30Z","reach":0.6438,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.2792,"inband10":0.2025,"contact":0.2752,"gap":118.55,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":256,"doc":"docs/concepts/far-end-posterior-3r.md","short":"Far-end posterior 3R","description":"The far-end scan found a fold-free J1 line (straight back from GH, horizontal) whose hub stays in one band in 100 % of poses; this morphology builds the 3R on it with every hub-end choice and routed links.","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R (O20 hub on the posterior-horizontal GH line bvec(-24, 4), at either end) -> routed link 1 -> J2: ACTIVE R (hub at either end of its GH line) -> routed link 2 -> J3: ACTIVE R (posterior arm wdir(80, -90), or its far end) -> neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Measured, fatal: the fold-free arcs send link rays through the neck / chest in 10-20 % of poses (links park at 400 mm) and every far end of a GH line lies in the torso, axilla or neck.","verdict":"killed after the coarse sweep: best honest 118.55 (best overall 98.03, links parked at 400 mm) vs the plain 3R control 51.33; the fold-free posterior layout sends link rays through the neck / chest in 10-20 % of closing poses and every far-end hub choice is worse than the near end"},{"id":"fivebar-roll","name":"Spherical five-bar pointer plus an active arm roll","family":"spherical five-bar + roll","lens":"closed-loop","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:08:53Z","reach":0.6312,"follow":0.8833,"inband14":0.3442,"inband10":0.1849,"contact":0.0,"gap":89.13,"v_gap":89.13,"v_reach":0.6312,"v_follow":0.8833,"v_inband14":0.3442,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":20,"doc":"docs/concepts/fivebar-roll.md","short":"Spherical five-bar pointer plus an active arm roll","description":"Two cranks on the scapula dome and two couplers form a closed spherical A-frame. Its apex is one hub on the upper arm, and the arm turns about that hub (roll) relative to the A-frame. REST: the base hubs sit side by side on the posteromedial dome (or the anterolateral dome, see sweep). The elbows are ~60 deg out on the rim, and the couplers converge on the arm hub at the deltoid / acromion edge. OVERHEAD: the arm hub climbs to the lateral dome, the A-frame folds (the cranks swing toward the hub) and the couplers get short over the top. ACROSS-BODY: the hub goes anterior and the A-frame opens forward over the front of the shoulder. BEHIND-BACK: the hub goes posterior-down; one coupler crosses the posterior deltoid, the other the lateral cap. The arm hub is the only moving joint that carries moment; the elbows are small pins about 8 mm tall.","topology":"Two branches close on one arm hub at the arm-fixed axis W:\n- Branch 1: S -> R_A1 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis u1) -> crank 1 (arc a1) -> R_B1 (passive elbow pin P14, axis e1) -> coupler 1 (arc c1, two-force member) -> R_C (passive, axis W).\n- Branch 2: S -> R_A2 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis u2) -> crank 2 (arc a2) -> R_B2 (passive elbow pin, axis e2) -> coupler 2 (arc c2) -> R_C.\n- u1 = u2 is allowed: a coaxial 'rhombus' with one double-row base hub.\n- Coupler 1 -> R_D (ACTIVE roll, about the same axis W as R_C) -> arm-end plate on the upper-lateral arm -> neck to BOTTOM.\n\n6 joints: 3 active (A1, A2, D), 3 passive (B1, B2, C). The arm hub at W stacks three bodies (arm / coupler 1 / coupler 2) on two bearing rows. Variant: replace D by a third base-driven leg (see convergent-spm-twistleg).","main_risk":"The workspace needs long cranks: crank 45 closes 0.87 of the poses, crank 30 only 0.66. At full reach the elbows ride 60 deg from their bases, which is over the rim where the champion's J2 loses its points. Two elbows and two couplers are more metal over the cap than one J2 and link 2. The best base pairs in the screen are only 20 deg apart (near-rhombus), and the parallel singularity (couplers aligned at W) may eat into that reach. The triple stack at W is taller (~16 mm, two bearing rows) unless coupler 2 rides an outer ring around the hub. The posteromedial bases the screen prefers sit where the trapezius bunches overhead. Like the champion's JN, the arm hub is on arm skin: keep W inside…","verdict":"not worth a GPU sweep: best gap 89.13 vs the champion 3R 68.93 (Bench(60,160), march, K2 dense, K6); the skin-aware prefilter over ~17 000 configurations shows the same reach-vs-proximity front, worse than the 3R's (4 rigid arcs + 2 elbows over the cap instead of 2 arcs + 1 elbow)"},{"id":"floating-centre-3r","name":"Floating-centre 3R: the spherical chain on a closure-set 3-axis float (stage beside J1), rigid cuff","family":"spherical 3R + passive base float","lens":"literature","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:37:06Z","reach":0.9062,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.3183,"inband10":0.2269,"contact":0.0,"gap":58.17,"v_gap":135.93,"v_reach":0.5,"v_follow":0.7182,"v_inband14":0.1725,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":41,"doc":"docs/concepts/floating-centre-3r.md","short":"Floating-centre 3R","description":"It is the champion 3R, except that J1's hub sits on a small float plate in the base and the arm member is rigid. As the handler slips over the bone (up to ~15 mm overhead, judge/base_slip.py) and the GH translates, the float lets the whole chain stay centred on the real GH. The links over the deltoid and arm keep their designed gap to the arm skin; only the base plate rides the slipping skin. At rest the float is centred; overhead (largest slip) it is displaced up to its travel; across-body and behind-back, by the slip law's few mm. The metal placement is the parent 3R's, plus about 5 mm under J1.","topology":"Scapula base plate on the user's handler -> passive 3-axis float (two crossed miniature roller slides in the base tangent plane, +/-12 mm, plus a +/-4 mm normal slide, about 5 mm under J1's hub) -> J1 R (ACTIVE) -> link 1 -> J2 R (ACTIVE) -> link 2 -> J3 R (ACTIVE, arm hub) -> arm member rigid on the user's cuff (no cuff slides).","main_risk":"Today's bench has no GH-vs-scapula translation, so on the plain bench this concept equals its parent; it needs the slip extension below to show anything. It is a topology modifier applicable to any spherical chain more than a new linkage. The float adds ~5 mm under J1 and ±12 mm of base travel. When the slipping skin carries the base and the bone carries the arm, it is unclear which side should stay aligned (the scapula-skin metal now gets -delta). The chain's weight moves onto the cuff (R11, small). In MVP2 only moments pass the float, so assist forces go through the human GH, as in all self-aligning exos.","verdict":"Not worth a GPU sweep as a morphology. Plain bench: gap 58.17 vs its own parent's 65.05 on the same code, but the gain is the skin-contoured float plate's samples (0.83 in band); the chain itself gets worse (chain-only in-band 14 0.250 -> 0.161, plate-excluded gap 73.95). Slip bench (the float's real claim): direction right (in-band 0.35-0.41 vs cuff-slide parent 0.19-0.20) but the travel the slip law needs (+-20 tangent, 6-9 mm normal) makes the plate too big: best 73.46 (central) / 136.74 (high) vs the cuff-slide parent's 71.03 / 70.42."},{"id":"fold-sph3r","name":"Spherical 3R on fold-frontier axes","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"fold-frontier","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T00:04:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T01:33:17Z","reach":0.9,"follow":0.9667,"inband14":0.4577,"inband10":0.3184,"contact":0.0049,"gap":44.72,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":5636,"doc":"docs/concepts/fold-sph3r.md","short":"Spherical 3R on fold-frontier axes","description":"The plain spherical 3R with its axes re-seeded from the fold frontier (tools/fold_frontier.py, docs/research/fold/frontier.json): the (J1, J3, a1, a2) layouts that keep the J1-J3 axis angle inside the closure band for 85-97 % of the bench workspace at orientation level. Rest / overhead / across: as any spherical 3R; the frontier layouts put J1 anterolateral and nearly horizontal (front of the acromion) and J3 roughly radial on the lateral arm.","topology":"- Scapula base (S) on the user's scapula handler.\n- J1 ACTIVE: O20 hub (preloaded MR117-2RS pair, 12.25 mm stack), axis b = bvec(az, el) through GH.\n- Link 1: 12 x 3 mm strap on the great circle J1 -> J2 (arc a1).\n- J2 ACTIVE: O20 hub, axis through GH (two-circle closure, branch sigma).\n- Link 2: 12 x 3 mm strap J2 -> J3 (arc a2), passing over link 1 and the neck.\n- J3 ACTIVE: O20 hub, axis W = wdir(wd, wa) fixed in the humerus.\n- Arm-end plate + 14 x 6 neck rigid to BOTTOM's bar (member 3). No cuff slide.","main_risk":"Proximity: a scapula-fixed hub on a near-horizontal anterolateral ray sits over the anterior deltoid / humeral head, which moves under it; short arcs make the links sweep over the neck and chest.","verdict":"screened, the best plain spherical 3R so far (0 passive translations, 0 cuff slides): 44.72 at q 0.98 (score 83.7; near-touch gap 53.66, metal 0.00 mm in 8 poses) and 49.03 at q 1.0 (score 82.2, near-touch clean), vs 51.33 at rail-elbow k2221's axes and 65.05 / 68.93 at the champion's; not 90+. The fold frontier does not transfer to a concurrent chain: the 792 frontier seeds score 74.4-170 (median 118) because the fold-free J1 / J2 rays run inside the arm / trunk (hub parking, tools/fold_parking.py), and CMA walks every frontier start back to J1 over the acromion; gate converged over 4790 CMA tries (0.60 / 0.29 / 0.01 score pts). Frozen Bench(110,360) exact: 57.99 at k2221's axes (51.33…"},{"id":"foldfree-rus-truss","name":"Fold-free human-closed 3-RUS truss: three scapula crank hubs, O5 rods to arm ball studs, the user's GH closes the loop","family":"human-closed RUS truss (spherical parallel mechanism on the user's GH)","lens":"deployable structures / overconstrained and spherical linkage kinematics","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":15,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T02:16:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:17:36Z","reach":0.0,"follow":0.0,"inband14":0.0,"inband10":0.0,"contact":0.0,"gap":275.0,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":6,"doc":"docs/concepts/foldfree-rus-truss.md","short":"Fold-free human-closed 3-RUS truss","description":"Leg-fold theorem: each RUS leg closed by GH is a spherical RRR with its OWN fold band angle(c_k, Q_k) in [|beta_k - gamma_k|, beta_k + gamma_k]; the truss gives three independently placed bands instead of the 3R's one, seeded from the fold-free (c, q) pairs of docs/research/fold/ends.json (posterior-horizontal crank axis + posterior humeral-head stud: fold-free at 65 / 65).","topology":"- Scapula base (S) on the user's scapula handler: three O20 crank hubs on GH rays (ACTIVE revolutes) joined by 12 x 3 base brackets.\n- Each hub carries a crank of arc beta_k (12 x 3 Ti strap); at its tip an O12 U-joint (passive, 2 rot).\n- O5 Ti rod (rigid, its bow fitted) to an O12 rod end on a ball stud (passive, 3 rot) on a 14 x 4 arm saddle at the humeral-head level.\n- No exo joint at GH: the user's glenohumeral joint closes all three loops.","main_risk":"Crank parking (a 60-85 deg crank circle sweeps the arm / trunk), the parallel singularity with clustered studs, and ~15 fitted rigid parts on one shoulder.","verdict":"KILLED after the coarse screen: 5 honest full-model configurations seeded from the fold-free legs take no pose (gap 275.0), the red-team ceiling with every part-part collision OFF is 253.41 (reach 0.34, in-band 0.18, contact 0.53) vs the leader 26.34 fast / 33.84 frozen exact. The fold lever works PER LEG (leg-fold theorem: each RUS leg closed by GH is a spherical RRR with its own band; 188/280 seat-stud pairs fold-free, legs valid 0.93-0.98 in the model) but the truss dies on crank parking, the parallel singularity and part count"},{"id":"fourbar-virtual-joint","name":"Polycentric 3R: J2 (and optionally J3) replaced by a spherical four-bar 'virtual joint' with force-only pins","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Polycentric 3R","description":"It looks like the champion 3R, except that the J2 hub on the rim is gone. Two short rockers join a plate on the dome (L1) to the plate that carries the arm hub (L2), with four small pins. REST: the J1 hub is on the dome top. L1 is a small plate with its two pins 15-25 deg down from J2's old spot, still on stable skin. The two rockers cross over the acromion edge, and L2 runs from its pins to the arm hub. OVERHEAD: the four-bar folds, and the crossing point (the virtual J2) slides along the rim, so L2 rolls over the top of the shoulder like a polycentric knee, instead of pivoting about a fixed spot whose skin radius runs 58-93 mm. ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: J1 swings the whole assembly forward / backward, and the rockers' crossing tracks the shoulder's front / back edge.","topology":"S -> J1 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis a) -> L1 (plate on the dome) -> spherical four-bar:\n- L1 carries pins p1 (ACTIVE: rocker-1 angle) and p2 (passive);\n- rocker 1 (arc lambda1) and rocker 2 (arc lambda2) are two-force members;\n- L2 (the coupler) carries pins q1, q2 (passive).\nL2 -> J3 (ACTIVE, O20 hub, arm-fixed axis W) -> arm-end plate -> neck to BOTTOM.\n\nAll axes pass through GH. 6 joints: 3 active (J1, p1, J3), 3 passive (p2, q1, q2). Rockers that cross give a polycentric joint whose coupler 'rolls' round the rim. Open rockers give a 'morphing link 2' that bends with the J2 angle. Option: a second four-bar in place of J3, with ground pins on the arm at the low-motion patch / BOTTOM saddle, so the deltoid-belly hub disappears.","main_risk":"The IK is numerical: two unknowns, several roots, and toggle positions that are easy to hit across a workspace this large (the bench includes axial -180..135). The champion's J3 angle wraps through +-180 on the bench poses, so the arm hub must stay a full-turn bearing. Four pins plus two rockers are more metal near the rim than one hub. The gain may be small if the virtual J2 path cannot both roll with the skin and keep the reach. More pins add friction and backlash (minor with MR bearings). Modelling cost is the highest in this set.","verdict":""},{"id":"fraction-pauldron","name":"Fraction pauldron: graded telescoping lames on a gimbal (each lame turns a fixed fraction of the outer angle, rolling-cage coupled), J2 hub, twist on a cuff arc rail","family":"graded lames / distributed fraction","lens":"proximity-atlas","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":4,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T18:07:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:42:55Z","reach":0.2562,"follow":0.95,"inband14":0.5662,"inband10":0.3448,"contact":0.0392,"gap":101.68,"v_gap":104.42,"v_reach":0.2562,"v_follow":0.95,"v_inband14":0.5388,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":203,"doc":"docs/concepts/fraction-pauldron.md","short":"Fraction pauldron","description":"The proximity atlas says the cap skin moves with graded fractions of the arm swing (~0.1 over the top, ~0.8 over the deltoid). This is the realisable form of that idea: only fractions of ONE angle about ONE axis can be coupled exactly with 1-D elements, so the gimbal's outer angle is spread over telescoping lames like a lobster-tail pauldron.","topology":"scapula plate S -> N-1 lames telescoping on circular tracks about one scapula-fixed axis x through GH (lame k at a fixed fraction g_k of the outer angle alpha; rollers on lame k roll between lames k-1 and k+1, bearing-cage principle) -> tip lame T (g=1) -> J2: O20 hub about z (perpendicular to x, carried by T) -> arm strap A -> J3: carriage on a humerus-fixed thin-section arc rail around the upper arm (cone gamma about the humerus axis) -> K7 neck to BOTTOM","main_risk":"","verdict":"dead: best gap 101.7 vs the 3R baseline 68.93 (in-band 0.57 beats its 0.44, reach 0.26 kills it)"},{"id":"geodesic-strap-gimbal","name":"Geodesic strap gimbal: J2 as a self-stowing strap between two hubs","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Geodesic strap gimbal","description":"Only two hubs: J1 on the acromion top (scapula) and J3 on the anterolateral deltoid (arm). They are joined by one 20 mm strap that always runs the shortest way between them over the shoulder, 2-5 mm above the skin. There is no third hub and no link elbow. Over the bench workspace the hub separation is 23-157 deg (39-135 deg on the clips) for the default pair b = bvec(70,35), W = w_dir(55,30), which has a 22.5 deg singular margin (computed here on the 470 bench poses). Rest (arm hanging): the hubs are ~85-90 deg apart and the strap runs from the acromion over the lateral shoulder down to the deltoid. Abduction 90 deg: about 40 deg apart. Overhead: the hubs come within ~25-35 deg of each other over the top of the shoulder; the strap is short and its tail is wound in the spool beside J3. Across-body: the hubs separate and the strap is long over the front of the shoulder. Behind-back: the…","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: R hub, ACTIVE (axis b through GH, on the acromion top) -> link 1 = J1's rotor + the deployed part of a strap lying on the great circle G perpendicular to p2 = unit(b x W_S). The strap is anchored at the J1 rotor, so it is link-1-fixed -> J2, ACTIVE: the strap's extension s = angle(b, W_S). Link 2's guide block (rollers pinching the strap, one encoder roller) slides along the strap, which is a rotation about p2 -> link 2 = the guide block + a spool (the strap tail winds onto an O16 constant-force spool) + a short arm to the J3 rotor -> J3: R hub, ACTIVE (axis W through GH, on the anterolateral deltoid; its lower member is BOTTOM's arm end). Passive: the spool angle (set by s) and the guide block's radial slide (set by the strap's radial profile). a1 = a2 = 90 deg (a gimbal). Generalisation: a1 = a2 = gamma < 90 deg puts the strap on the small circle of angular radius gamma about p2. Realisation: a Bi-STEM pair of superelastic NiTi tapes (0.3 x 20 mm), or a 2-3 stage band-synchronised telescoping Ti arc.","main_risk":"Gimbal lock: with hub-able axes the best singular margin found over the bench workspace is only ~20-22 deg (the strap must cover 23-157 deg). Near that margin J1 and J3 spin fast and an unpowered chain can hesitate. The deployed strap is a curved cantilever up to ~150 mm long; it needs Bi-STEM or telescoping stages, and twisting under load misaligns J3. The strap's radius profile is fixed in link 1's frame, so where the skin under the geodesic changes with the arm (the deltoid end) it must stand off. The spool and guide block (~20 x 20 x 9 mm) beside J3 add a lump on the deltoid. Joint technology: J2 is a strap-and-roller slide, not a bearing joint, so it needs the user's acceptance.","verdict":""},{"id":"gh-tripod-telescoping-struts","name":"GH tripod: three telescoping struts from quiet scapular seats to quiet arm strips, with the human shoulder as the spherical joint (bold / weird)","family":"","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":12,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"GH tripod","description":"Scapula seats sit on the quietest base skin: B1 acromion top, B2 posterior-superior, B3 posterior scapula at GH level. Arm seats sit on the quiet strips: C1 anterolateral at s 110, C2 posterolateral at s 150, C3 on BOTTOM's outer face at s ~220.\n- Rest: three struts fan down from the top/back of the shoulder to the lateral arm.\n- Overhead: the arm seats rise above the shoulder, and the struts shorten and bunch over the top.\n- Across the body: the posterior struts stretch over the back of the arm.\n- Behind the back: the anterior strut stretches over the front.\nThere are no hubs anywhere on the shoulder cap.","topology":"Three legs, each U - P - U:\n- U: passive cardan on a scapula-fixed seat.\n- P: active telescoping strut, Ti tube in tube, OD 8.\n- U: passive cardan on an arm-fixed seat (a small proximal cuff, or BOTTOM's outer face).\nThe exo has no rotation centre; the human GH is the only spherical constraint (a 3-UPU parallel mechanism about the human joint).","main_risk":"1. Straight members over a convex, bulging shoulder: a chord between a scapular seat and an arm seat passes through the deltoid unless it stands off. Expect most strut length out of band and many poses with a strut inside the deltoid.\n2. Extension ratio: an arm seat moves ~200 mm between rest and overhead, so telescopes need ~2:1.\n3. Singular poses where the three moment vectors become coplanar (to be measured).\n4. It relies on the arm's own GH: soft-tissue compliance at the cuffs becomes tracking error, and the assist load in MVP2 passes through the human joint.\nI expect this to score poorly on in-band. Its value is as a bound and as an R10-free reference.","verdict":""},{"id":"human-closed-crank-rods","name":"Human-closed 3-RUS truss: dome cranks and thin rods to arm studs, with the user's own GH joint as the centre","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":15,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Human-closed 3-RUS truss","description":"Three short cranks turn on the top / posterior / anterior dome. From each crank tip a thin rod runs down over the shoulder to a ball stud on the arm. The arm is held like the platform of a small hexapod, with the user's shoulder joint as the pivot. REST:\n- crank hubs on the acromion top (bvec(60,75)), the scapular spine root (bvec(-10,40)) and the lateral clavicle end (bvec(150,40));\n- rods down the lateral, posterolateral and anterolateral deltoid to studs on the BOTTOM bar and the posterolateral saddle, 100-160 mm down the arm.\nOVERHEAD: the studs come up beside the acromion, the rods shorten their span (rod-endpoint distances on the bench poses: acromion-top to lateral arm at 160 mm runs 142-222 mm), and the cranks swing to take up the distance change. ACROSS-BODY: the posterior rod lies across the back of the shoulder and the anterior rod gets short. BEHIND-BACK: the anterior rod…","topology":"S -> three crank hubs on the stable dome (ACTIVE revolutes, O20; spherical cranks whose axes c_k pass through GH, so the crank tips ride a circle on the skin sphere) -> crank tip -> U-joint (passive, 2) -> straight or slightly bowed Ti rod (O5 tube) -> ball stud (passive, 3) on an arm saddle (lateral BOTTOM bar region plus the posterolateral arm, inside the rigid-cuff window).\n\nThe exo has NO joint at the GH centre: the user's own glenohumeral joint is the closing spherical joint. 9 joints: 3 active, 6 passive (15 passive coordinates).","main_risk":"Straight rods over a convex cap: the chord between a dome point and an arm point passes INSIDE the deltoid unless the ends are lifted or the rod is bowed. My rest-pose check put a straight acromion-to-lateral-arm rod ~38 mm inside the lateral deltoid at GH height. Bowing helps but makes the rod a curved bending member. Expect poor in-band unless the rods run in the posterior and anterior 'valleys' (axillary folds), which squeeze in adduction.\nCrank size: covering a rod-endpoint distance range of ~80 mm needs crank radii ~40 mm (a big circle on the dome) or dropped poses. Dead centres (crank in line with the rod) give bifurcation when unpowered. 15 passive coordinates. The orientation…","verdict":""},{"id":"human-closed-telescopes","name":"Human-closed 3-UPS 'artificial deltoid': straight telescopes from dome brackets to arm studs","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":15,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Human-closed 3-UPS 'artificial deltoid'","description":"Three straight telescopic struts run like the three heads of the deltoid, from small brackets on the clavicle end, acromion and scapular spine to studs on the arm around the deltoid insertion. They lengthen and shorten as the muscle heads do. REST: the middle strut runs down the lateral deltoid, the anterior strut over the front of the cap, and the posterior strut over the back. Brackets lift each end 6-10 mm so the chord clears the muscle. OVERHEAD: the middle strut shortens (acromion-top to lateral arm at 160 mm: 142-222 mm on the bench poses, ratio 1.56) and the arm studs rise beside the acromion. ACROSS-BODY: the posterior strut lengthens, the anterior one shortens. BEHIND-BACK: the reverse. Metal is three O8-O10 tubes, three O14 U-joints and three O14 studs.","topology":"S -> three dome brackets (anterior: lateral clavicle; middle: acromion; posterior: scapular spine) -> U-joint (passive, 2) -> telescopic strut, O10 / O8 Ti tubes on a linear rolling or iglidur bushing (ACTIVE prismatic, its length sensed) -> ball stud (passive, 3) on the arm saddle (deltoid-insertion region, BOTTOM bar, posterolateral arm).\n\nNo exo joint at GH: the user's glenohumeral joint closes the loop. No rotary hub at all. 9 joints: 3 active (prismatic), 6 passive (15 passive coordinates).","main_risk":"Straight struts over a convex, bulging cap: the chord from a dome bracket to an arm stud dips into the deltoid (worst when the deltoid bunches overhead and with the biceps bulge). The only levers are bracket and stud lift and placement, which trade directly against in-band. Stroke: several useful anchor pairs need length ratios > 1.8 (e.g. clavicle to lateral arm at 110 mm: 66-169 mm, ratio 2.56). Those need two-stage telescopes (thicker) or drop poses; good pairs stay at 1.5-1.6. Telescopes under side load bind on plain bushings, so they need rolling bushings. Cables pull only, so MVP2 needs two cables or a spring per strut. 15 passive coordinates. The orientation readout depends on GH…","verdict":""},{"id":"j2-arc-sector-3r","name":"Fold-free spherical 3R whose J2 is a link-1 arc-rail sector with a link-2 roller carriage (no J2 hub; virtual J2 axis)","family":"spherical 3R (virtual-axis J2)","lens":"round3:J2 as a short curved rail with a real carriage, on a fold-free axis layout","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:23:25Z","reach":0.8375,"follow":0.8833,"inband14":0.3348,"inband10":0.2309,"contact":0.0209,"gap":71.53,"v_gap":66.87,"v_reach":0.8812,"v_follow":0.8833,"v_inband14":0.3377,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":298,"doc":"docs/concepts/j2-arc-sector-3r.md","short":"Fold-free spherical 3R whose J2 is a link-1 arc-rail sector with a link-2 roller carriage","description":"Hub 1 on the acromion top (bvec(96, 65)); fold-free arcs a1 60 / a2 80 (kit: closure 0.90, reach 0.82 in the hub version vs 0.706 for the leader). No metal on the J2 axis point (in band 3 %): J2 is a flat Ti arc rail, pitch radius 30 mm about the J2 axis at ~52 mm along it, a 125 deg sector on link 1's end on the J1 side; a 4-V-roller carriage fixed to link 2 at chi +90 deg rides it (psi -46..+58 deg p2-p98). Link 2 runs from the carriage to the O20 J3 hub on the lateral deltoid, then the K7 neck.","topology":"scapula handler -> base -> J1 ACTIVE R (through GH, O20) -> link 1 with arc-rail sector -> J2 ACTIVE R about a virtual axis through GH (circular rail + roller carriage) -> link 2 -> J3 ACTIVE R (through GH, O20) -> K7 neck -> BOTTOM -> cuff. The rollers are the active joint's bearing; 0 passive DOF.","main_risk":"(1) Link 2 is the unsolved limiter: 0.10-0.71 in band along its great circle (frame set by closure, as the champion's link 2 at ceiling 0.04). (2) Rail ends: travel plus the carriage half-length reaches the in-band sector's edge; end stops cost ~2-4 % of poses. (3) A cantilevered custom V-arc with eccentric preload: play, stiffness and arc sensing less mature than a hub encoder. (4) Close to rail-elbow (dead, refuted by its own 3R control): the hub control at identical axes is mandatory.","verdict":"dead: the rail loses 13.4 gap points to its own hub control at identical axes (71.12-71.53 vs 57.75); link 2 never above 0.6 in band (max 0.26); an in-window rail is in band (0.998) but caps reach at 0.46"},{"id":"lamellae-pauldron","name":"Revolved-lamellae pauldron (every joint a large thin ring, links nest as revolved shells)","family":"","lens":"weird-compliant","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Revolved-lamellae pauldron","description":"This takes the user's own hint: 'if they could fully overlap each other, you'd get rid of a lot of collision issues ... within 14mm'. Two members that turn relative to each other about an axis can overlap with a constant gap only if their facing surfaces are surfaces of revolution about that axis. So each joint becomes a large open ring, and the two links overlap around it as revolved dishes (2 mm + 0.5 mm gap + 2 mm). The dish profile follows the skin's radial profile around that axis, and the lames are free-form away from the rings (EF). Overhead-first: the folded overhead chain is a stack of up to three lames (~7.5 mm + liner) instead of hubs standing 12.25 mm proud. J1's ring sits on the dome (skin 39-68 mm from GH, < ~11 mm change). REST: the lames lie like a pauldron over the top and side of the shoulder, with the J3 ring on the stable lateral arm strip. OVERHEAD: the lames slide…","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R as a large thin-section ring (pitch radius 20-40 mm), axis u1 through GH -> link 1 = a dished Ti lame (1.5-2 mm). Wherever it overlaps a neighbour around a ring, it is a surface of revolution about that ring's axis -> J2: ACTIVE R ring about u2 (pitch radius 20-40) -> link 2 lame -> J3: ACTIVE R ring about the arm-fixed axis j3 (on the lateral arm strip) -> cuff. Spherical 3R kinematics with general a12 and a23. Every joint is an open ring, and every link is a shell segment that nests over its neighbour like the lames of a pauldron.","main_risk":"The skin under a revolved overlap zone is not a surface of revolution about the joint axis. A ring at a 25 deg cap angle around p* sees skin at 39-68 mm around its circumference (up to 116 mm in the neck sector), so the dish must clear the highest point on its circle and stands off elsewhere. Pick each axis by its revolvedness (a sweep objective). Shells cover more skin and weigh more (~25-40 g per ring plus 1.5-2 mm lames), still well under 500 g. The kinematics are a 3R's, so the J2 knee excursion overhead remains. Large-ring housings are custom.","verdict":""},{"id":"lazy-tongs-j2","name":"Spherical lazy-tongs J2 (a scissor arc instead of a folding knee)","family":"","lens":"weird-compliant","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":21,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Spherical lazy-tongs J2","description":"The excess problem: over the everyday workspace, the J1-J3 angle of any spherical 3R swings ~90-120 deg. Measured: >= ~89 deg for any dome J1 axis with any arm-fixed J3 axis, and 32-151 deg for the posterior site of back-anchored-ring-3r. A one-hub knee with both links >= ~60 deg then throws J2 far off the J1-J3 line in the short configuration: with the baseline's 77/81 deg links spanning ~69 deg, J2 sits ~78 deg sideways. That is why the baseline's J2 hub lands on the back over the medial scapula overhead. The lazy-tongs never leaves the J1-J3 great circle; the excess becomes band width, not sideways travel. With n = 6, link length lambda = 24 deg and r 65 mm, the band is ~8 mm wide when long and ~26 mm when short. REST: J1 is on the scapula (dome or posterior site), J3 on the arm, and the tongs lies on the direct arc between them over the deltoid. OVERHEAD: the tongs contracts in…","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R (O20 hub or ring), axis u1 through GH -> link 1 = a short end block (~12 deg of arc) -> J2: ACTIVE R about the remote axis u2 = unit(u1 x w), realised as a 1-DOF spherical scissor (lazy-tongs) of n = 4-8 cells. The scissor lies on the minor great-circle arc G from the J1 end block to the J3 end block. Every scissor pivot axis is radial through GH (MR63/MR85 miniature bearing pairs, ~5 mm stack). Each end block holds one fixed pivot and one pivot in a short passive arc slot, so the tongs leaves the two blocks only rotation about u2. J2 is measured at the first pivot (cell angle phi) -> link 2 = end block -> J3: ACTIVE R (O20 hub or ring), arm-fixed axis j3 through GH -> cuff. a12 = a23 = 90 deg, so the J1 and J3 piercing points both lie on G. There is no metal at the piercing point of u2.","main_risk":"Mechanical complexity: ~20 pivots (miniature bearing pairs) whose backlash and friction add up. Toggle: as the cells straighten in the long configuration the force ratio blows up, so keep the cell angle >= 15 deg. The middle cells slide along the arc over different skin as the tongs extends, so their undersides must clear the highest skin on their whole travel. The crossing link layers create shear and pinch points (left to the cover). The band is ~26 mm wide when short. Stiffness across the sphere depends on the pin stacks. MVP2 loads (~10 N.m) through slender scissor links need FEA.","verdict":""},{"id":"lazy-tongs-polar","name":"Spherical lazy-tongs polar positioner (multi-rhombus scissor from a scapula pole hub to a deltoid hub)","family":"","lens":"literature","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":9,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Spherical lazy-tongs polar positioner","description":"A thin titanium lazy-tongs band lies on the shoulder: two layers of 7 x 2.5 mm bars on O8 rolling pivots, about 6.5 mm in total. It runs between a base hub over the medial scapular spine / supraspinatus and an arm hub on the posterior deltoid, always along the shortest path between them. Its length follows the hub distance and its width shrinks as it stretches. Rest: the hubs are about 58 deg apart and the scissor fans across the back of the shoulder (about +/-26 deg, +/-33 mm wide). Overhead: the posterior deltoid rises and comes forward, Theta grows toward about 150 deg, and the band stretches into a narrow (+/-12 deg) strip over the top of the shoulder from the spine to the raised deltoid. Across-body: the deltoid swings forward and lateral, and the band stretches over the posterior-lateral cap. Behind-back: the posterior deltoid comes back toward the spine, the band collapses into…","topology":"Scapula base -> base hub at pole p1 (axis radial through GH) carrying TWO coaxial R joints, both ACTIVE: theta_a (root a-bar vs base) and theta_b (root b-bar vs base); J1 = (theta_a+theta_b)/2 is the scissor's azimuth about p1, J2 = theta_b-theta_a its opening -> spherical lazy-tongs of n = 3 rhombi along the great circle p1 -> e: 3n = 9 passive pivots, all with radial axes through GH (n-1 centre crossings C_1..C_(n-1), 2n side pivots S_k+/-, 1 pivot between the two end half-bars at C_n) -> arm hub at e (arm-fixed direction on the posterior deltoid): J3 R ACTIVE (arm member vs the end a-half-bar, radial axis) -> rigid arm member on the user's upper-arm cuff through the user's passive self-aligning slides (outside the count, GH translation).","main_risk":"Kinematic margin. A frames-only check over the 470 bench poses found no (base pole, arm hub) pair better than Theta in 25-153 deg, with both ends near the polar singularity e = +/-p1 where J1 is undefined. The scissor must therefore stretch about 6:1 (n >= 3) and is wide (+/-27 deg, about 35 mm) when collapsed; the bench workspace includes axial -180..135 deg. The 3n+3 = 12 pivots accumulate backlash, and slender bars may not carry MVP2's ~10 N.m (the force ratio rises near collapse). Prior evidence is mixed: this repo's re-optimised 2-rhombus scissor reached ~50 % coverage (arm-rom stage, different body and metric) against 65-76 % for a free 4R. It has never been measured for in-band share.","verdict":""},{"id":"lone-helix-humeral","name":"Lines-of-non-extension helix: 2R pointing over the top + a helical, skin-profiled humeral-rotation rail","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Lines-of-non-extension helix","description":"Over the shoulder: a compact 2R pointing chain, with J1 at the anterior-superior acromion / clavicle-tip region (the singularity home) and J2 on the cap's equator. Down the arm: link 2 lies along the lateral arm. At its end, a small two-slide carriage rides an arm-fixed helical rail, a 10 mm band that winds ~120-180 deg around the upper arm. The helix follows a line of non-extension of the skin (as in the MIT BioSuit), and its radius follows the non-round arm. Rest: the helix runs from the posterolateral arm high up to the anterolateral arm lower down (or the reverse). Overhead, across-body and behind-back: link 2 points with the arm. Internal / external rotation runs the carriage along the helix, moving it up or down the arm by the pitch, so that at each station it stays in that station's body-free window.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1 = hub (axis a1 at the 2R singularity home) [ACTIVE] -> link 1 -> J2 = hub (axis a2, at a12 from a1 and perpendicular to the humerus axis) [ACTIVE] -> link 2 (the pointing link: it holds the humerus axis direction and runs down the lateral arm) -> carriage stage on link 2: an axial slide along the humerus axis [PASSIVE] + a radial slide [PASSIVE] -> J3 = carriage on an arm-fixed HELICAL rail around the upper arm, whose station and radius change with its angle (humeral rotation) [ACTIVE] -> arm.","main_risk":"Link 2 still rides over arm skin that rotates under it (a pointing-only link over the arm's non-round section), so its band may be poor. The body-free windows at successive stations may not chain into a helix, because the trunk sits medial at every station. The two-slide carriage stage is bulky (~25 x 20 x 9 mm). The J1 home (anterior-superior, scapula ceiling ~30 %) is not on good ground, and J2 is still a moving hub over the cap equator.","verdict":""},{"id":"magrace-arc-chain","name":"Hubless chain on magnet-preloaded open ball races","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Hubless chain on magnet-preloaded open ball races","description":"Flat: every joint is a 7 mm stack of curved plates (race 2.5 + balls 2 + carriage 2.5) on a small circle around its axis; there are no hubs and no caps. Rest: J1's race arcs over the posterior-superior scapula (base-fixed, low-motion skin) around an axis that may point into the back or neck. Link 1 runs from its carriage over the top of the shoulder to J2's race. J3's race wraps part-way round the upper arm at deltoid level if W is the humerus axis (the humeral ring), or circles a spot on the deltoid otherwise. Overhead: the carriages sit at the ends of their races, which stay put on their own members, while the axis pierce points (acromion tip, humeral head) carry no metal. Across-body and behind-back: J3's arm-fixed race turns with the arm while the link-2 carriage stays with link 2, and an open race can stop short of the trunk-contact side.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1, ACTIVE arc joint: a Ti race plate (12 mm wide, 2.5 mm thick, 90 deg gothic groove, 0.5 mm 17-4PH strip in its floor) lies on the cone of angular radius gamma1 about axis b, spanning J1's travel, on the proximal or distal member. The other member's 25 mm carriage rides it on 8 caged O2 Si3N4 balls, held down by 4 O3 x 1.5 N52 magnets; the race has no lips -> link 1 (strap) -> J2, ACTIVE arc joint: a race and carriage on the cone of radius gamma2 about p2 -> link 2 -> J3, ACTIVE arc joint: a race and carriage on the cone of radius gamma3 about W, with the arm-end member on BOTTOM. W may be the humerus axis, in which case the race is the humeral ring. No hub sits at any axis pierce point, and axes may point where no hub could sit (into the back or neck, down the arm). Gamma can exceed 90 deg, which puts the race around the other end of the axis line.","main_risk":"Each race spans its joint's whole travel. J1's travel is ~190-220 deg over the workspace: at gamma 25 deg and R 70 mm that is ~120 mm of plate per joint, more metal sweeping over moving skin than a hub, especially J2's race on link 1. The magnetic preload (~12 N) holds MVP1 loads only; MVP2 needs lips or a wire race (+2-3 mm). Open races collect debris (the shoulder-pad cover helps). It is a custom bearing, not a catalogue part, so the user should agree to it as MVP1's 'basic rolling bearing'.","verdict":""},{"id":"meridian-goniometer","name":"Goniometer pointer: J2 is an arc rail running beside the arm's elevation path, no J2 hub","family":"spherical 3R with a virtual-axis arc-rail J2","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T23:42:11Z","reach":0.775,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.1154,"inband10":0.0773,"contact":0.0821,"gap":102.5,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":267,"doc":"docs/concepts/meridian-goniometer.md","short":"Goniometer pointer","description":"There is no J2 hub. The rail runs alongside the arm's elevation path, 35-55 deg to the front or back of the plane holding the arm and the J1 pole, like a goniometer cradle; the carriage stays level with the arm root and moves with it. At rest the rail runs from the front of the shoulder down beside the arm root. Overhead the carriage sits at the rail's upper end near the crown edge. Across-body and behind-back, J1 turns the whole rail about p*.","topology":"S -> J1 R active: donut at p* (as in pointer-cslide) -> link 1 = arc rail (wire-race or crossed-roller curved slide, 6 mm band), lying on a small circle about a2 at cone alpha_r = 35-55 deg on one side of the plane through a1 and the arm, fixed to the J1 rotor by a short root -> J2 R active with a VIRTUAL axis a2 = normalize(a1 x t): carriage (40-50 mm shoe) on the rail -> link 2 = carriage + short bridge to the arm root -> J3 R active: humeral C-slide as in pointer-cslide -> cuff.","main_risk":"The same 32 deg pole problem as pointer-cslide. A long rail (the q2 range, ~90 deg, plus the shoe) fixed to J1's rotor sweeps around p* with q1 (~190 deg) and must clear the body over that whole sweep. Rail plus carriage stack is ~10-12 mm. The rail is a curved cantilever.","verdict":"screened, NOT converged (gate fails on the top-5 spread 5.8 points): best honest gap 102.5 (score 62.7) on Bench(60,160) fast - far behind the spherical 3R champion (68.93) and the verified leaders (rail-elbow 64.7, seed-offset-3r-slides 66.47 on Bench(110,360)). The spec (pointer + C-slide) scores 112.24 (reach 0.46). The arc rail is 0-6 % in band in every honest configuration: replacing a J2 hub by a virtual-axis rail makes J2 worse, not better"},{"id":"offset-arc-selfalign","name":"Skin-shaped offset arc + self-aligning cuff (non-spherical trombone)","family":"arc slide / trombone","lens":"ideation A","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:47:46Z","reach":0.6062,"follow":0.7333,"inband14":0.296,"inband10":0.1585,"contact":0.2167,"gap":133.12,"v_gap":133.12,"v_reach":0.6062,"v_follow":0.7333,"v_inband14":0.296,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":47,"doc":"docs/concepts/offset-arc-selfalign.md","short":"Skin-shaped offset arc + self-aligning cuff","description":"The trombone layout with the curvature freed. The band is a circle whose centre is not GH, so it can drop from ~48 mm over the top of the shoulder to ~60 mm over the deltoid instead of riding a single GH sphere. The swivel and hub axes can also sit where the skin wants them. The cost is a position error of up to ~15-20 mm, taken by a small slide stage between the J3 hub and BOTTOM on the cuff. REST / OVERHEAD / ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: metal positions as in trombone-arc (top swivel, band over the acromion to the lateral deltoid, tail over the trapezius overhead, over the scapula top across-body, over the clavicle region behind the back). The difference: the band follows a skin-shaped circle, and the cuff stage shifts up to 20 mm along and 15 mm around the arm.","topology":"S -> J1: swivel about a line parallel to b, offset delta1 (<= 12 mm) from GH -> L1 guide -> J2: arc fixed to L2. The arc is a circle in 3-D whose axis line is parallel to p2 and passes delta2 (<= 15 mm) from GH; its radius is chosen to follow the skin rather than the GH sphere. It slides through the guide. -> L2 -> J3: O20 v5 hub about a line parallel to W, offset delta3 (<= 12 mm) -> the arm-side plate sits on a passive 3-slide self-aligning cuff stage (+-20 mm along the humerus, +-15 mm around the arm, +-4 mm radial, on rolling miniature guides) -> H (cuff). Active: J1, J2, J3. Passive: 3 cuff slides (outside the count, set by closure).","main_risk":"Slide travel: P0's self-aligning 2R slider needed 74 mm over the workspace. The cuff stage adds ~8 mm of height and some mass on the arm, and must stay torque-free (R17: <= 5 N at the cuff), which calls for miniature rolling guides. The radial error must stay <= 4 mm or the liner gets squeezed. On the bench it looks like the trombone with extra metal. There are more parameters to fit.","verdict":"dead as specified: gap 133.1 vs 68.9 for the spherical 3R; not worth a GPU sweep. The offsets + self-aligning stage gain 11 points over its own spherical version with slides <= 4 mm, but the rigid circular band's tail (past the guide) cannot clear the neck/trapezius overhead at any radius, so the band sits 114 mm from its axis (> 31 mm off the skin). Successor: stow the tail (coil-stowed arc)."},{"id":"offset-axis-skin-hubs","name":"Off-sphere skin hubs + passive cuff stage: axis directions spherical, axis positions free","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"modelling","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":541,"doc":"","short":"Off-sphere skin hubs + passive cuff stage","description":"Hubs are no longer forced onto rays through GH at fixed radii. J1 lies flat on the crown, J2 on the top of the posterior or anterior deltoid wherever the skin is, J3 on the lateral arm; each hub's lower plate rests on its own skin patch at +2 mm. The end of link 2 rides a short rail along the lateral arm under BOTTOM: overhead it slides toward the shoulder, across-body away from it, and the transverse guides shift a few mm.","topology":"S -> J1 R active: O20 v5 hub or KAA15XL0 donut lying flat ON the crown skin, axis direction a1, but its axis line does not pass through GH -> link 1 -> J2 R active: hub on skin, offset axis line -> link 2 -> J3 R active: hub on the arm, offset line -> passive self-aligning cuff stage under the saddle (passive, outside the count): one along-arm miniature linear guide (THK RSR5 / RSR7: 5 / 7 mm rail, block height 6 / 8 mm, travel +-40 mm) + two short transverse guides (THK RSR3: 3 mm rail, 4 mm tall, +-8 mm), or a 2-axis flexure parallelogram (+-5 mm) -> user's cuff.","main_risk":"Travel. An offset e turned by q moves the cuff end ~2e sin(q/2), so with q up to ~150 deg the transverse offsets must stay under ~4-5 mm (+-8 mm guides), or the motion must be steered along the arm; the hubs gain far less freedom than hoped. The passive stage adds ~6-8 mm of stack under the saddle, plus friction and compliance at the cuff (R17 limits: 5 N and 5 deg at the cuff). A slide that moves the chain end against the arm skin is the R10 problem in reverse.","verdict":""},{"id":"offset-flex-aligner","name":"Non-concurrent 3R on a compliant translation cuff stage","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Non-concurrent 3R on a compliant translation cuff…","description":"Three hubs, each seated flat on its own skin, perpendicular to the local skin normal at a low-motion spot. J1 is on the scapula's acromion/spine. J3 is on the deltoid near W ~ (112, -15) deg, where the proximity agent found a 94 % flat-disc ceiling. The two straps joining them are not constrained to a sphere about GH. Under BOTTOM, a 60 x 40 mm Ti leaf-spring stage lets the arm-end block shift relative to the cuff. Rest: the stage is centred (the chain is assembled at rest). Overhead and across-body: the stage deflects by the chain's own translation error, up to its +-12 / 8 / 4 mm limits, and on a real body also by the GH translation the bench does not model. Behind-back: the same.","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: R hub, ACTIVE; its axis line passes through a point c1 (not GH), in a direction near the local scapula skin normal -> link 1 -> J2: R hub, ACTIVE, axis line through c2 -> link 2 -> J3: R hub, ACTIVE, on a 'floating arm-end' block, with its axis near the local arm skin normal at a low-motion spot -> floating block -> a PASSIVE compliant 3-translation stage: along-arm P (+-12 mm) and around-arm P (+-8 mm) as two Ti parallel-leaf flexures (0.5 x 10 x 60 mm leaves), plus radial P (+-4 mm) as a thin leaf pair -> BOTTOM / the upper-arm cuff (the user's).","main_risk":"The stage travel is finite. The design's own offsets (|e| up to ~1.8x the offset) plus real GH translation must fit within +-12 / 8 / 4 mm, which limits axis offsets to ~5 mm and the bench gain to the few points that flat hubs buy. A 3-axis stage adds metal and mass at the cuff. On the bench (no GH translation) it may well look worse than the baseline; its real case is R10/R14.","verdict":""},{"id":"offset-pointer-slip-sleeve","name":"Offset-centre 2R pointer with a sliding twist sleeve at the cuff","family":"spherical 2R pointer + cuff twist","lens":"literature","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:01:17Z","reach":0.9,"follow":0.95,"inband14":0.3974,"inband10":0.2871,"contact":0.0864,"gap":58.9,"v_gap":70.06,"v_reach":0.8125,"v_follow":0.95,"v_inband14":0.3733,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":366,"doc":"docs/concepts/offset-pointer-slip-sleeve.md","short":"Offset-centre 2R pointer with a sliding twist…","description":"A J1 hub on the anterior-superior acromion; link 1 runs over the top of the shoulder to the J2 hub on the posterior-lateral cap; one bar runs from J2 down the lateral arm and ends in a short rod at about 200 mm down the humerus. There a sleeve on the user's cuff (O12 linear-rotary bushing on a small 2-axis gimbal) both spins about and slides along the rod. No hub on the deltoid, no ring, no arm-end neck. Overhead: the bar lies along the lateral (now medial-facing) side of the raised arm. Across-body: along its outer side. Behind-back: along the posterior-lateral side. The sleeve slides 30-50 mm along the rod and the cuff gimbal tilts up to about 25-34 deg (frames-only check).","topology":"Scapula base -> J1 R hub (ACTIVE, axis a1 through a centre O placed 0-30 mm from GH) -> link 1 -> J2 R hub (ACTIVE, axis through O, perpendicular to a1) -> bar (link 2) ending in a straight O6 rod on the line O -> C -> linear-rotary sleeve on the rod: J3 R (ACTIVE, spin about the rod) + slide P (passive) -> Hooke U at the cuff (2 passive R, axes perpendicular to the rod) -> the user's upper-arm cuff.","main_risk":"With O off GH, the links rotate about O while the shoulder skin rotates about GH, so they move against the skin by about |o| x angle. With O at GH this reduces to cuff-sleeve-3r. The measured J1-J3 angles mix in GH translation (an orientation error of about delta/lambda, ~3 deg per 10 mm) when used for MVP1 tracking. The cuff gimbal plus sleeve adds about 16-19 mm of bulk at the cuff, and the rod needs 30-50 mm of slide when O is 20-40 mm from GH. The bar is a long cantilever (stiffness and mass).","verdict":"screened after polish (score 78.58, gap 58.90 vs bar 74.9 / 68.93), with a caveat: the shoulder half (hub 2, bar cap, bar arm run) still floats >= 32 mm off the skin (4-13 % in band); the cuff end and hub 1 carry the in-band number. Not converged by the gate (best at eval 193/252, last third) but on a flat ridge (+0.26 over the second half, top-5 within 0.12)"},{"id":"offset-skin-normal-3r","name":"Non-concurrent 3R on skin-normal hubs with a passive self-aligning cuff (2 slides)","family":"offset axes","lens":"weird-compliant","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T23:32:45Z","reach":0.7063,"follow":0.9667,"inband14":0.7792,"inband10":0.3892,"contact":0.0116,"gap":32.61,"v_gap":42.59,"v_reach":0.6111,"v_follow":0.9636,"v_inband14":0.784,"v_contact":0.021,"n_configs":9869,"doc":"docs/concepts/offset-skin-normal-3r.md","short":"Non-concurrent 3R on skin-normal hubs with a passive self-aligning cuff","description":"Freed from concurrency, each hub can sit flat on stable skin with its axis along the local skin normal (minimal rim stand-off on sloping skin), and the links become flatter straps between hubs instead of arcs on a sphere around GH. The same slides absorb the real GH-vs-scapula translation (R10; the published bone-pin data in FINDINGS show +35 mm superior at 90 deg scaption), which no spherical family can. REST: J1 is on the dome, J2 on the back of the acromion, J3 on the lateral arm; the slides are centred. OVERHEAD: the arm turns ~110 deg relative to the scapula. The axis offsets give up to ~2 d sin(55 deg) = 1.6 d of mismatch (d = how far an axis misses GH), taken by the slides along and across the arm; the hubs stay on their stable skin. ACROSS THE BODY and BEHIND THE BACK: the metal sits as for a spherical chain with the same directions, with the cuff slides travelling.","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R (O20 hub on the dome; its axis is the local outward skin normal, which misses GH by 5-25 mm) -> link 1 (a flatter strap following the skin between hub centres) -> J2: ACTIVE R (hub on the posterior acromion / posterior deltoid, axis skin-normal in the home pose) -> link 2 -> J3: ACTIVE R (hub on the lateral arm strip, axis skin-normal) -> passive self-aligning cuff module: P_s, a rolling miniature rail along the humerus axis (+-20 mm), plus P_t, a rolling rail tangential across the lateral arm (+-15 mm) -> user's cuff. The axes do not meet.","main_risk":"Slide travel: axes that miss GH by 15-25 mm produce 25-40 mm of mismatch over the workspace. That means long rails and a cuff that shears the skin (R11), plus ~30-50 g of rails. The radial component must be designed out. On the bench (GH fixed in S) the slides only absorb the design's own error, so its R10 advantage is invisible here. The in-band gain from skin-normal hubs may be small, because near the shoulder the radial direction is already within ~10-30 deg of the skin normal. Modelling cost is high.","verdict":"screened, a leader on the screening bench: gap 32.61 (score 88.14) with the seed's VModel checks on; the spec's all-skin-normal 2-slide chain cannot close (17.7 % of poses at +-0.5 mm) - the exact form is J1/J2 on GH rays + a free, skin-normal-tilted J3 line + 2 slides in hub 3's plane (no radial slide); J3 tilt + 7 mm offset buys 14.1 gap at the best seats (46.71 -> 32.61); level with / better than the 3-slide skin-normal-hubs-selfaligning (35.86): the radial slide is not needed on this bench; hand to the verifier (Bench(110,360) exact) and then the R10 judge (2 vs 3 slides)"},{"id":"pauldron-lame-telescope","name":"Pauldron: lobster-tail lames as a remote-centre J2 that shares the elevation like the skin (scapulohumeral rhythm inside the mechanism)","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Pauldron","description":"A row of 3-5 overlapping curved Ti lames, like a plate-armour pauldron or a lobster tail. It runs from a hub on top of the shoulder (exo-acromion) down over the deltoid cap to a hub on the arm just under the acromion edge. Each lame is a spherical band 24-40 mm wide and ~3 mm thick that slides on the one below along circular tracks about a2, with rolling elements at the band edges. The coupling keeps the gaps equal, so each lame turns a fixed share of the elevation: lame k moves like the skin k/(N-1) of the way from the acromion to the arm. The lame radii rise by one lame thickness toward the arm, matching the skin, which rises from the acromion (41-50 mm) to the cap (55-60 mm). Rest: the stack is extended down over the lateral cap. Overhead: the arm hub rises toward the top hub and the lames telescope together under the acromion. Across-body: the stack swings forward about J1 and…","topology":"Serial spherical 3R with a12 = a23 = 90 deg and no J2 hub. Scapula base -> J1 = v5 O20 hub on the exo-scapula top (axis a1 through GH) [ACTIVE] -> lame 0 -> N-2 intermediate lames [PASSIVE: each held at a fixed share k/(N-1) of the J2 extension by an equal-share coupling, either pin-in-slot or a small lazy-tongs strip along the lame centreline] -> lame N-1. J2 = the stack's total extension, a remote-centre rotation about a2 (the normal of the great circle through J1 and J3) [ACTIVE] -> J3 = v5 O20 hub at the arm's low-motion spot (axis a3) [ACTIVE] -> arm piece -> BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Span ratio: J1-J3 spans 46-128 deg on the bench fit clips (2.8x) and 27-150 deg over the workspace for top / tubercle placements (my check). That needs N >= 4 lames with ~30 % overlap (max/min = 1 + (N-1)(1 - overlap) = 3.1 at N = 4), so the workspace extremes stay out of reach. The stack thickness (N x ~3 mm + rolling elements) lifts the outer lames. There are many track surfaces (friction, and pinch points between lames, which are the cover's job), and the coupling is fiddly. Thin lames must carry MVP2's 10 N.m through ~25 mm-wide bands. The chain is singular when theta13 -> 0 or 180 deg (kept within 25-155).","verdict":""},{"id":"pointer-cslide","name":"The user's split: 2R donut pointer + shoulder-side wire-race C-slide for humeral twist","family":"spherical 2R pointer + humeral-twist C-rail","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T22:36:31Z","reach":0.5,"follow":0.9333,"inband14":0.6739,"inband10":0.5209,"contact":0.0556,"gap":69.84,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":479,"doc":"docs/concepts/pointer-cslide.md","short":"The user's split","description":"J1 sits on the anterior acromion / lateral clavicle edge. J2 always rides beside the arm, 90 deg off its axis: anterolateral at rest, on the shoulder top overhead. The C-rail wraps the lateral half of the arm root, with the shoe on the arm's lateral side. Overhead the arm comes within 32 deg of J1 (its closest approach), so the deltoid passes right under J1. Across-body, the rail's open side faces the chest. Behind-back, J1 turns ~180 deg and J2 moves posterior.","topology":"S -> J1 R active: KAA10XL0 or KAA15XL0 donut (25.4x34.9x4.76 / 38.1x47.6x4.76) on an S-fixed bracket over the anterior acromion, axis a1 = p* ~ [0.36, 0.72, 0.60] (S-local; the best two-sided pointer pole over the 470 bench poses) -> link 1 (~90 deg arc) -> J2 R active: donut, a2 = +-normalize(a1 x t), i.e. 90 deg from both a1 and the arm -> link 2 = pointing link (follows the arm direction, not its twist), carrying an open C-rail (custom caged-ball wire-race band 26x6 mm; 2 + 6 + 2 = 10 mm stack) on a circle about the humerus axis at station s, radius rho -> J3 R active: arm-fixed 50-60 deg shoe on the rail (humeral axial rotation; 67-87 deg of travel per pipeline/girdle/ring_travel.json) -> shoe neck along the arm -> user's cuff / BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Measured today on the bench frames: the best two-sided pointer pole has only 32 deg of margin (p*). ring_travel's clip-optimal pole n = [-0.525, -0.253, 0.812] drops to 14.6 deg on the workspace poses, against 45-55 deg on the clips. p* lies where the overhead arm comes, so J1-deltoid contact overhead is likely. A rail at the deltoid cap needs r >= 82-91 mm to be body-free (pipeline/girdle/ring_roundness.json) against ~55-60 mm of skin: 20+ mm of stand-off unless it moves to s 120-160 (17-21 mm skin spread). The rail is a cantilever (~2 deg twist for a hollow 26x6 band at 25 N.m).","verdict":"screened, NOT converged (gate: +13.44 score over the 2nd half, +5.68 over the last third, top-5 within 1.06): best gap 69.84 (score 74.60) on Bench(60,160) fast with the skin-fitted saddle band - level with the spherical 3R champion (68.93 / 74.93) but behind the verified leaders (rail-elbow 64.7, seed-offset-3r-slides 66.47 on Bench(110,360)); the spec's cylindrical band scores 173-178 (band floats 30 mm). Reach 0.50 is the ceiling: the twist window holds 98 deg while the workspace needs ~150-180"},{"id":"posterior-max-margin-cardan","name":"Posterior max-margin Cardan","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"ideation A","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:59:42Z","reach":0.8938,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.2264,"inband10":0.1334,"contact":0.0469,"gap":72.67,"v_gap":80.76,"v_reach":0.8167,"v_follow":1.0,"v_inband14":0.2226,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":16,"doc":"docs/concepts/posterior-max-margin-cardan.md","short":"Posterior max-margin Cardan","description":"Everything sits behind the frontal plane:\n- J1 is low on the back of the scapula, 119-135 mm from GH.\n- Link 1 runs up the infraspinatus to J2 on the posterior acromion and scapular spine. Under J2 the scapula-frame skin moves a median of 10 mm (p90 38-49 mm), against 29 mm (p90 71 mm) for the baseline's J2.\n- Link 2 runs down the posterior deltoid to J3 on the posterior upper arm, 109-125 mm from GH.\n\nThe front and top of the shoulder are empty: overhead and across-body, nothing is in front of or above the acromion. Reaching behind the back, the arm swings toward the chain (the main risk).","topology":"- Scapula base (S), low on the infraspinatus.\n- J1 ACTIVE: O20 hub with a wedge or skin-normal seat, axis b = bvec(-28, -19) (posterior-inferior-medial).\n- Link 1: a 90 deg strap up the back of the scapula.\n- J2 ACTIVE: O20 hub at the common perpendicular, p2 = +(b x w)/|b x w|. On this branch it rides the posterior-superior shoulder: scapula-frame az 45-60, el 30-40, about 50 mm from GH.\n- Link 2: a 90 deg strap down the posterior deltoid.\n- J3 ACTIVE: O20 hub with a wedge seat, W = wdir(42, -64) on the posterior upper arm.\n- Arm-end + neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Hub obliquity:\n- At both end poles the radial rays meet the skin about 49 deg off the normal (back of the scapula, posterior arm), 110-135 mm from GH. J1 and J3 therefore need wedge or tilted seats that add stack.\n- If hubs are restricted to skin within 30 deg of the normal and moving at most 15-25 mm, the best margin drops to 15-17 deg (computed).\n\nOther risks:\n- Long links, about 150 mm each.\n- Posterior arm skin moves 26-58 mm in the humerus frame: bad ground for J3.\n- Behind-back reaches squeeze link 2 between the arm and the back.","verdict":"not worth its own GPU sweep: the Cardan itself scores 80.2 at best (k6; 81.87 after the hub-footprint fix, k15) and the global max-margin placement 103.7; the best number (72.67, honest audit) is the shorter-arc variant a1=a2=75 at the posterior seed, still worse than the champion 3R (68.93 baseline row, kit template 70.19, 65.05 on the kit's honest flat-hub geometry per FINDINGS). Hand to the spherical-3R GPU sweep only the observation that a posterior chain with ~75 deg arcs and ~10 deg margin follows every clip frame (0 flips) with reach 0.89"},{"id":"raglan-knee-3r","name":"Raglan-knee 3R (pin-knee leader with links bent along the lines of least skin motion)","family":"offset 3R, exact 2-slide subfamily, planar revolute closure","lens":"weird-tailoring (raglan routing on the leader)","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T02:10:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:46:21Z","reach":0.6875,"follow":0.9667,"inband14":0.9011,"inband10":0.5167,"contact":0.0175,"gap":23.0,"v_gap":31.52,"v_reach":0.6,"v_follow":0.9727,"v_inband14":0.9016,"v_contact":0.0152,"n_configs":2797,"doc":"docs/concepts/raglan-knee-3r.md","short":"Raglan-knee 3R","description":"The tailor's raglan seam applied to the best buildable design: measure, in each link's own moving frame, the sphere-traced need of every direction over every pose the chain takes (raglan map), and bend the link along the best line (one planform turn, constant 8 x 3 section, tangent-arc corner, no loft); then let CMA move the seats with the routes, and try the posterior J1 line (the far-end scan's fold-free lead) in this family.","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R (O20 hub on a GH ray) -> link 1 (8 x 3 Ti bent bar: two straight legs + ONE tangent arc in the planform, fixed in the link) -> J2: ACTIVE R (O20 hub on a GH ray) -> link 2 (same, its own turn) -> J3: ACTIVE R (offset skin-normal O20 hub on the deltoid) -> E strap -> pin a -> knee link -> pin b -> arm pad on BOTTOM's cuff (flat two-pin knee, 5 mm iglidur pin stack).","main_risk":"The leader's CMA already put both links on their raglan lines (the map's argmax is the great circle); the limiter is the J2 fold (reach), which no routing touches.","verdict":"VERIFIED v_gap 31.52 (score 88.54) on Bench(110,360) exact frozen vs champion 66.97 same bench and checks; routing is a small lever on the leader, the seat move found with it a bigger one: raglan map argmax = the great circle at the k4216 seats; routes only 24.79 fast (25.89 at fixed part sizes; 32.33 frozen Bench(110,360) exact, 33.41 fixed) vs the pin knee 26.34 / 33.84; CMA seats + routes (A2, 1009 tries, converged) 23.00 fast (score 91.64; 23.62 at fixed sizes; same seats with straight links 24.49: J3 3.3 deg more proximal brings hub 3 / knee / pad into band); posterior-J1 seats dead (122.45); A2 worn FROZEN on Bench(110,360) exact 31.52 (score 88.54; 32.11 at fixed sizes; in-band 0.90…"},{"id":"raglan-routed-3r","name":"Raglan-routed 3R on far-end hubs (posterior J1 line, links on the lines of least skin motion)","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"weird-tailoring","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:20:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:10:29Z","reach":0.8688,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.504,"inband10":0.2932,"contact":0.0,"gap":42.72,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":4405,"doc":"docs/concepts/raglan-routed-3r.md","short":"Raglan-routed 3R on far-end hubs","description":"A pattern maker's answer to the 3R's floating links: draft each link along the measured line where the skin keeps one distance from THAT link over every pose (raglan map: the K3 requirement on a (t, d) grid of the link's own moving frame), with one planform turn (constant section, tangent-arc corner, no loft). Plus a grading pass over the whole layout space (three-hub band scan) for layouts where all three hubs can be pinned in one 12 mm band. REST: hub 1 on top of the shoulder, hub 2 behind it, hub 3 on the lateral arm; link 1 bows out over the posterior deltoid instead of dropping straight down the back. OVERHEAD / ACROSS / BEHIND: as the plain 3R (same kinematics), the bowed link keeps its skin distance where the straight one crossed the acromion edge.","topology":"S -> J1: ACTIVE R (O20 hub, axis through GH) -> link 1 (8 x 3 strap with ONE planform turn fixed in the link: hub -> W -> hub, W = cos(d) slerp(h1, h2, t) + sin(d) n_12) -> J2: ACTIVE R (O20 hub, axis through GH) -> link 2 (same, its own turn) -> J3: ACTIVE R (O20 hub, axis through GH) -> arm-end plate + K7 neck rigid to BOTTOM's bar. Each hub may sit at either end of its axis line (e1, e2, e3).","main_risk":"Hub 2 floats (F 128-145 mm) in every plain-3R basin with good reach: link 2 and hub 2 stay out of band; the graded family that pins hub 2 (F 65) loses reach to the fold and needs a 100 deg link 2 down the deltoid.","verdict":"best plain spherical 3R (0 passive DOF of any kind): 42.72 on Bench(60,160) fast (CMA gate converged, 4396 tries) and 50.06 worn FROZEN on Bench(110,360) exact (vs 57.99 for the straight-link 3R at k2221's axes, same protocol): one 38 deg planform turn in link 1 keeps it over skin that rides with it (raglan map). Dilution-corrected (link 1 at its straight sample count) 43.97 fast: the gain is mostly real. Still behind the pin knee (33.84 frozen) and osn (42.59): hub 2 floats at 128 mm (0 % in band) in every plain-3R basin with good reach"},{"id":"rail-elbow","name":"Rail elbow: J2 is a curved rail on link 1, link 2 a carriage beside the arm-end hub","family":"spherical 3R (J2 as a rail)","lens":"ideation A","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:02:35Z","reach":0.9,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.3375,"inband10":0.209,"contact":0.0,"gap":56.25,"v_gap":64.7,"v_reach":0.8875,"v_follow":0.9667,"v_inband14":0.2655,"v_contact":0.0,"n_configs":2243,"doc":"docs/concepts/rail-elbow.md","short":"Rail elbow","description":"Rest:\n- The J1 hub sits on the lateral or posterior acromion. Link 1 is a short strap to a curved rail: 140-190 deg of a circle of radius about 45-55 mm about the J2 axis, section 8 x 5 mm.\n- The rail lies over the deltoid cap and posterior shoulder, about 60-65 mm from GH.\n- The carriage sits next to the J3 hub on the top-lateral deltoid. There is no metal at J2's pole.\n\nOverhead: the rail on link 1 swings with q1 over the shoulder top while the carriage runs along it; the arm-end hub stays on the deltoid cap. Across-body and behind-back: the carriage reaches the rail's ends (the extremes of the J2 angle).","topology":"- Scapula base (S).\n- J1 ACTIVE: O20 hub, axis b on scapula skin a hub can sit on (radial ray within 30 deg of the skin normal).\n- Link 1: a strap from J1 plus a circular rail arc fixed on link 1, concentric with the J2 axis p2 through GH. The rail lies on the circle at angular radius a2 + d_off about p2: the locus of the J3 pole relative to link 1, offset sideways by d_off so it runs beside the J3 hub. There is no hub at p2's pole.\n- J2 ACTIVE: a rolling carriage on the rail; the rotation about p2 is the joint value.\n- Link 2 = the carriage + a 15 mm bridge to J3's upper plate.\n- J3 ACTIVE: O20 hub at W, fixed to the arm on a deltoid-cap spot a hub can sit on (delta 75-115, e.g. the low-motion spot (112, -15)).\n- Arm-end plate + neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"- J2 travel: the J2 angle spans 94 deg on the clips but 159 deg over the functional workspace for the r18 chain (17-176 deg). For the layouts with the best singularity margin it spans 119-133 deg (computed).\n- The rail is therefore a 140-190 deg arc plus carriage on link 1: long intermediate metal that swings with q1 and must clear the arm and the acromion in every pose.\n- At constant radius it stands off where the skin under it is not the deltoid cap.\n- Its free ends are cantilevers (stiffness).","verdict":"REFUTED by the in-model control: the plain spherical 3R (flat O20 hubs, _template.py, q 1.0) at the rail-elbow's own axes scores 51.97 at k1530's axes (vs rail-elbow 57.19), 55.40 at k1841's (vs 59.34), 59.46 at k172's (vs 63.72): the rail costs 4-5 gap points; the gain came from the axes the CMA found. Converged best k2221 56.25 (score 79.55, Bench(60,160) fast; 3R at its axes 51.33); k1530 57.19 -> Bench(110,360) exact frozen 64.52 needs the 2:1 cable-loop telescope (MVP1-illegal); the MVP1-legal rigid rail (CMA R, not converged) reaches 59.34 by shrinking the rail to a 21 mm ring around p2 (-> the champion's J2 hub). Next: CMA + L1 CAD of the plain 3R at the new axes (51.97, score 81.1…"},{"id":"risley-turret","name":"Risley turret: J1 and J2 as two overlapping rings on top of the scapula","family":"spherical 3R (ring J1)","lens":"ideation A","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:34:29Z","reach":0.7312,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.5699,"inband10":0.3973,"contact":0.0995,"gap":63.17,"v_gap":75.42,"v_reach":0.6528,"v_follow":0.9182,"v_inband14":0.5243,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":66,"doc":"docs/concepts/risley-turret.md","short":"Risley turret","description":"Everything that rotates against moving skin moves onto the stable top of the scapula. Two stacked, partly overlapping flat rings sit on the supraspinatus/acromion cap, their centres ~38 mm apart at R 55. One strap runs from the upper ring down over the lateral shoulder to the deltoid hub. REST: both rings on the top, J2 on the side of the top away from the arm; the strap runs over the acromion to the lateral deltoid. OVERHEAD (sep ~40 deg): the strap path shortens and lies over the top. J2 stays >= 90 deg from the arm (measured on every clip frame for A1 40 / A2 75). ACROSS-BODY: J1 turns the ring pair forward and the strap runs over the front of the shoulder. BEHIND-BACK: the strap runs over the posterior deltoid. S-frame skin map: the top cap (scapula-frame latitude >= 45-60 deg) has skin radius 37-54 mm with only 1-10 mm of spread between poses.","topology":"S -> J1: ring (annulus, O30-48) about b on the S-top -> L1: small plate joining J1's upper race and J2's lower race -> J2: ring about p2. With A1 only 30-45 deg, its pierce point also stays on the S-top cap. -> L2: J2's upper race + one strap down to W -> J3: O20 v5 hub (or ring) about W on the lateral deltoid -> H. Active: J1, J2, J3. Passive: none.","main_risk":"Closure band: a 3R only reaches sep = angle(b, W) in [A2-A1, A2+A1]. Measured with a 5 deg margin, A1 40 / A2 75 closes 91 % of clip frames and 59 % of the workspace; with W0 = wdir(80,20), 85 % and 70 %. The champion closes 100 % and 96 %, so R3/R16 may fall. Where L1 bridges the two overlapping rings, the stack reaches up to ~16 mm. Link 2 still spans the whole moving gap from the top to W.","verdict":"worth a GPU sweep as a SUBTYPE of the spherical 3R: a flat J1 ring bearing on the scapula top cap (93 % in band) + O20 hubs at J2/J3, honest gap 63.17 vs the champion's 68.93 (70.27 on the same kit checks); the spec's two-ring turret itself is dead (best 75.79, ring 2 floats 80-150 mm)"},{"id":"rolling-cone-3r","name":"Hubless spherical rolling-contact joints: conical Rolamite / CORE pairs with apex at GH (bold, weird)","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Hubless spherical rolling-contact joints","description":"There are no hubs and no pins. Each joint is two thin bevel-wheel-like rims standing on edge, ~8 mm tall, rolling on each other like toothless bevel gears, with the straps wrapped around them. At rest J1's pair stands on the crown, J2's over the deltoid top and J3's on the lateral arm. As the arm moves the rims roll, so each joint's contact point walks along its rims: the joint itself travels over the skin by up to (rim radius x phi), which a hub cannot do.","topology":"S -> J1 active: a conical rolling-contact pair. Two Ti cone-sector rims (apex at GH, half-angles ga and gb, 2.5 mm wall, radial extent from skin+2 to skin+10 mm) roll on each other along a radial contact line, held by 2-3 crossed flexure straps (0.1 mm spring steel or Ti foil, wound in opposite senses: the Jacob's-ladder / Rolamite principle) -> link 1 -> J2 active: second cone pair -> link 2 -> J3 active: third cone pair on the arm saddle. Every contact line is a ray from GH, so each pair is a 1-DOF spherical motion with a rolling instantaneous axis. No pins, no hubs.","main_risk":"Off-plane loads: strap-held rolling joints resist only in their rolling plane, so the cones need side flanges or guides (extra width). Each joint's range is limited by its rim arc (+-90-120 deg needs long rims). The rims stand radially, edge-on to the skin, so their footprint is narrow but the links must sit on top. A precise conical rolling surface on Ti is unusual to make (DFM), and strap fatigue over all-day wear is unproven. Modelling is nonlinear: no closed-form IK.","verdict":""},{"id":"rolling-cone-elbow","name":"Rolling-cone elbow: J2 is a pair of strap-bound cones rolling about GH, with no hub","family":"","lens":"ideation A","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Rolling-cone elbow","description":"Rest: the J1 hub sits on the acromion. Link 1 runs to a standing curved rim (an 8 mm tall, 2 mm thick conical band, radially oriented, 60-90 mm long) over the posterior-lateral shoulder. Link 2's rim rolls on it and link 2 continues to the J3 hub on the deltoid cap. The chain's 'elbow' is two thin rims touching along a radial line: no hub, no cap.\n\nOverhead and across-body: the planet rim rolls along the sun rim. Each rim needs only about half the arc that a rail of the same J2 travel would, because both surfaces move.","topology":"- Scapula base (S).\n- J1 ACTIVE: O20 hub, axis b.\n- Link 1 ends in a 'sun' conical rim: axis s fixed in link 1, half-angle alpha1, apex at GH.\n- J2 ACTIVE: no-slip rolling contact with link 2's 'planet' conical rim (axis c, half-angle alpha2, external contact angle(s, c) = alpha1 + alpha2). Two or three crossed 0.1 mm steel straps hold the pair together, Jacob's-ladder style. It is a 1-DOF spherical joint whose instantaneous axis is the contact generator through GH.\n- Link 2.\n- J3 ACTIVE: O20 hub at W (deltoid cap, delta 75-115).\n- Arm-end plate + neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"- Novel and fiddly. The cones must stay in contact under every load direction: straps take tension one way and contact the other, so it needs crossed straps on both sides plus preload.\n- The rims stand 8 mm radially and still need skin clearance.\n- Slip under load would corrupt the angle reading.\n- Fatigue of 0.1 mm straps over 10^6 cycles.\n- The singularity map has to be built from scratch.\n- Sensing psi needs an encoder on the rolling pair.","verdict":""},{"id":"rolling-elbow-fivebar","name":"Hubless five-bar: Jacob's-ladder rolling-contact elbows where the loop makes them force-only","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Hubless five-bar","description":"The same closed A-frame as fivebar-roll, but each elbow is two flat link ends lying side by side on the skin and rolling on each other's curved edges, held by thin crossed straps like a Jacob's-ladder toy. There is no hub, cap or stack at the elbows. REST: base hubs on the dome; each crank ends in a rounded edge (O17-25 at r 60) touching the coupler's rounded edge at the rim, couplers converging on the arm hub. OVERHEAD: as the A-frame folds, the contact point rolls along both edges, so each elbow's pivot itself moves along the rim. ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: as fivebar-roll, with the rolling contacts on the front / back rim. Elbow height = link thickness (4 mm), against 8 mm for a P14 pin and 12.25 mm for an O20 hub.","topology":"As fivebar-roll, but each elbow is a rolling-contact joint instead of a pin:\n- each leg: S -> R_Ak (ACTIVE, O20 hub, dome axis u_k) -> crank k, ending in a convex edge disc (angular radius rho about c1k, fixed in the crank);\n- that disc rolls without slip on coupler k's edge disc (radius rho about c2k), held by three crossed 0.1 mm steel or Ti flexure straps (Jacob's ladder); 1 DOF, no bearing, both link ends on ONE layer;\n- coupler k -> R_C (passive) at the arm-fixed axis W.\nCoupler 1 -> R_D (ACTIVE roll about W) -> arm plate -> neck to BOTTOM.\n\nThe joints at W and the base are real rolling bearings. 6 joints: 3 active, 3 passive (C plus two rolling elbows).","main_risk":"It is outside the user's MVP1 scope phrase 'basic rolling bearings': flexure straps are new hardware (strap fatigue, preload, wear of the rolling faces). Ask before building. Radial retention: a rolling pair has no capacity along the sphere's radius, so it needs a small V-lip on the edges or the straps' width. The kinematics change (coupled 2R elbows), so the elbow paths differ from fivebar-roll and the IK is numerical. Strap wrap limits each elbow to about +-120 deg of psi. Sensing a rolling elbow is not needed (passive), but a rolling elbow cannot later become an active joint.","verdict":""},{"id":"rolling-fin-j2","name":"Rolling-fin J2: strap-constrained spherical rolling cones on top of the scapula","family":"","lens":"ideation A","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Rolling-fin J2","description":"Two small curved walls stand on the stable top of the scapula, like the Risley turret's rings, and roll on each other as the arm moves. The contact point, and with it J2's axis, travels along the walls, so J2 is a joint with a moving axis. One strap runs from the moving fin down to the deltoid hub. REST: fins on the top, contact at one end of the fins, strap over the acromion to the lateral deltoid. OVERHEAD: the contact rolls to the other end, and the strap's root turns over the top. ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: J1 turns the fin pair forward / backward, and the strap runs over the front / back of the shoulder. The fins are 6 mm tall, so J2 has no hub stack.","topology":"S -> J1: O20 v5 hub about b -> L1: hub upper plate + fin F1, a radial Ti fin 6 mm tall and 2.5 mm thick on a cone about u1 through GH (half-angle gamma1) -> J2: fin F2 on L2 (cone about u2, half-angle gamma2, with angle(u1,u2) = gamma1+gamma2) rolls on F1 without slip, held by two crossed 0.1 mm stainless flexure straps (figure-8) -> L2: F2 + strap to J3 -> J3: O20 v5 hub about W -> H. Active: J1, J2 (rolling coordinate phi), J3. Passive: none.","main_risk":"The benefit is unproven: the moving axis has to actually widen the band, so test it first with a 50-design kinematic screen. Crossed flexure straps may count as 'cables' under the MVP1 rule (ask the user). IK needs a 1-D root find with several roots, so the solution can jump branches between frames. Slip/creep and strap fatigue. The fins are thin walls (pinch points, stiffness).","verdict":""},{"id":"rotator-cuff-pair","name":"Rotator-cuff pair: supraspinatus and infraspinatus arcs converging on an exo-tubercle hub","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Rotator-cuff pair","description":"Two curved struts laid out like the supraspinatus and the infraspinatus. One runs from the top of the scapula forward-laterally over the acromion edge. The other runs from the back of the scapula around the posterior cap. Both converge on the arm just under the acromion edge, where the real cuff inserts on the greater tubercle. That is the arm's lowest-motion spot, w(112,-15), 60 mm from GH, with a 94 % disc ceiling. A v5 hub there carries leg 1; the arm piece holds the hub and runs to BOTTOM. Rest: both struts hug the top and back of the cap. Overhead: the tubercle rises under the acromion, leg 1 shortens, and leg 2 wraps further around the back. Across-body: leg 2 lengthens over the back. Behind-back: leg 2 shortens and leg 1 swings back. No metal sits over the front of the shoulder.","topology":"Parallel: two legs plus one hub. Leg 1 (supraspinatus line): radial pivot on a scapula pad A1 over the supraspinous fossa / top [PASSIVE] -> telescoping great-circle arc, length theta1 [ACTIVE] -> radial v5 hub at the 'exo-greater-tubercle' B1, the arm's low-motion spot [ACTIVE: the twist angle between leg 1's arc and the arm pad]. Leg 2 (infraspinatus line): radial pivot on a pad A2 over the infraspinous fossa / spine [PASSIVE] -> telescoping arc, length theta2 [ACTIVE] -> radial pivot on a second arm pad B2 on the posterior cap, or coaxial on B1 [PASSIVE] -> arm piece -> BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Span ratio: ~2.6x on the clips and ~5x over the workspace for these point pairs (my check: 46-128 / 27-150 deg). That forces lazy-tongs or 3-stage telescopes. The B1 hub cap (12.25 mm) must never be crossed by leg 2. The sensing Jacobian of (theta1, theta2, twist) can degenerate where the two legs become coplanar (e.g. arm at the side with the insertion between the two origins). The two legs and the single hub carry all of MVP2's torque.","verdict":""},{"id":"rus-tripod-gh-centre","name":"3-RUS tripod that uses the wearer's own GH as the spherical centre","family":"","lens":"weird-compliant","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":15,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"3-RUS tripod that uses the wearer's own GH as the…","description":"The cranks live only on stable scapula skin: the dome (skin 39-68 mm from GH, < ~11 mm change, never visited by the humerus), the infraspinous fossa and the top-front of the acromion. Nothing rigid has to follow the moving transition except three slim struts, which can be bent to hug it. REST: three 'suspenders' run from the cranks over the deltoid cap to balls on the anterolateral, lateral and posterolateral arm strip. OVERHEAD: the arm balls come within ~70 deg of the dome pivots and the chord lengths shrink. Measured: dome pivot to a lateral ball at s150, 145-222 mm over all poses; to an anterolateral ball at s100, 100-171 mm. The cranks swing to take up the change and the struts fold along the top and back of the raised arm, so the bunching acromion only has to clear O6 rods. ACROSS THE BODY: the posterior strut stretches across the back of the shoulder and the anterior one shortens…","topology":"S -> three ACTIVE R crank hubs (O20 v5). Their axes do NOT pass through GH (near skin-normal), and the pivots sit on stable scapula skin: the dome near p*, the posterior scapula (lat -10..+10 / lon -125..-100) and the top-front of the acromion (lat ~50 / lon ~10), 55-130 mm from GH -> crank i (radius rc 20-55 mm) -> passive U joint (miniature needle-bearing cardan, O10) -> rigid curved strut i (Ti tube O6 or a 6 x 4 bar, chord length 90-220 mm, bent to follow the deltoid) -> passive S joint (ball, O8-10) in a socket on an arm-fixed bracket at b_i on the user's cuff / arm strip (s 80-180, th -90..+90). The wearer's GH closes the loop; there is no exo joint at GH. Variant switch: telescoping straight struts with an ACTIVE P (linear encoder) and no crank (3-UPS).","main_risk":"Conditioning, measured. With pivots ~60 mm from GH and balls 52-72 mm off the humerus axis, every pivot-ball pair tried has some pose whose chord line passes within 1-4 mm of GH (that strut then has no lever about GH). The smallest singular value of the 3x3 strut-moment matrix reaches ~0 for both triples tried (5th percentile 0.006-0.009 per 100 mm). So there are Type-II singularities inside the workspace: there the arm can rotate without moving the cranks, and an unpowered tripod loses tracking (R3, R17). Pivots far from GH on the scapula body and balls on brackets away from the humerus axis may fix it, at a cost in stand-off. Chord ranges of 70-110 mm need 35-55 mm cranks, which sweep…","verdict":""},{"id":"scapula-halo-track","name":"Scapula halo track: J1 is a closed cam track on low-motion scapula skin and the J2 hub rides it","family":"spherical 3R, track base (J1 = carriage on a closed non-circular scapula track)","lens":"ideation A","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:18:55Z","reach":0.5312,"follow":0.7833,"inband14":0.5591,"inband10":0.3406,"contact":0.0,"gap":87.64,"v_gap":87.64,"v_reach":0.5312,"v_follow":0.7833,"v_inband14":0.5591,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":126,"doc":"docs/concepts/scapula-halo-track.md","short":"Scapula halo track","description":"Rest:\n- A Ti ring (8 x 5 mm section, about 240 mm long, about 50 g) rides 2-5 mm over the top of the shoulder on skin fixed to the scapula.\n- In the scapula frame it sits at el about 15-35 deg at the back over the spine and supraspinatus (55-60 mm from GH), el about 40-55 laterally over the acromion, el about 60-75 at the front over the anterior acromion and clavicle, and el about 80 medially near the summit.\n- Over the fit clips this skin moves at most 13-20 mm against the scapula, and at most 5 mm over most of the ring.\n- The carriage and the J2 hub sit in the posterior or posterolateral sector. A short link 2 (55-65 deg when W is at the proximity agent's low-motion spot, about (112, -15)) reaches the J3 hub on the top-lateral deltoid just below the acromion edge.\n\nOther poses (from a kinematic screen with a sample loop):\n- Overhead: the crossing stays posterior (ring azimuth…","topology":"Scapula base (S): a closed halo track T fixed to the handler, bent along an iso-motion contour of the scapula-frame skin around the acromion summit. It runs low over the supraspinatus and scapular spine at the back and climbs over the acromion laterally and anteriorly. -> J1 ACTIVE: a bogie carriage on T. Its coordinate s (position along the track) is the joint value, so J1 is a 1-DOF spherical joint whose path is non-circular. The carriage frame is [u(s), track tangent, normal]. -> J2 ACTIVE: an O20 hub on the carriage. Its axis is p2 = u(s), the radial direction from GH through the carriage. -> link 2: a strap, arc a2 (55-115 deg). -> J3 ACTIVE: an O20 hub at W, fixed to the arm on skin a hub can sit on (deltoid cap, delta 75-115 from the down-the-arm axis). -> arm-end plate + neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"- Reach against track placement: the loop that stays on the best skin closes only 59-67 % of the workspace (behind-back has no crossing). The 20 mm loop reaches 88 %, but link 2 becomes long (115 deg) over the deltoid.\n- Near-tangent crossings are singular: two roots close together, as at rest. There the carriage speeds up, and unpowered the arm can push the carriage across the track (transmission angle, R17).\n- A non-circular track needs a bogie carriage (2 roller pairs about 15 mm apart) with some play at changes of curvature.\n- The user's handler must carry a ring around the acromion.","verdict":"Not worth a GPU sweep as it stands (gap 87.6 vs the baseline's 68.9). The track itself is 80 % in band. But the loop that stays on in-band scapula skin closes only 64.5 % of poses, and the rigid link 2 (scapula to arm) is 14 % in band. AUDIT: death stands. Best re-measured on Bench(60,160) fast: gap 87.64. A loop following the full in-band zone (eroded 1-2 cells, no max-smoothing) closes 67-73 % but leaves the zone (track 97-149 mm or link 2 0-3 % in band): gap 111-148."},{"id":"scapular-five-bar-twist","name":"Scapular five-bar + twist: both pointing joints are scapula-fixed hubs on the two quietest seats (bold)","family":"","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Scapular five-bar + twist","description":"Both pointing actuators and their hubs are scapula-fixed on the quietest seats (acromion top 2-5 mm, posterior-superior 0.2-2.4 mm). The four straps converge on C on the upper lateral arm.\n- Rest: leg A runs down the lateral shoulder, leg B over the posterior deltoid, C on the lateral deltoid with the neck to BOTTOM.\n- Overhead: both legs fold over the top-back as C rises.\n- Across the body: leg B stretches; leg A folds over the top.\n- Behind the back: leg A stretches forward-lateral; leg B folds.","topology":"Scapula base with TWO fixed active hubs: A on the acromion top, sdir(55,15); B on the posterior-superior zone, sdir(40,100) -> proximal straps L_A (alpha_A) and L_B (alpha_B) -> passive elbow hubs E_A and E_B -> distal straps D_A (beta_A) and D_B (beta_B) -> these meet at C, an arm-fixed site at armpt(30,0,~80) with a stacked double O20 hub. At C, the D_A-D_B joint is passive and the D_B-arm joint is the active twist about GH-C. All axes pass through GH: a spherical 2-DOF five-bar plus a coaxial twist, ending in a neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"1. Twice the moving metal. Kinematic bands: acromion -> C is 25-138 deg (-> alpha_A = beta_A = 72); posterior -> C is 57-162 deg (-> alpha_B ~110, beta_B ~55). That is ~310 deg of straps crossing the shoulder vs 158 in the baseline, so in-band will probably drop.\n2. Reach is the intersection of both legs' reach.\n3. Four straps converge on the deltoid at C, where the bulge is.\n4. Six joints vs the user's wish for 3.","verdict":""},{"id":"scapular-race-j2-carriage","name":"Scapular race: J1 is a rail on the bony rim and J2 rides its carriage","family":"spherical 3R (J1 as a scapula-fixed race)","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T23:10:51Z","reach":0.575,"follow":0.85,"inband14":0.617,"inband10":0.2763,"contact":0.0774,"gap":78.54,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":8737,"doc":"docs/concepts/scapular-race-j2-carriage.md","short":"Scapular race","description":"Two revolutes with axes through GH can never share a location: their hubs sit on their own axes, and two lines through GH meet only at GH. The rail breaks this, because a revolute's bearing can lie anywhere on a circle around its axis. So J1 becomes a 10 mm wide, 4 mm tall rail fixed to the base, lying ~4 mm over bony, intrusion-free rim. J2 sits on the rail carriage and always rides that quiet rim. The single crossing strap L2 runs from the carriage over the deltoid to J3.\n- Rest (precheck geometry): carriage on the posterior/top part of the rim, L2 down the posterior deltoid to the septum barrel.\n- Overhead and across the body: the carriage slides along the rim; the rail never moves.\n- Behind the back: the carriage goes to the anterior end.\n- Poses that would need J2 off the rail are not reachable.\nKinematic hint (also useful for the hub-only GPU family): in the scapula frame, the…","topology":"Scapula base carrying a fixed arc RAIL. J1 is an active R about a1 through GH; the rail is a circle of angular radius gamma around a1, laid on intrusion-free acromion / scapular-spine / clavicle-rim skin -> rolling carriage. The carriage is L1: there is no link -> J2: O20 hub mounted on the carriage, active R. Its axis is the carriage's own direction from GH, so alpha12 = gamma -> L2 strap, the only moving-frame link -> J3, active R. Default J3: the posterolateral septum barrel on its own arm cuff (see septum-barrel-arm-link). Option: a lateral O20 hub at s 5-30 plus a neck to BOTTOM.","main_risk":"Reach. Kinematic precheck: I required the used part of the rail to lie on trunk skin that the arm never enters (13-pose probe) with <= 20 mm skin-radius variation. That leaves at best ~45-50 % of the functional workspace. Two best configurations:\n- a1 = sdir(0,20), gamma 50, alpha23 80, branch +1, J3 septum barrel: J2 phi -104..+64, skin R 45-61 mm.\n- a1 = sdir(40,-100), gamma 70, alpha23 70, branch -1: phi 20..116.\nWithout the skin constraint the chain closes in 92-99 % of poses (a1 = sdir(40,0), gamma = alpha23 = 60), but J2 must circle ~280 deg, through the arm.\nSo this is probably a high-band, low-reach clip/overhead specialist. A constant-radius rail over 44-62 mm skin also varies its…","verdict":"dead (converged, 8737 tries): best honest gap 78.54 (score 71.44) vs champion 68.93 - a high-band, low-reach specialist (in-band14 0.62, rail 0.96, reach 0.575); gap 0 is impossible for a one-radius race (raw-skin ceiling: reach <= 0.71 at R <= 80)"},{"id":"scapular-skate-remote-j1","name":"Scapular skate: J1 is a puck gliding on a flat plate on the back of the shoulder (remote-centre rotation, passive in-plane slip)","family":"","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Scapular skate","description":"A rotation about the normal of a flat region maps that plane onto itself. So everything J1 carries on the plate (puck, J2's base) stays at constant height over the flat back of the shoulder, whatever q1 is. There is no L1 swinging through the air and no J1 hub on the shoulder top.\n- Rest: the puck sits on the infraspinatus ~84 mm from the foot of GH on the plate, J2 tilted on it, L2 down the posterior deltoid to J3 on the posterolateral upper arm.\n- Overhead: the puck rotates along its arc toward the spine/upper plate; L2 shortens over the top-back.\n- Across the body: the puck moves toward the medial/upper part of the plate.\n- Behind the back: the puck swings toward the lateral/lower edge; this is where it may leave the flat region.\nThe plate is base metal over the scapula's quiet skin (0.2-2.4 mm Lagrangian spread at lat 30-60, lon 60-120).","topology":"Scapula base = a flat Ti plate (3 mm, ~110 x 90 mm), parallel to the posterior scapular skin plane (fitted normal ~sdir(27,75), ~59 mm behind GH) -> SKATE: a puck on a ball cage under a retaining lip. It has 3 planar coordinates: the in-plane rotation is the active J1 (about the plate normal through GH, a remote axis with no hub), and the 2 in-plane translations are passive -> J2: O20 hub on the puck, active R. Its axis runs from GH through the puck point and is tilted ~55 deg to the plate -> L2 strap -> J3: active R, either an O20 hub at armpt(30,40,~80) plus a neck to BOTTOM, or the septum barrel.","main_risk":"1. The posterior skin is less flat and less scapula-fixed than hoped. A plane fit over 219 x 165 mm has 9.6 mm rms (31 mm max), and those trunk points drift 12 mm (median; 29 mm p90) from the plane across the 13 probe poses. So keep the plate to ~110 x 90 mm near lat 0-45, lon 60-120.\n2. The kinematic band from the plate normal to the s30 posterolateral hub is 35-145 deg, so alpha12 ~55 (J2 84 mm from the foot point) and alpha23 ~90. The puck sweeps an 84 mm-radius arc and leaves the plate for large q1.\n3. J2's hub stands 55 deg tilted on the puck, so one rim is ~8 mm higher.\n4. MVP2 has to drive a remote-centre rotation by cable, which is awkward.","verdict":""},{"id":"seed-2r-cring","name":"2R spherical shoulder + C-ring axial bearing on the upper arm","family":"distributed DOF","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:49:49Z","reach":0.4437,"follow":0.7333,"inband14":0.3886,"inband10":0.2552,"contact":0.1154,"gap":129.98,"v_gap":129.98,"v_reach":0.4437,"v_follow":0.7333,"v_inband14":0.3886,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":25,"doc":"docs/concepts/seed-2r-cring.md","short":"2R spherical shoulder","description":"Only two hubs over the shoulder; the axial-rotation joint becomes a low ring hugging the arm where the skin is stable (mid upper arm), so link 2 only has to reach the ring, not wrap the deltoid.","topology":"scapula base -> R1 hub (axis through GH, on top/back of the shoulder) -> link -> R2 hub (axis through GH) -> link to an open C-ring thin-section bearing (axis = humerus long axis) around the upper arm just below the deltoid insertion -> user's upper-arm cuff; passive self-aligning slide (along the humerus) between ring and cuff","main_risk":"","verdict":"dead: gap 129.98 vs 68.93 for the spherical 3R on the same bench (25 configurations); not worth a GPU sweep - the limiter is structural: the C-track's carriage must travel ~220 deg round the arm (axial rotation turns p2's azimuth 1:1) but a circular track about any GH-through humerus-fixed axis hugs the arm only over its lateral +-60 deg"},{"id":"seed-arc-rails","name":"Three concentric arc rails about the GH centre (no hubs)","family":"remote-centre rails","lens":"remote-centre","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:26:30Z","reach":0.894,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.186,"inband10":0.097,"contact":0.01,"gap":73.0,"v_gap":73.0,"v_reach":0.894,"v_follow":1.0,"v_inband14":0.186,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":449,"doc":"docs/concepts/seed-arc-rails.md","short":"Three concentric arc rails about the GH centre","description":"Replace every hub by a curved guide rail lying on the skin at constant radius from GH; nothing stands up 12 mm off the skin; rails overlap the shoulder like a spherical cage.","topology":"scapula base -> curved rail 1 (circular arc centred on GH, lying on the back/top of the shoulder) with carriage -> curved rail 2 (meridian arc centred on GH, carried by carriage 1) -> carriage 2 -> open C-ring rail around the upper arm (axial rotation) -> cuff; passive slide at the cuff","main_risk":"","verdict":"dead - not worth a GPU sweep: bigger rails are always worse (the hub limit wins, i.e. the 3R it came from); converged best 73.0 does not beat the baseline 68.93 and parks the J2 rail 17 cm out (inband14 0.19)"},{"id":"seed-offset-3r-slides","name":"Non-spherical offset 3R with passive self-aligning slides","family":"offset axes","lens":"serial-topology","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:15:34Z","reach":0.7688,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.595,"inband10":0.3496,"contact":0.11,"gap":57.95,"v_gap":67.83,"v_reach":0.7611,"v_follow":0.9182,"v_inband14":0.5024,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":632,"doc":"docs/concepts/seed-offset-3r-slides.md","short":"Non-spherical offset 3R with passive…","description":"Drop the requirement that all axes meet at GH: offset axes let links lie flat on the skin; the passive slides make up the difference.","topology":"scapula base -> R1 (axis offset 10-30 mm from GH) -> R2 (offset) -> R3 (axis near the humerus axis) -> passive 2-DOF self-aligning slide pair (prismatic along and across the humerus) -> cuff","main_risk":"","verdict":"not worth its own GPU sweep: net -0.84 gap vs the in-model spherical control with the same directions (57.95 vs 58.79, inside greedy-drop noise) - the slides cost 6.4, R1/R2 offsets buy back 7.3; the gain over the 68.93 baseline is the model's free-underside fit (62.58 at the champion directions) and a direction re-tune that belongs to the spherical family (hand d_el -6.4, d_wd +4.1, d_wa +3.6 to its GPU sweep)"},{"id":"seed-pauldron-shells","name":"Nested sliding shells (pauldron) + arm ring","family":"nested shells","lens":"body-hugging","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:01:06Z","reach":0.75,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.2718,"inband10":0.1513,"contact":0.0164,"gap":79.46,"v_gap":79.46,"v_reach":0.75,"v_follow":1.0,"v_inband14":0.2718,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":107,"doc":"docs/concepts/seed-pauldron-shells.md","short":"Nested sliding shells (pauldron) + arm ring","description":"Metal as thin overlapping spherical shells over the deltoid like armour lames; the joints are the large-diameter sliding interfaces (iglidur liners), so the metal sits close to the skin everywhere.","topology":"scapula base shell -> shell 1 (spherical cap segment centred on GH, sliding over the base shell about axis u1) -> shell 2 (sliding over shell 1 about axis u2, both through GH) -> C-ring axial bearing on the arm -> cuff","main_risk":"","verdict":"dead: best honest gap 79.46 (reach 0.75, follow 1.00, in-band 14 0.27, contact 0.016) vs the 3R champion 68.93; not worth a GPU sweep. The ring-bearing joints do not remove the 3R family's middle-axis limiter (J2): a revolute concentric with GH must be a surface of revolution, so spreading J2 over a 250 deg ring makes its one radius clear the worst of the arc (about 120 mm vs 61-86 mm along u2). Transferable: a scapula-fixed J1 ring bearing (7 mm stack) sits at one radius of 58 mm, 65-94 % in band - graft it on the champion."},{"id":"seed-rcm-parallelogram","name":"Base yaw + double-parallelogram remote-centre elevation + arm ring","family":"RCM parallelogram","lens":"closed-loop","status":"modelling","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":4,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":12,"doc":"","short":"Base yaw + double-parallelogram remote-centre…","description":"Surgical-robot RCM idea: the parallelogram puts the virtual elevation axis at GH while its bars run along the skin behind the shoulder.","topology":"scapula base -> R1 (axis through GH, roughly vertical-ish in S) -> planar double parallelogram (all 5-6 joints parallel, its remote centre of motion at GH) giving elevation in the plane set by R1 -> C-ring axial bearing on the upper arm -> cuff; passive slide at the cuff","main_risk":"","verdict":""},{"id":"seed-spm-3rrr","name":"Low-profile spherical parallel 3-RRR wrapped around the shoulder","family":"spherical parallel","lens":"closed-loop","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:18:04Z","reach":0.1812,"follow":0.3,"inband14":0.3552,"inband10":0.2514,"contact":0.0208,"gap":193.44,"v_gap":193.44,"v_reach":0.1812,"v_follow":0.3,"v_inband14":0.3552,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":51,"doc":"docs/concepts/seed-spm-3rrr.md","short":"Low-profile spherical parallel 3-RRR wrapped…","description":"Spreads the load over three short legs that each stay close to their own patch of skin instead of one long serial chain over the whole shoulder; singularities can be placed outside the range.","topology":"scapula base plate carries 3 active R joints (axes through GH) at 3 spots around the shoulder (back, top, front); each leg: proximal arc link -> passive R (axis through GH) -> distal arc link -> passive R on a moving 'platform' ring around the upper arm (axes through GH)","main_risk":"","verdict":"dead: gap 193.44 vs 68.93 for the 3R on the same bench; not worth a GPU sweep - the limiter is structural (leg closure / leg-leg collisions over the shoulder workspace, and nine rigid parts each clearing the highest skin they sweep)"},{"id":"septum-barrel-arm-link","name":"Septum barrel: near-axial J3 lying along the quietest arm strip on its own cuff (the humerus is the link to BOTTOM)","family":"","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Septum barrel","description":"Joints sit where the rig's skin moves least.\n- J1 sits on the bony acromion top (2-5 mm skin spread in the scapula frame).\n- J3 sits on the posterolateral intermuscular-septum strip. In the arm frame, th 0..45 deg and s 120-240 mm from GH, the material-point radial spread over 13 poses is only 0.7-1.6 mm, the quietest skin on the body model (the anterolateral strip, -45..0, is 2-4 mm).\n- An axis through GH can only be tangent to skin along the arm, so J3 there is a barrel: two MR117 back-to-back, OD 14, 24 mm long, lying along the arm, rather than a hub standing 12.25 mm off it.\nMetal by pose:\n- Rest: barrel on the posterolateral arm ~150 mm below GH beside BOTTOM; L2 climbs the posterior deltoid to J2 behind the shoulder; L1 crosses to the acromion.\n- Overhead: the barrel faces back/up with the raised arm; J2 sits over the posterior-superior scapula; L1 short over the top.\n- Across the…","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: O20 hub, active R, a1 = sdir(55,15) on the acromion top -> L1 strap -> J2: O20 hub, active R, floating -> L2 strap -> J3: BARREL hinge, active R. Its axis passes through GH and is aimed down the posterolateral septum strip, 17-24 deg off the humeral axis, so the barrel lies ALONG the arm with its axis nearly tangent to the skin -> a separate proximal arm cuff (saddle) at s 120-180, th 30-40, beside BOTTOM. No neck: the humerus itself is the rigid link between this cuff and the user's cuff/BOTTOM.","main_risk":"1. J3 is now nearly the humeral axis, so q3 carries most of the axial rotation (+-75 deg at a given direction). L2 and J2 swing around the deltoid cap relative to the arm skin, and the cap is 50-82 mm from the humeral axis depending on azimuth.\n2. L1 gets long. Kinematic band from the acromion to the septum point (s150, th 20) is 55-150 deg (p5-p95), so alpha12 ~100 and alpha23 ~50. With the central axis sdir(35,-5) the band is 29-128 deg (-> 75/55).\n3. A GH-aimed line converges on the humeral axis proximally, so the barrel must be short: its underside rises ~10 mm over 24 mm.\n4. A second cuff adds skin pressure (R11), and the barrel cuff must clear BOTTOM (30 mm wide at th ~0, r 56-75 mm).","verdict":""},{"id":"skin-cam-rails","name":"Skin-following cam rails with a telescoping crossing (profiled-radius version of the crossed rails)","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Skin-following cam rails with a telescoping crossing","description":"The same picture as the crossed rails, but each rail is a 3-D curve that hugs its own skin. The exo-acromial arch dips to 2-4 mm over the acromion, where the skin is low (37-45 mm from GH), and rises over the supraspinatus / spine (55-60 mm). The arm collar follows the deltoid cap (45-64 mm across poses in the arm frame), then carries on down the lateral arm as the neck to BOTTOM, following the arm skin. At the crossing, a short ball-spline post on the J2 axis lets the two carriages sit at different heights: one carriage stack plus 0-15 mm of slide. Rest, overhead, across-body and behind-back: as in the crossed rails (the crossing travels along both rails). The difference is that the rails keep a constant clearance from their own skin instead of a constant radius, and the slide post grows where the arm rail's skin is higher than the arch's.","topology":"Same chain as crossed-muscle-rails, but the rails are not spheres. Scapula base -> R1 = carriage 1 on a scapula-fixed rail whose directions lie on the cone (a1, a12) and whose radius r1(phi) follows the scapula skin [ACTIVE] -> C2 = cylindrical joint on the radial axis a2 at the crossing: rotation [ACTIVE] + radial slide along a2 [PASSIVE, set by the two rail profiles] (rolling ball spline / linear ball bushing) -> R3 = carriage 2 on an arm-fixed rail on the cone (a3, a23) whose radius r2(psi) follows the arm skin; the rail continues down the lateral arm as the neck to BOTTOM [ACTIVE] -> arm.","main_risk":"(1) The slide post: where r2 - r1 at the crossing exceeds the carriage stack by more than 15-20 mm, J2 becomes a tall post. The upper rail's profile must then be raised to keep the slide short, which costs band. (2) The ball spline carries the J2 tilt moment (10 N.m in MVP2), which drives its section size and friction. (3) Non-circular rails need custom carriages that follow a varying radius and curvature (4 wheels on a 3-D curve). They are harder to make and to keep free of play, and need 5-axis machining. (4) All of the crossed rails' other risks remain (rail length, stack, tangency).","verdict":""},{"id":"skin-normal-3r-knuckle-cufflink","name":"All-skin-normal 3R closed by three pins (J2 knuckle + pinned cuff link)","family":"offset axes, all-revolute self-alignment","lens":"round3:self-alignment / loop closure through cheap rotational passive joints","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:19:55Z","reach":0.6937,"follow":0.9667,"inband14":0.8675,"inband10":0.51,"contact":0.0059,"gap":24.47,"v_gap":31.89,"v_reach":0.6167,"v_follow":0.9636,"v_inband14":0.8705,"v_contact":0.0061,"n_configs":2229,"doc":"docs/concepts/skin-normal-3r-knuckle-cufflink.md","short":"All-skin-normal 3R closed by three pins","description":"All three O20 hubs sit square on their skin (snh k722 seats: tilts 0.34 / 0.42 / 0.21, lines 5.1 / 6.9 / 6.8 mm off GH). The 3D drift that snh took with a 3-translation stage (refuted: its radial slide would stand ~25 mm) is taken by three pins. (a) Knuckle: link 2 is split 30 mm past hub 2 by a passive pin parallel to J2, flat in hub 2's skin plane; it swings 9..35 deg and supplies the drift along hub 3's normal. (b) Hub 3's 45 mm E arm + a 30 mm cuff link pinned to an arm pad take the in-plane residual (20.6 x 23.0 mm; elbow 68-118 deg). Rest: three flat hubs, two flat straps, one knuckle boss. Overhead / across the body the knuckle folds a few degrees; the radial stroke never appears as height.","topology":"scapula -> J1 ACTIVE R (tilted, 5.1 mm off GH) -> L1 -> J2 ACTIVE R (tilted, 6.9 mm off GH) -> crank (30 mm, hub-2 rotor) -> pin k PASSIVE R (parallel to J2) -> L2 -> J3 ACTIVE R (tilted, 6.8 mm off GH) -> E arm (45) -> pin a PASSIVE R (parallel to J3) -> cuff link (30) -> pin b PASSIVE R (parallel to J3) -> arm pad -> humerus (loop closed by GH). Spatial Grubler: 7 bodies, 6 x 1-DOF joints + GH(3): M = 6(7-1-7) + 9 = 3, no redundant constraint. Orientation fixes q1, q2 + qk, q3 + qa + qb; the radial closure fixes qk (one root, authority >= 26.5 mm/rad at p1); the two-circle closure fixes qa, qb. All passive coordinates set by closure (R17).","main_risk":"(1) The in-band premise (snh chain 0.891) comes from a sweep record that is itself under audit for skin-contoured plates (floating-centre-3r was refuted for that) - the model must use flat straps at their real height. (2) Reach is lower than osn at snh seats (fold-free 0.768 bench, ws 0.681 -> reach ~0.656 = 24 gap fast, worse exact): 90+ needs the fold-frontier seeds or a fold lever. (3) Six joints to sense (twist = q3 + qa + qb, J2 = q2 + qk). (4) The knuckle stack (+3.15 on L2) and the +-6.7 mm tangential shift of hub 3 over the deltoid. (5) Near the fold the radial authority falls; check the 5 mm/rad floor on full 10 fps clips.","verdict":"VERIFIED at v_gap 31.89 on Bench(110,360) exact frozen (score 88.40, not the claimed 91.10 fast; below the 90 target, behind the pin knee's verified 30.35), far ahead of the champion's 75.94; 0 passive translations, 3 passive pins. Caveats: liner give >= 2 mm swings the knuckle into contact; the pins must be read to track the arm (R5 counting rule, user ruling needed). fast-bench leader candidate, claim pending the frozen exact wear: k1187 (J1-site knuckle, CMA B1 converged) 24.47 on Bench(60,160) fast = score 91.10 vs the pin-knee 26.34; knuckle-locked control at the same seats 26.91 (+2.44 from the knuckle); J2-site best 25.97 (locked 32.93, +6.96). Lever = small axis-direction decoupling…"},{"id":"skin-normal-3r-pin-knee","name":"Leader 3R closed by a flat two-pin knee instead of the crossed cuff slides (0 passive translations)","family":"offset 3R, exact 2-slide subfamily, planar revolute closure","lens":"round3:self-alignment / loop closure through cheap rotational passive joints","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":2,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:32:47Z","reach":0.6937,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.9033,"inband10":0.5323,"contact":0.0,"gap":20.63,"v_gap":30.35,"v_reach":0.6056,"v_follow":1.0,"v_inband14":0.9043,"v_contact":0.0091,"n_configs":22169,"doc":"docs/concepts/skin-normal-3r-pin-knee.md","short":"Leader 3R closed by a flat two-pin knee instead of the crossed cuff slides","description":"offset-skin-normal-3r (k4216) with its arm end rebuilt. J1 and J2 are O20 hubs on GH rays (acromion top, posterior acromion); J3 is an O20 hub on the lateral deltoid with its axis tilted b3 toward the skin normal so its line misses GH by 7 mm (b3 0.23) to 19 mm (b3 0.6). The 2 crossed RSR3M guides, carriage A, strip, carriage B and bridge are gone. Hub 3's rotor carries a flat Ti arm (E) to pin a; a flat Ti link runs from pin a to pin b on an arm pad fixed to the user's cuff/BOTTOM. Both pins are parallel to J3 and lie in hub 3's plane, tangent to the lateral arm. Rest: the knee lies flat at ~90 deg below hub 3. Overhead / across the body / behind the back the J3 line drift (11.6 x 14.1 mm at k4216, all in hub 3's plane) opens and closes the knee (77-105 deg with E 45 / link 30; 55-140 deg with 20 / 20); nothing stands up.","topology":"scapula (user's handler) -> J1 ACTIVE R (axis on a GH ray) -> L1 -> J2 ACTIVE R (axis on a GH ray) -> L2 -> J3 ACTIVE R (axis d3 tilted b3, line offset d from GH) -> E arm -> pin a PASSIVE R (parallel to d3) -> knee link -> pin b PASSIVE R (parallel to d3) -> arm pad on BOTTOM/cuff -> humerus; the loop closes through the wearer's GH. Mobility: q1, q2 set by pointing d3 (spherical IK); the relative motion link 2 -> arm is a pure rotation about the GH line parallel to d3 (the 2-slide closure theorem), i.e. planar; the planar RRR (J3, a, b) has exactly 3 planar DOF, so q3, qa, qb are set by a closed-form two-circle closure. Spatial Grubler gives 2 because of one redundant (d3-normal) constraint, which is identically 0.000 mm in this subfamily; manufacturing error and the radial part of the real GH translation go to the cuff liner (<= 3 mm, stated, as in osn). No undetermined coordinate (R17).","main_risk":"(0) Exact bench at k4216: honest pin knee 41.58 vs the zero-passive spherical reference 41.73 and osn 43.36 - a tie, so it ranks behind spine-parked-j2-3r unless a b3 > 0.23 re-CMA pays on the exact bench. (1) The honest pin stack is the 3/3 emulation, not 2/2: 30.11 fast (score 89.1), so k4216 alone does not cross 27.5 - it needs the re-CMA and the freed b3. (2) Fast vs exact: osn went 32.61 -> 42.59 on the verifier's Bench(110,360) exact; the exact numbers above are the ones that count. (3) Reach is untouched (0.706 fast / 0.611 exact = 19-29 gap points from the J2 fold) - 90+ on the exact bench needs a fold lever stacked on it. (4) Hub 3 stays 0.495 in band. (5) The swept link (up to 48…","verdict":"screened: 20.63 (score 92.50) on Bench(60,160) fast, converged; worn FROZEN on Bench(110,360) exact 30.35 (score 88.96) - 11-12 points better than the zero-passive spherical 3R (41.73) and the 2-slide leader (42.59) with 0 passive translations (2 iglidur pins, 5 mm stack), but short of 90 on the verification bench: the whole gap is reach (J2 fold); freed b3 does not pay (37.04 at b3 0.54)"},{"id":"skin-normal-hubs-selfaligning","name":"Skin-normal hubs + passive cuff translation stage (non-concurrent 3R)","family":"non-concurrent 3R + passive XYZ cuff stage","lens":"distributed-dof","status":"refuted","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T22:44:00Z","reach":0.6562,"follow":1.0,"inband14":0.8031,"inband10":0.3491,"contact":0.0179,"gap":35.86,"v_gap":44.3,"v_reach":0.592,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":0.792,"v_contact":0.027,"n_configs":755,"doc":"docs/concepts/skin-normal-hubs-selfaligning.md","short":"Skin-normal hubs + passive cuff translation stage","description":"Each hub stands square on its seat: the 12.25 mm stack is normal to the skin. Seats are chosen purely by quietness and flatness: J1 on the bony acromion, J2's home on the posterior-superior zone (0.2-2.4 mm spread). A GH-radial hub on sloping skin sits tilted, and an O20 rim then rises 10*sin(tilt), i.e. 5-8 mm on the 20-30 deg slopes of the posterior shoulder; the stack being normal removes that.\n- Rest: J1 flat on the acromion, J2 flat on the supraspinous area, L2 down the posterior deltoid to J3, stage at BOTTOM's top.\n- Overhead / across the body / behind the back: as for a 3R with these axis directions, plus the stage sliding by the offset-induced error (expected 10-60 mm).","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: O20 hub, active R. Its axis is the skin NORMAL at the acromion seat, not through GH; the line passes ~5-20 mm from GH -> L1 -> J2: O20 hub, active R. Its axis is the skin normal at its home seat on the posterior-superior zone -> L2 -> J3: active R, either a septum barrel or a lateral hub with axis through GH -> passive XYZ translation stage (along the arm on a short rail merged into BOTTOM's top, tangential, radial; +-25 mm) -> arm cuff/BOTTOM.","main_risk":"1. Stage travel and metal: the stage moves ~sum of 2*d_k*sin(q_k/2). Axis offsets of 10-20 mm over 100-150 deg swings give 30-60 mm, and a 3-axis stage on the arm is heavy and tall.\n2. The radial slide changes the arm-end height.\n3. The bench has GH fixed to the scapula, so it shows only the cost, not the R10 benefit.\n4. The skin normals over the shoulder cap pass near GH anyway, so the in-band gain may be small.\nHonest expectation: a modest bench score, valuable mainly for R10.","verdict":"REFUTED as buildable (red team 2026-10-15, two lenses): its cuff is a 3-translation stage whose radial slide has no real component (stands ~25 mm: block + travel), a hard-rule failure; as logged k 722 jams (148 gap) once the verifier's VModel checks are on; repaired (neck 12-13 mm, across carriage added) it scores 37.8-38.5 on Bench(60,160) and 45.76 on Bench(110,360) exact vs the champion's 75.94. Its 2-slide subfamily is offset-skin-normal-3r."},{"id":"skin-stretch-scissor","name":"Skin-stretch scissor: a tapered spherical lazy-tongs from the scapula top to the deltoid","family":"","lens":"ideation A","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":14,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Skin-stretch scissor","description":"Instead of a rigid link that must clear every pose, a small lattice of 6 mm Ti bars stretches between the hub on top of the scapula and the deltoid hub, along the shortest path over the shoulder, the way the skin there stretches. Node k always sits at fraction k/N of the path, and each node has its own fixed radius from GH. The lattice can therefore taper from ~46 mm over the top to ~60 mm over the deltoid, and the bars stay rigid because the node radii never change. REST (sigma ~95-110 deg): stretched, ~12 mm wide, along the acromion-deltoid line. OVERHEAD (sigma ~40 deg): folded into a ~27 mm wide fan over the top of the shoulder, with no tail. ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: the lattice swings with J1 over the front / back of the shoulder top, and stretches to sigma ~117 deg behind the back. Stack: two bar layers + O8 pivot bosses = 5.5 mm.","topology":"S -> J1: O20 v5 hub (or ring) about b on the S-top -> L1 = J1's upper plate, holding the scissor root (one pin + one lateral slot) -> J2: a spherical lazy-tongs of N = 4 units along the great circle b->W, every pivot axis radial through GH. Its single DOF is sigma = angle(b, W), a rotation about p2 = b x W. -> L2 = J3's upper plate, holding the scissor tip (pin + slot) -> J3: O20 v5 hub about W on the lateral deltoid -> H. Active: J1, J2 (the scissor), J3. Passive: 3N+2 = 14 scissor pivots/slots, all dependent (the lazy-tongs has mobility 1).","main_risk":"Play and stiffness: 14 small pivots in series amplify clearance (a lazy-tongs multiplies pivot play by ~N at the tip), and the lattice is soft near full stretch. Every pivot needs preloaded miniature bearings (MR63 class). The kinematics change to A1 = A2 = 90 deg, which is singular at sigma -> 0/180; with the champion's b and W0, the workspace reaches sigma 8 and 158 deg. Folded overhead, the lattice is ~27-30 mm wide and covers more moving skin. There are pinch points between bars (the cover's job) and ~14 bearings to assemble.","verdict":""},{"id":"sliding-rivet-pauldron","name":"Sliding-rivet pauldron: hubless nested plates with follower-in-slot joints","family":"spherical 3R (plates + slots)","lens":"ideation A","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T18:00:01Z","reach":0.4688,"follow":0.5833,"inband14":0.3369,"inband10":0.16,"contact":0.4086,"gap":176.96,"v_gap":172.87,"v_reach":0.5,"v_follow":0.6333,"v_inband14":0.2966,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":40,"doc":"docs/concepts/sliding-rivet-pauldron.md","short":"Sliding-rivet pauldron","description":"Like the lames of a plate-armour pauldron on sliding rivets: four 2 mm Ti plates stacked over the shoulder, each at its own radius where they overlap. The S cap is innermost over the top, then L1, then the L2 band; the H saddle sits under the band's arm end. Plates can slide over each other but cannot collide there. No hub anywhere: every joint is a pair of slots. REST: the S cap (~O50) over the supraspinatus/acromion, L1 a ~O40 disc on it, and the L2 band running from the top down over the acromion to a saddle on the lateral deltoid. OVERHEAD: the band slides through L1's followers, and its far end extends over the trapezius (the same tail as the trombone, but as a plate). ACROSS-BODY / BEHIND-BACK: L1 turns on the cap, and the band runs over the front / back of the shoulder. Stack: 8.5 mm at the top (3 plates + gaps + follower heads), 6 mm at the arm end.","topology":"S cap plate (on top of the scapula) -> J1: 2 needle-roller followers on L1 running in 2 concentric arc slots about b in the S cap -> L1: small plate (the 'guide' plate) -> J2: 2 followers on L1 running in 2 parallel slots of the L2 band; both slots are circles about p2 (polar A1 +- 6 deg) -> L2: band (a ~40 mm wide plate along the b->W path) -> J3: 2 followers on the H saddle running in 2 concentric arc slots about W in L2's arm end -> H: deltoid saddle (+ BOTTOM stub). Active: J1, J2, J3 (each slot pair is one revolute). Passive: none.","main_risk":"Two followers 7-16 mm apart carry the joint moment, so tilt/play and follower preload on 2 mm plates matter (FEA, R9). The slots weaken the plates. Only the slot zones need to be spherical, but the L2 band must ramp from the top radius (~50 mm) to the arm-end radius (~62 mm) between them. It inherits the trombone's tail. Plates are heavier (~180 g).","verdict":"dead: best gap 177.0 vs the 3R baseline 68.93; the sliding band's tail (constant radius, ~80 deg of excess travel stored past b) runs into the neck / arm in 20-40 % of poses; trimming the J2 range, moving the J2 rollers onto an L1 arm toward W, post-mounted band and ring tracks did not remove it - not worth a GPU sweep"},{"id":"spine-parked-j2-3r","name":"Zero-passive spherical 3R at the leader's seats: J2 parked over the posterior acromion, slim O15 J2/J3 hubs","family":"spherical 3R","lens":"round3:zero-passive spherical chain that removes the hub-2-over-deltoid limiter by placement","status":"verified","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T01:35:52Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T03:15:16Z","reach":0.6625,"follow":0.9667,"inband14":0.8764,"inband10":0.5217,"contact":0.0238,"gap":28.49,"v_gap":34.29,"v_reach":0.5944,"v_follow":0.9545,"v_inband14":0.8781,"v_contact":0.0154,"n_configs":40880,"doc":"docs/concepts/spine-parked-j2-3r.md","short":"Zero-passive spherical 3R at the leader's seats","description":"Three hubs, every axis through GH, no stage, no slides. Hub 1 on the acromion top (bvec(96-101, 63-65)), hub 2 behind it over the posterior acromion / scapular spine (branch sigma -1 and a short link 1 keep J2's circle on the posterior-superior cap, so hub 2 never crosses the lateral deltoid), hub 3 on the lateral deltoid with the leader's d3 direction (the d3 'tilt' is a spherical freedom: b3 0 -> 0.23 took the osn reference from 46.71 to 33.40). J2 and J3 are slim O15 hubs (same MR117 pair in a 2 mm Ti wall); J1 stays O20. Arm end: K7 neck to BOTTOM. R10 options judged separately: cuff liner, one RSR3M along the humerus, or the horizontal base follower slide (u = (-0.56, 0.01, 0.83) ISB, 15.2 -> 8.4 mm fit / 6.6 held-out, judge/base_stage.py sources).","topology":"scapula handler -> base -> J1 ACTIVE R (through GH) -> link 1 (12x3 Ti) -> J2 ACTIVE R (through GH, sigma -1) -> link 2 (12x3 Ti, over link 1 and the neck) -> J3 ACTIVE R (through GH) -> neck 14x6 -> BOTTOM -> cuff. 0 passive in the chain (optional: 1 base slide for R10, set by a bony follower - outside the bench).","main_risk":"(0) Ranked 1st by the judge: on Bench(110,360) exact its VModel form (osn slides 0, k4216) scores 41.73, level with or better than every passive arm end measured at the same seats (osn 43.36, honest pin knee 41.58). (1) Reach is capped near 0.61-0.64 by the J2 fold: the short link 1 that parks hub 2 narrows the window. Expected 35-42 after CMA - it does not cross 27.5 alone; its role is the cleanest buildable control (0 passive) that every passive design must beat. (2) q 0.98 turns the arm-over-hub-2 poses into dropped contact poses: a pinch the red team must rule out as non-everyday. (3) The O15 wall (2 mm Ti, M14x0.5 cap thread, ~1.2 mm under the thread root) is MVP1-only; hand calc…","verdict":"screened: 28.49 fast (score 89.64, k7008, CMA stream G); best FROZEN Bench(110,360) exact so far 34.79 (score 87.35; the CMA-B shape, fast 30.24); frozen exact of the 28.49 and 29.31 shapes queued on the farm; converged; 0 passive translations / 0 passive rotations; ties the pin knee (33.84 exact), beats the 2-slide leader (42.59 verified) with no slides; does not cross 27.5"},{"id":"spm-coaxial-turret","name":"Coaxial 3-RRR spherical parallel 'turret': nested dome rings, force-only legs to one arm saddle","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Coaxial 3-RRR spherical parallel 'turret'","description":"A small turret of three concentric rings sits on the stable posterior-top dome of the scapula. Three thin legs fan out from it, each bending once at a small elbow pin, and land on three pins of one compact saddle on the upper-lateral arm. REST: the rings are over the supraspinous fossa and acromion top. There, at scapula-frame latitude 40-70, the skin is 37-64 mm from GH and changes by <= 7 mm per direction in my ray casts over rest, abduction 90, scaption 150, flexion 90, cross-body and extension. The elbows sit about 60 deg from A, on the posterior, lateral and anterior rim. The distal straps lie down the deltoid to the saddle. OVERHEAD: the saddle climbs to the lateral dome. Rotation about A is unlimited, so the cranks just turn round the turret, the elbows swing to the posteromedial side and the straps shorten over the top. ACROSS-BODY: the saddle goes anterior. The posterior leg…","topology":"Scapula S (on the user's handler) -> R1a, R1b, R1c: three ACTIVE revolutes on ONE axis A through GH, built as three nested thin-section ring bearings (polar radii 9 / 15 / 21 deg about A, about O17 / O28 / O40 at r 55) on the posterior-top dome -> crank k: a strap leaving ring k along a meridian, arc alpha1 -> R2k: passive elbow pin (O14 single-bearing pin, axis e_k through GH) -> distal link k: strap, arc alpha2 (a two-force member) -> R3k: passive platform pin (O14, arm-fixed axis v_k through GH; a rod-end ball stud is an option) -> one arm-fixed saddle carrying the three platform pins -> neck to the user's BOTTOM bar. 9 joints, all axes through GH. Cuff slides (outside the count) handle GH translation, as for the baseline.","main_risk":"Workspace forces long legs, so the elbows do not stay on the dome. My kinematic screen used the bench poses (110 clip + 360 ws), the evaluator's rotation convention, 8 deg leg margins, no skin and no parallel-singularity check. The best coaxial layout closes 0.93 of the poses, and only with alpha1 60 / alpha2 120 (A = bvec(-40,40), platform centre wdir(60,30), gamma 20). With alpha1 45 it closes 0.66, with alpha1 30 only 0.24. So three elbows ride the rim like the champion's J2 (an 18-point R1 loss zone), plus three long straps: in-band may well be worse than the baseline. The three platform pins crowd the arm patch and must stay inside the rigid-cuff window (theta -20..+62 deg about the…","verdict":""},{"id":"stop-relay-rocker-3r","name":"Stop-relay rocker 3R: J1 on a spring-centred acromial rocker that moves only once J2 is on a hard stop","family":"scheduled 4th axis: spherical 3R + passive base rocker (force-closed by stops and a centring spring)","lens":"round4: move the fold boundary with a determined element (honest singular pass / detent)","status":"screened","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":1,"t_ideated":"2026-10-16T04:40:00Z","t_screened":"2026-10-16T05:09:43Z","reach":0.6813,"follow":0.9167,"inband14":0.8516,"inband10":0.3676,"contact":0.052,"gap":35.24,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":6252,"doc":"docs/concepts/stop-relay-rocker-3r.md","short":"Stop-relay rocker 3R","description":"At rest it looks exactly like the verified leader spine-parked-j2-3r (CMA-B / k7008 seats): J1 on the acromion top, J2 parked over the posterior acromion, J3 on the lateral deltoid, 12x3 Ti straps. The difference is under hub 1. A small slim pivot hub (O15) sits on the acromion / AC top, 30 deg from J1 (about 31 mm at the hub radius), with its axis through GH at bvec(-159.9, 75.4). A 30-deg Ti rocker plate carries hub 1 from that pivot, and two opposed preloaded torsion springs centre it. Hub 2 has two hard stops at the leader's own jam margins, q2 = -34.1 deg (theta = angle(J1, J3) = 43.1) and q2 = -137.4 deg (theta = 104.4).\n\nMode A (every fit clip, and 0.639 of the big-bench workspace): the rocker sits on its centre and the chain is the leader, metal for metal. Overhead flexion and abduction past the folded margin (theta < 43.1): J2 reaches its stop before the fold, the arm keeps…","topology":"scapula (handler) -> R0 PASSIVE (pivot, axis c through GH, spring-centred, |q0| <= 75, sensed) -> rocker plate -> J1 ACTIVE R (axis u1 through GH, O20) -> link 1 -> J2 ACTIVE R (O15, hard stops at q2 = -34.1 / -137.4) -> link 2 -> J3 ACTIVE R (O15, arm-fixed axis) -> K7 neck -> BOTTOM -> cuff","main_risk":"1. R17 / dynamics: the switch is a force-closed relay, not a kinematic one.\n   - Bearing friction or chain inertia above T0 could trip the rocker early in mode A.\n   - Friction could leave hysteresis or chatter at the switch.\n   - The wearer feels up to about 0.35 N.m while the rocker is out. It must be run in judge/dynamics.py with the stop and spring modelled.\n2. The fit clips never cross the switch. Every gained pose is a workspace pose, so the 10 fps continuity check does not exercise the relay. Build a synthetic overhead-flexion clip.\n3. MVP2: in modes B/C, J2 sits on its stop and the rocker is unactuated. Overhead assistance then needs a 4th motor or stays passive.\n4. The…","verdict":"in progress: first measurements (local + 256-config farm coarse): best 35.24 fast (leader spine-parked 28.49 fast at k7008, CMA-B 30.24); CMA-ES streams running on the farm"},{"id":"tape-spool-humeral","name":"2R shoulder + self-stowing conformal humeral tape","family":"","lens":"overhead-first","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":3,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:15:45Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"2R shoulder + self-stowing conformal humeral tape","description":"Two O20 hubs. J1 is scapula-fixed on the lateral acromion edge. Link 1 is a ~90 deg strap to J2, which sits over the posterior shoulder when the arm hangs. Link 2 runs from J2 down the posterior-lateral deltoid to a 22 x 20 x 7 mm carriage about 100 mm below GH. An 18 mm NiTi tape lies 2-3 mm over the arm skin, wrapped part-way round the arm inside the body-free window (theta -20..+62 deg about the humerus; 0 = lateral, + = posterior; FINDINGS), from its anchor to the carriage, and then into an O14 spool. Rest: the carriage is lateral and the tape covers only the arc between anchor and carriage. External rotation: the carriage moves toward the anchor and the tape winds in. Internal rotation / behind-back (Reach back pocket): the carriage runs to the far end of the window and the tape pays out behind it, but no tape exists beyond the carriage on the trunk side. Overhead: the 2R points…","topology":"Scapula base -> J1: R hub, ACTIVE (axis b, hub-able: the lateral acromion edge) -> link 1 -> J2: R hub, ACTIVE (axis p2, arc a1 from b) -> link 2, which carries the humerus long axis at arc a2 from p2 (a 2R pointing chain) -> a link-2 carriage mount at station z down the arm -> a PASSIVE radial slide P (toward the humerus axis) and a PASSIVE yaw pivot R (axis parallel to the humerus axis) -> the carriage, with 4 MR52 guide rollers and 1 encoder roller pinching the tape -> humeral rotation phi, ACTIVE. The carriage runs along a pre-shaped superelastic NiTi tape (0.35 x 18 mm, 15 mm transverse curvature). One tape end is anchored to the upper-arm cuff region (the BOTTOM side); the tape lies arm-fixed 2-3 mm over the arm skin, in the plane perpendicular to the humerus at station z. Behind the carriage the tape winds onto an O14 constant-force spool on the carriage (PASSIVE, angle set by phi). There is no arm-end hub and no neck.","main_risk":"The deployed tape is a curved cantilever between anchor and carriage, and carriage loads bend and twist it. MVP1's <= 5 N at the cuff is probably fine; MVP2 needs a Bi-STEM pair or a rigid telescoping arc. The tape is fixed in the humerus frame, so it stands off where the deltoid or triceps bulge. The 2R's singular margin (19-27 deg) means fast J1 rates near it. The 67-87 deg of humeral travel, plus carriage and spool, must fit the -20..+62 deg window. NiTi spool fatigue. Joint technology: the humeral DOF is a tape-and-roller track, not a bearing joint, so it needs the user's acceptance.","verdict":""},{"id":"three-head-deltoid-spm","name":"Three-headed exo-deltoid: a 3-RRR spherical parallel mechanism with every actuator on the scapula","family":"","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":6,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Three-headed exo-deltoid","description":"Three thin arc 'heads' fan over the shoulder cap like the deltoid. Their base hubs sit on the exo-scapula, on ground the scapulohumeral rhythm protects: anterior-superior over the front of the acromion, middle on the acromion top, posterior on the spine. Each head is two 12 mm Ti arcs, proximal (base face + 3.15) and distal (+ 6.3), meeting at an elbow hub. The distal arcs end on three small arm pads on the cap band just below the acromion edge (posterolateral to lateral, delta ~95-115: the arm's low-motion band). The pads are tied by an arm-fixed bridge that continues to BOTTOM. Rest: the three elbows sit over the anterior, lateral and posterior deltoid. Overhead: the pads rise under the acromion and the heads fold, with the elbows moving up and out over the top and back; the posterior head stretches. Across-body: the posterior head stretches toward the front and the anterior head…","topology":"Spherical parallel mechanism, all 9 axes through GH. Three legs run from the exo-scapula to an arm platform made of three small pads on the cap band; each leg follows one head of the deltoid. Base R: a radial hub on the scapula (anterior head over the anterior acromion, middle head on the acromion top, posterior head on the scapular spine) [ACTIVE] -> proximal arc link -> elbow R [PASSIVE] -> distal arc link -> platform R: a radial hub on an arm pad [PASSIVE] -> arm. 3 active (base hubs), 6 passive (elbows + platform joints).","main_risk":"More metal over the cap (6 arcs, 9 hubs, 3 layers), all of which must stay in band. Legs collide under axial rotation, because the heads cross each other. The reachable set is the intersection of the three legs' sets, each limited by its own collisions. For MVP2 control there are 8 assembly modes and type-II (platform) singularities. The 9 bearings add friction (R17), though each is lightly loaded. Heavier than a serial 3R.","verdict":""},{"id":"tongs-tripod","name":"Tongs tripod: spherical 3-RPR whose three 'prismatic' legs are lazy-tongs scissor lattices","family":"","lens":"closed-loop","status":"ideated","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":24,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T15:12:21Z","t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":0,"doc":"","short":"Tongs tripod","description":"Three small scissor lattices stretch from three dome points to three points on an arm plate, like three folding 'tongs'. As the arm moves, each lattice stretches or folds evenly. Each of its nodes always sits at the same fraction of the path between its scapula point and its arm point, so the lattice stretches along with the skin between them. REST: one leg runs from the anterior dome down the anterolateral cap, one from the posterior dome down the posterior deltoid, and one from the top down the lateral cap. All three are ~12 mm wide when stretched, with nodes 6 mm tall. OVERHEAD: the arm plate rises to the lateral dome. The legs whose arcs shorten fold into fans 25-30 mm wide over the top; the posterior leg lengthens behind the acromion. ACROSS-BODY: the posterior leg stretches over the back of the shoulder and the anterior leg folds. BEHIND-BACK: the reverse.","topology":"Three legs in parallel from the scapula to an arm plate. Leg k:\n- S -> base boss on stable dome skin (axis a_k through GH), carrying two coaxial half-bar pivots. Their relative angle is the leg's ACTIVE extension; the mean is passive.\n- Lazy tongs: n = 2 units of 6 x 2 mm Ti bars on two layers, every pivot axis through GH. 1 DOF: the arc length Theta_k = angle(a_k, b_k).\n- Two coaxial half-bar pivots on an arm-fixed boss (axis b_k, passive) -> arm plate -> neck to BOTTOM.\n\nNo O20 hub anywhere; all bosses ~O16 x 9 mm and pins O7 x 6 mm. 27 joints: 3 active, 24 passive.","main_risk":"Workspace before any skin check. My kinematic screen (bench poses, extension 25-125 deg, |det| > 0.15) gets 0.74 of all poses (clips 0.85) with legs bvec(-180,40) -> wdir(60,30), bvec(-30,40) -> wdir(100,-60) and bvec(150,80) -> wdir(115,90). But two of those arm axes are outside the rigid-cuff window (theta -20..+62), and one base sits on the medial trapezius, which bunches overhead. Re-screen with the window first; expect lower. Other risks:\n- three lattices are a lot of thin metal over the cap;\n- the lattices 'breathe' sideways (half-width delta up to ~15 deg when folded);\n- 24 pins accumulate friction and backlash into the arm readout;\n- a lattice near full stretch is very compliant and…","verdict":""},{"id":"trombone-arc","name":"Trombone arc: one curved slide through a swivel guide on top of the scapula","family":"spherical 3R realised as a sliding arc (circular-guided RCM)","lens":"ideation A","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T14:54:13Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T19:43:39Z","reach":0.775,"follow":0.9,"inband14":0.2793,"inband10":0.102,"contact":0.0872,"gap":88.29,"v_gap":88.29,"v_reach":0.775,"v_follow":0.9,"v_inband14":0.2793,"v_contact":0.0872,"n_configs":144,"doc":"docs/concepts/trombone-arc.md","short":"Trombone arc","description":"A single 10 mm wide Ti band, bent to a circle about a virtual axis. It runs from a hub on the lateral deltoid up over the shoulder and through a swivelling guide on top of the scapula (supraspinatus/acromion region, where the baseline base hub is already 82 % in band). Past the guide, the free end of the band sticks out on the side away from the arm, like a trombone slide. REST (sep b-W ~90-110 deg): the guide sits near the far end of the arc. The band lies from the deltoid hub over the acromion to the top, with 10-30 deg of tail over the medial-posterior top. OVERHEAD (sep ~40 deg): W comes up to ~40 deg from the guide and the band slides out. Its tail (up to ~90 deg of arc, ~90 mm) lies over the trapezius toward the base of the neck/upper back. Only a short piece sits between the guide and the arm. ACROSS-BODY: J1 swivels forward. The band runs from the top over the front of the…","topology":"S (scapula base on the user's handler) -> J1: swivel about b (S-fixed axis through GH). It is a thin-section ring (KAA15XL0 class, O38 bore) on the S-top skin, and its upper race carries the guide. L1 = the guide: 4 preloaded needle cam-follower pairs. -> J2: rotation about p2. It is a Ti arc on L2, a circle about p2 at polar angle A1 that passes through b, sliding through the guide. No metal sits at p2's pierce point. -> L2 = arc + short connector -> J3: O20 v5 hub about W on the lateral deltoid (W is humerus-fixed, ~19 deg below the GH level). Its lower plate is on the arm-side BOTTOM stub. -> H. Active: J1, J2, J3. Passive: none. The user's cuff liner and the optional self-aligning cuff slides stay outside the count.","main_risk":"(1) The tail: when the arm is overhead, up to ~90 mm of band sticks out past the guide (130 deg of arc over the workspace range), and it may reach the neck/head or upper back. A 2-stage telescoping arc halves it. (2) Constant radius: every point of the band is R_a from GH, while the skin under it runs from ~44 mm (top) to ~60 mm (deltoid), so parts of the band sit 12-16 mm out. (3) Near W, the band's circle about p2 may pass inside the arm cone in poses where p2 swings close to the arm (the champion's p2 comes within 11 deg of the arm on the workspace). (4) A ~100 deg open arc cantilevered from W: twist/stiffness (FINDINGS: a 24x4 Ti band twists ~5 deg at 25 N.m over 75 mm, so ~2 deg at 10…","verdict":"Dead, not worth a GPU sweep: honest best gap 88.29 vs the champion's 68.93 after 144 configurations. The single-stage arc never fits: its tail runs into the neck and head, so the band floats at 400 mm. The 2-stage telescoping variant (the measured fix for the tail) closes and follows, but it still carries a constant-radius sleeve/band 40-60 mm off the skin (0 % in band)."},{"id":"twin-rail-one-hub","name":"Twin rails, one hub: virtual J1 on a crown rail, virtual J3 on an arm rail","family":"spherical 3R (virtual J1 / J3 on arc rails)","lens":"biomimetic-girdle|hardware-unlocks","status":"dead","active_dof":3,"passive_dof":0,"t_ideated":"2026-10-15T17:21:09Z","t_screened":"2026-10-15T23:42:28Z","reach":0.7438,"follow":0.95,"inband14":0.1365,"inband10":0.0838,"contact":0.0,"gap":91.97,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":1510,"doc":"docs/concepts/twin-rail-one-hub.md","short":"Twin rails, one hub","description":"Neither the J1 pole nor the J3 pole has a hub, so those poles may point into the body. The crown rail is a 26 mm band laid like a headband over the acromion and supraspinatus. This base metal is shaped exactly to skin that moves with S (measured today: 47.7-55 mm from GH, rest to 150 deg), so it never moves against that skin. The arm rail runs along the lateral arm 100-200 mm from GH, shaped to the arm skin. Only J2 and the short link 2 between the carriages move over the deforming deltoid. Overhead: carriage 1 sits at the front or back end of the crown rail and J2 at the crown edge. Across-body and behind-back: the carriages move to opposite ends of their rails.","topology":"S -> J1 R active with a VIRTUAL axis a1 through GH. An S-fixed open arc rail carries it: either a custom caged-ball wire-race C-slide (docs/research/ring_and_shell_hardware.md option A: 26 mm-wide hollow Ti band, 6 mm radial, 2 hardened race wires per edge, 3 mm balls) or a plain iglidur J band slide (8.5 mm stack). The rail lies on a circle about a1 at cone alpha12, over the crown -> carriage 1 (40-60 mm shoe) = J2's lower member -> J2 R active: the ONLY hub (KAA15XL0 donut, ~9 mm), axis a2 = the carriage's ray through GH, mounted BESIDE the rail at the same level (side outrigger, not stacked on it) -> link 2 = short strap -> carriage 3 -> J3 R active with a VIRTUAL axis a3 (humerus-fixed, through GH): an ARM-fixed arc rail on a circle about a3 at cone alpha32, on the lateral upper arm inside the -20..+62 deg sector, fixed to the cuff saddle under BOTTOM.","main_risk":"The J1 travel used must fit on the crown patch (roughly +-35 deg around c*), and J3 travel plus the carriage arc must fit in the 82 deg arm sector. Both cap the joint ranges, and the alpha >= ~58 deg constraint from the crown-ring concept still applies, so it is not certain a 3R exists whose q1 and q3 both fit. A long open rail is a curved cantilever (a 26x6 hollow band twists ~2 deg at 25 N.m). Rail plus carriage is ~10 mm, which leaves no margin to 14 mm where the deltoid bulges under the arm rail in flexion.","verdict":"dead (gate converged): best honest 91.97 (score 66.6) vs the spherical champion 68.93 and the verified leaders 64.7 / 66.47; the rails replace the pole hubs, which were never the limiter (they sit 98-100 % in band as O20 hubs), and add their own: a constant-section rail must clear the highest skin anywhere on its 120-190 deg arc in any pose (crown rail 0.0006 in band), the arm rail must span the humeral rotation range (cov 1.0 needs 420 deg) and collapses into a ring bearing round a lateral humeral pole; J2 and link 2 stay at 0 % in band. Even the shaped crown rail (cam + uncharged radial float) only reaches 97.64 at the coarse leader's axes."},{"id":"virtual-pole-4bar-j1","name":"virtual-pole-4bar-j1","family":"","lens":"","status":"modelling","active_dof":null,"passive_dof":null,"t_ideated":null,"t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":790,"doc":"","short":"virtual-pole-4bar-j1","description":"","topology":"","main_risk":"","verdict":""},{"id":"virtual-pole-4bar-j2","name":"virtual-pole-4bar-j2","family":"","lens":"","status":"modelling","active_dof":null,"passive_dof":null,"t_ideated":null,"t_screened":null,"reach":null,"follow":null,"inband14":null,"inband10":null,"contact":null,"gap":null,"v_gap":null,"v_reach":null,"v_follow":null,"v_inband14":null,"v_contact":null,"n_configs":764,"doc":"","short":"virtual-pole-4bar-j2","description":"","topology":"","main_risk":"","verdict":""}]};
